const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"Athens. A hall in Timon's house.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Poet, Painter, Jeweller, Merchant, and Others, at several doors.","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["Good day, sir."],"id":2,"m":"Good morning, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["I am glad you're well."],"id":3,"m":"I'm glad to see you well."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["I have not seen you long. How goes the world?"],"id":4,"m":"I haven't seen you for a long time. How is the world treating you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["It wears, sir, as it grows."],"id":5,"m":"It wears out, sir, as it gets older."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["Ay, that's well known;","But what particular rarity? what strange,","Which manifold record not matches? See,","Magic of bounty! all these spirits thy power","Hath conjur'd to attend! I know the merchant."],"id":6,"m":"Yes, everyone knows that. But is there anything special, anything strange that no history can match? Look, the magic of generosity! Your power, Timon's bounty, has conjured all these people here to wait on him. I know the merchant."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["I know them both; th' other's a jeweller."],"id":7,"m":"I know them both. The other one is a jeweller."},{"t":"sp","who":"MERCHANT","lines":["O, 'tis a worthy lord!"],"id":8,"m":"Oh, he's a fine lord!"},{"t":"sp","who":"JEWELLER","lines":["Nay, that's most fix'd."],"id":9,"m":"Yes, that's certain."},{"t":"sp","who":"MERCHANT","lines":["A most incomparable man; breath'd, as it were,","To an untirable and continuate goodness.","He passes."],"id":10,"m":"A man beyond comparison, trained, you might say, to a tireless and never-ending goodness. He surpasses everyone."},{"t":"sp","who":"JEWELLER","lines":["I have a jewel here—"],"id":11,"m":"I have a jewel here..."},{"t":"sp","who":"MERCHANT","lines":["O, pray let's see't: for the Lord Timon, sir?"],"id":12,"m":"Oh, please let me see it. Is it for Lord Timon, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"JEWELLER","lines":["If he will touch the estimate: but for that—"],"id":13,"m":"If he will pay what it's worth. But as for that..."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["When we for recompense have prais'd the vile,","It stains the glory in that happy verse","Which aptly sings the good."],"id":14,"m":"(Reciting to himself) When we praise wicked men in return for payment, it spoils the glory of the poem that rightly celebrates the good."},{"t":"sp","who":"MERCHANT","lines":["[Looking at the jewel] 'Tis a good form."],"id":15,"m":"(Looking at the jewel) It's a well-shaped stone."},{"t":"sp","who":"JEWELLER","lines":["And rich: here is a water, look ye."],"id":16,"m":"And a rich one. Look at the clear sparkle in it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["You are rapt, sir, in some work, some dedication","To the great lord."],"id":17,"m":"You're lost in thought, sir, working on some poem, some dedication to the great lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["A thing slipp'd idly from me.","Our poesy is as a gum, which oozes","From whence 'tis nourish'd: the fire i' the flint","Shows not till it be struck; our gentle flame","Provokes itself, and like the current flies","Each bound it chafes. What have you there?"],"id":18,"m":"Just something that slipped out of me without effort. Poetry is like the gum that oozes from a tree where it grows. The fire in a flint doesn't show until it's struck, but our gentle flame lights itself and, like a running stream, flows past each bank that holds it in. What have you got there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["A picture, sir. When comes your book forth?"],"id":19,"m":"A picture, sir. When is your book coming out?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["Upon the heels of my presentment, sir.","Let's see your piece."],"id":20,"m":"As soon as I've presented it to Timon, sir. Let me see your painting."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["'Tis a good piece."],"id":21,"m":"It's a good piece."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["So 'tis: this comes off well and excellent."],"id":22,"m":"So it is. This has come out excellently."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["Indifferent."],"id":23,"m":"It's all right."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["Admirable! How this grace","Speaks his own standing! what a mental power","This eye shoots forth! how big imagination","Moves in this lip! to the dumbness of the gesture","One might interpret."],"id":24,"m":"It's wonderful! See how the figure's grace shows his high rank! What power of mind shines from this eye! What great imagination moves in this lip! You could put words to the silent gesture."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["It is a pretty mocking of the life.","Here is a touch; is't good?"],"id":25,"m":"It's a fair imitation of life. Here's a touch of detail. Is it good?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["I'll say of it,","It tutors nature: artificial strife","Lives in these touches, livelier than life."],"id":26,"m":"I'll say this of it: it teaches nature a lesson. Art competing with nature lives in these brushstrokes, more lifelike than life itself."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter certain SENATORS, who pass over the stage.","id":27},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["How this lord is followed!"],"id":28,"m":"How many people follow this lord!"},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["The senators of Athens: happy man!"],"id":29,"m":"Those are the senators of Athens. Lucky man!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["Look, more!"],"id":30,"m":"Look, there are more!"},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["You see this confluence, this great flood of visitors.","I have, in this rough work, shap'd out a man","Whom this beneath world doth embrace and hug","With amplest entertainment: my free drift","Halts not particularly, but moves itself","In a wide sea of wax: no levell'd malice","Infects one comma in the course I hold:","But flies an eagle flight, bold and forth on,","Leaving no tract behind."],"id":31,"m":"You see this crowd, this great flood of visitors. In this rough draft I have shaped a man whom the whole world below heaven embraces and welcomes most warmly. My poem's free movement doesn't stop at any one person but ranges over a wide sea of subjects. No pointed malice infects a single comma of it. It flies boldly onward like an eagle and leaves no trail behind."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["How shall I understand you?"],"id":32,"m":"What do you mean by that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["I will unbolt to you.","You see how all conditions, how all minds—","As well of glib and slipp'ry creatures as","Of grave and austere quality—tender down","Their services to Lord Timon: his large fortune,","Upon his good and gracious nature hanging,","Subdues and properties to his love and tendance","All sorts of hearts; yea, from the glass-fac'd flatterer","To Apemantus, that few things loves better","Than to abhor himself: even he drops down","The knee before him, and returns in peace","Most rich in Timon's nod."],"id":33,"m":"I'll explain. You see how men of every rank and every temper, the smooth and slippery ones as well as the serious and strict, offer their services to Lord Timon. His great fortune, joined to his good and gracious nature, wins over every kind of heart and makes it his to love and serve. Yes, from the flatterer whose face is a mirror of his patron's, to Apemantus, who loves almost nothing better than hating himself. Even he kneels to Timon, and goes away content, rich in a nod from him."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["I saw them speak together."],"id":34,"m":"I saw them talking together."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["Sir, I have upon a high and pleasant hill","Feign'd Fortune to be thron'd: the base o' the mount","Is rank'd with all deserts, all kind of natures","That labour on the bosom of this sphere","To propagate their states: amongst them all,","Whose eyes are on this sovereign lady fix'd","One do I personate of Lord Timon's frame,","Whom Fortune with her ivory hand wafts to her;","Whose present grace to present slaves and servants","Translates his rivals."],"id":35,"m":"Sir, in my poem I have imagined Fortune sitting on a throne on top of a high and pleasant hill. The bottom of the hill is crowded with people of every merit and every nature who toil on this earth to improve their position. Among all those whose eyes are fixed on this sovereign lady, I portray one man like Lord Timon, whom Fortune beckons up to her with her ivory hand. Her favour instantly turns his rivals into his slaves and servants."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["'Tis conceiv'd to scope.","This throne, this Fortune, and this hill, methinks,","With one man beckon'd from the rest below,","Bowing his head against the steepy mount","To climb his happiness, would be well express'd","In our condition."],"id":36,"m":"That's well imagined. This throne, this Fortune and this hill, with one man called up from the rest below, bowing his head as he climbs the steep slope to his happiness, would make a fine painting in my art."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["Nay, sir, but hear me on.","All those which were his fellows but of late,","Some better than his value, on the moment","Follow his strides, his lobbies fill with tendance,","Rain sacrificial whisperings in his ear,","Make sacred even his stirrup, and through him","Drink the free air."],"id":37,"m":"No, sir, hear the rest. All those who were his equals only lately, some of them worth more than he was, at once follow in his footsteps. They fill his halls with their attendance, pour flattering whispers into his ear as if they were prayers, treat even his stirrup as holy, and breathe the free air only through him."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["Ay, marry, what of these?"],"id":38,"m":"Yes, of course. What about them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["When Fortune in her shift and change of mood","Spurns down her late beloved, all his dependants,","Which labour'd after him to the mountain's top","Even on their knees and hands, let him slip down,","Not one accompanying his declining foot."],"id":39,"m":"When Fortune changes her mood and kicks down her recent favourite, all his followers, who crawled after him to the top of the mountain on their hands and knees, let him slide down. Not one of them goes with him on the way down."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["'Tis common:","A thousand moral paintings I can show","That shall demonstrate these quick blows of Fortune's","More pregnantly than words. Yet you do well","To show Lord Timon that mean eyes have seen","The foot above the head."],"id":40,"m":"That's a common theme. I could show you a thousand moral paintings that show these sudden blows of Fortune more powerfully than words. Still, you do well to show Lord Timon that humble people have seen the mighty fall."},{"t":"sd","text":"Trumpets sound. Enter LORD TIMON, addressing himself courteously to every suitor: a MESSENGER from VENTIDIUS talking with him; LUCILIUS and other servants following.","id":41},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Imprison'd is he, say you?"],"id":42,"m":"He's in prison, you say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Ay, my good lord. Five talents is his debt,","His means most short, his creditors most strait:","Your honourable letter he desires","To those have shut him up; which, failing,","Periods his comfort."],"id":43,"m":"Yes, my good lord. His debt is five talents, his money is very short, and his creditors very strict. He asks you to write a letter to the men who have locked him up. If you don't, his hopes are at an end."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Noble Ventidius! Well:","I am not of that feather to shake off","My friend when he must need me. I do know him","A gentleman that well deserves a help,","Which he shall have: I'll pay the debt and free him."],"id":44,"m":"Noble Ventidius! Well, I'm not the kind of bird to shake off a friend just when he needs me. I know him to be a gentleman who deserves help, and he shall have it. I'll pay the debt and set him free."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Your lordship ever binds him."],"id":45,"m":"Your lordship puts him in your debt forever."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Commend me to him; I will send his ransom;","And being enfranchis'd, bid him come to me.","'Tis not enough to help the feeble up,","But to support him after. Fare you well."],"id":46,"m":"Give him my regards. I will send the money to free him, and once he's released, tell him to come to me. It isn't enough to help a weak man to his feet; you must support him afterwards too. Goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["All happiness to your honour."],"id":47,"m":"I wish your honour every happiness."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":48},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter an OLD ATHENIAN.","id":49},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD ATHENIAN","lines":["Lord Timon, hear me speak."],"id":50,"m":"Lord Timon, listen to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Freely, good father."],"id":51,"m":"Speak freely, good old man."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD ATHENIAN","lines":["Thou hast a servant nam'd Lucilius."],"id":52,"m":"You have a servant called Lucilius."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["I have so: what of him?"],"id":53,"m":"I have. What about him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD ATHENIAN","lines":["Most noble Timon, call the man before thee."],"id":54,"m":"Most noble Timon, call the man here."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Attends he here or no? Lucilius!"],"id":55,"m":"Is he here or not? Lucilius!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCILIUS","lines":["Here, at your lordship's service."],"id":56,"m":"Here, at your lordship's service."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD ATHENIAN","lines":["This fellow here, Lord Timon, this thy creature,","By night frequents my house. I am a man","That from my first have been inclin'd to thrift,","And my estate deserves an heir more rais'd","Than one which holds a trencher."],"id":57,"m":"This fellow here, Lord Timon, this servant of yours, visits my house at night. I am a man who has been careful with money all my life, and my estate deserves a more important heir than a man who waits at table."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Well; what further?"],"id":58,"m":"Well, what else?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD ATHENIAN","lines":["One only daughter have I, no kin else,","On whom I may confer what I have got:","The maid is fair, o' the youngest for a bride,","And I have bred her at my dearest cost","In qualities of the best. This man of thine","Attempts her love: I prithee, noble lord,","Join with me to forbid him her resort;","Myself have spoke in vain."],"id":59,"m":"I have only one daughter and no other family to leave what I've earned to. The girl is pretty, just old enough to be a bride, and I have raised her at great cost with the best accomplishments. This man of yours is trying to win her love. Please, noble lord, help me forbid him to see her. I have told him myself, with no result."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["The man is honest."],"id":60,"m":"The man is honest."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD ATHENIAN","lines":["Therefore he will be, Timon:","His honesty rewards him in itself;","It must not bear my daughter."],"id":61,"m":"Then let him stay honest, Timon. His honesty is its own reward. It shouldn't win him my daughter as well."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Does she love him?"],"id":62,"m":"Does she love him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD ATHENIAN","lines":["She is young and apt:","Our own precedent passions do instruct us","What levity's in youth."],"id":63,"m":"She is young and easily swayed. Our own past passions teach us how giddy the young can be."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["[To Lucilius] Love you the maid?"],"id":64,"m":"(To Lucilius) Do you love the girl?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCILIUS","lines":["Ay, my good lord, and she accepts of it."],"id":65,"m":"Yes, my good lord, and she returns my love."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD ATHENIAN","lines":["If in her marriage my consent be missing,","I call the gods to witness, I will choose","Mine heir from forth the beggars of the world,","And dispossess her all."],"id":66,"m":"If she marries without my consent, I swear by the gods I'll choose my heir from among the world's beggars and leave her nothing."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["How shall she be endow'd,","If she be mated with an equal husband?"],"id":67,"m":"What dowry will she have if she marries a man of equal rank?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD ATHENIAN","lines":["Three talents on the present; in future, all."],"id":68,"m":"Three talents now, and everything I own later."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["This gentleman of mine hath serv'd me long:","To build his fortune I will strain a little,","For 'tis a bond in men. Give him thy daughter:","What you bestow, in him I'll counterpoise,","And make him weigh with her."],"id":69,"m":"This gentleman has served me for a long time. I will stretch myself a little to make his fortune, because that is a duty between men. Give him your daughter. Whatever you give her, I'll give him the same, so that he will be her equal."},{"t":"sp","who":"OLD ATHENIAN","lines":["Most noble lord,","Pawn me to this your honour, she is his."],"id":70,"m":"Most noble lord, give me your word of honour on it and she is his."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["My hand to thee; mine honour on my promise."],"id":71,"m":"Here is my hand, and my honour stands behind my promise."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCILIUS","lines":["Humbly I thank your lordship. Never may","That state or fortune fall into my keeping","Which is not owed to you!"],"id":72,"m":"I humbly thank your lordship. May I never own any wealth or rank that I do not owe to you!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt LUCILIUS and OLD ATHENIAN.","id":73},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["[Presenting his poem] Vouchsafe my labour, and long live your lordship!"],"id":74,"m":"(Presenting his poem) Please accept my work, and long live your lordship!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["I thank you; you shall hear from me anon:","Go not away. What have you there, my friend?"],"id":75,"m":"Thank you. You'll hear from me soon. Don't go away. What have you got there, my friend?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["A piece of painting, which I do beseech","Your lordship to accept."],"id":76,"m":"A painting, which I beg your lordship to accept."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Painting is welcome.","The painting is almost the natural man;","For since dishonour traffics with man's nature,","He is but outside: these pencill'd figures are","Even such as they give out. I like your work;","And you shall find I like it: wait attendance","Till you hear further from me."],"id":77,"m":"A painting is welcome. A painting is almost a real man. Since dishonesty became part of human nature, man is all outward show, but these painted figures are exactly what they appear to be. I like your work, and you'll see that I mean it. Wait here until you hear more from me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["The gods preserve you!"],"id":78,"m":"The gods keep you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Well fare you, gentleman: give me your hand;","We must needs dine together. Sir, your jewel","Hath suffered under praise."],"id":79,"m":"Goodbye, sir. Give me your hand; we must dine together. Sir, your jewel has suffered from praise."},{"t":"sp","who":"JEWELLER","lines":["What, my lord! dispraise?"],"id":80,"m":"What, my lord? Has it been criticised?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["A mere satiety of commendations;","If I should pay you for 't as 'tis extoll'd,","It would unclew me quite."],"id":81,"m":"No, it has been praised to excess. If I paid you for it at the price it's been praised at, it would ruin me completely."},{"t":"sp","who":"JEWELLER","lines":["My lord, 'tis rated","As those which sell would give: but you well know,","Things of like value, differing in the owners,","Are prized by their masters. Believe't, dear lord,","You mend the jewel by the wearing it."],"id":82,"m":"My lord, it's priced at what the sellers would pay themselves. But you know that things of equal value are prized according to who owns them. Believe me, dear lord, you make the jewel better by wearing it."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Well mock'd."],"id":83,"m":"Nicely mocked."},{"t":"sp","who":"MERCHANT","lines":["No, my good lord; he speaks the common tongue,","Which all men speak with him."],"id":84,"m":"No, my good lord, he only says what everybody says."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Look who comes here. Will you be chid?"],"id":85,"m":"Look who's coming. Are you ready to be scolded?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter APEMANTUS.","id":86},{"t":"sp","who":"JEWELLER","lines":["We'll bear, with your lordship."],"id":87,"m":"We'll bear it along with your lordship."},{"t":"sp","who":"MERCHANT","lines":["He'll spare none."],"id":88,"m":"He won't spare anyone."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Good morrow to thee, gentle Apemantus!"],"id":89,"m":"Good morning to you, gentle Apemantus!"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Till I be gentle, stay thou for thy good morrow;","When thou art Timon's dog, and these knaves honest."],"id":90,"m":"Wait for me to wish you good morning until I am gentle, which will be when you are turned into Timon's dog and these rogues are honest."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Why dost thou call them knaves? thou know'st them not."],"id":91,"m":"Why do you call them rogues? You don't know them."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Are they not Athenians?"],"id":92,"m":"Aren't they Athenians?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Yes."],"id":93,"m":"Yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Then I repent not."],"id":94,"m":"Then I don't take it back."},{"t":"sp","who":"JEWELLER","lines":["You know me, Apemantus?"],"id":95,"m":"You know me, Apemantus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Thou know'st I do; I call'd thee by thy name."],"id":96,"m":"You know I do. I called you by your name: rogue."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Thou art proud, Apemantus."],"id":97,"m":"You are proud, Apemantus."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Of nothing so much as that I am not like Timon."],"id":98,"m":"Of nothing so much as of not being like Timon."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Whither art going?"],"id":99,"m":"Where are you going?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["To knock out an honest Athenian's brains."],"id":100,"m":"To knock out an honest Athenian's brains."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["That's a deed thou'lt die for."],"id":101,"m":"That's a crime you'll be executed for."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Right, if doing nothing be death by the law."],"id":102,"m":"True, if doing nothing is punishable by death. There are no honest Athenians to kill."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["How likest thou this picture, Apemantus?"],"id":103,"m":"What do you think of this picture, Apemantus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["The best, for the innocence."],"id":104,"m":"It's the best thing here, because it is innocent: it can do no harm."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Wrought he not well that painted it?"],"id":105,"m":"Didn't the man who painted it do good work?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["He wrought better that made the painter; and yet he's","but a filthy piece of work."],"id":106,"m":"The one who made the painter did better work, and even he is a filthy piece of work."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["You're a dog."],"id":107,"m":"You're a dog."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Thy mother's of my generation: what's she, if I be a dog?"],"id":108,"m":"Your mother is of my kind. What is she, if I'm a dog?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Wilt dine with me, Apemantus?"],"id":109,"m":"Will you dine with me, Apemantus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["No; I eat not lords."],"id":110,"m":"No. I don't eat lords."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["An thou shouldst, thou'dst anger ladies."],"id":111,"m":"If you did, you'd make the ladies angry."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["O! they eat lords; so they come by great bellies."],"id":112,"m":"Oh, they eat lords, and that's how they get big bellies."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["That's a lascivious apprehension."],"id":113,"m":"That's a dirty way of taking it."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["So thou apprehendest it, take it for thy labour."],"id":114,"m":"It's your understanding that took it that way, so keep it as your reward."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["How dost thou like this jewel, Apemantus?"],"id":115,"m":"What do you think of this jewel, Apemantus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Not so well as plain dealing, which will not cost a man","a doit."],"id":116,"m":"Not as much as I think of honest dealing, which doesn't cost a man a penny."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["What dost thou think 'tis worth?"],"id":117,"m":"What do you think it's worth?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Not worth my thinking. How now, poet!"],"id":118,"m":"Not worth thinking about. Hello, poet!"},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["How now, philosopher!"],"id":119,"m":"Hello, philosopher!"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Thou liest."],"id":120,"m":"You're lying."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["Art not one?"],"id":121,"m":"Aren't you a philosopher?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Yes."],"id":122,"m":"Yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["Then I lie not."],"id":123,"m":"Then I'm not lying."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Art not a poet?"],"id":124,"m":"Aren't you a poet?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["Yes."],"id":125,"m":"Yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Then thou liest: look in thy last work, where thou hast","feigned him a worthy fellow."],"id":126,"m":"Then you are lying. Look at your latest work, where you have pretended he is a worthy man."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["That's not feigned; he is so."],"id":127,"m":"That isn't pretence. He is worthy."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Yes, he is worthy of thee, and to pay thee for thy","labour: he that loves to be flattered is worthy o' the flatterer.","Heavens, that I were a lord!"],"id":128,"m":"Yes, he is worthy of you, and worthy to pay you for your work. A man who loves to be flattered deserves the flatterer. Heavens, I wish I were a lord!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["What wouldst do then, Apemantus?"],"id":129,"m":"What would you do then, Apemantus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Even as Apemantus does now; hate a lord with my heart."],"id":130,"m":"Just what Apemantus does now: hate a lord with all my heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["What, thyself?"],"id":131,"m":"What, hate yourself?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Ay."],"id":132,"m":"Yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Wherefore?"],"id":133,"m":"Why?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["That I had no angry wit to be a lord. Art not thou a merchant?"],"id":134,"m":"For having had no better sense than to become a lord. Aren't you a merchant?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MERCHANT","lines":["Ay, Apemantus."],"id":135,"m":"Yes, Apemantus."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Traffic confound thee, if the gods will not!"],"id":136,"m":"May trade ruin you, if the gods won't!"},{"t":"sp","who":"MERCHANT","lines":["If traffic do it, the gods do it."],"id":137,"m":"If trade ruins me, it will be the gods doing it."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Traffic's thy god, and thy god confound thee!"],"id":138,"m":"Trade is your god, and may your god ruin you!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Trumpet sounds. Enter a MESSENGER.","id":139},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["What trumpet's that?"],"id":140,"m":"What's that trumpet?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["'Tis Alcibiades, and some twenty horse,","All of companionship."],"id":141,"m":"It's Alcibiades, with about twenty horsemen, all his companions."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Pray entertain them; give them guide to us."],"id":142,"m":"Please welcome them and bring them to us."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt some attendants.","id":143},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","cont":1,"lines":["You must needs dine with me. Go not you hence","Till I have thank'd you; when dinner's done,","Show me this piece. I am joyful of your sights."],"id":144,"m":"You must dine with me. Don't leave until I've thanked you. When dinner is over, show me this painting. I'm delighted to see you all."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter ALCIBIADES, with his company.","id":145},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","cont":1,"lines":["Most welcome, sir!"],"id":146,"m":"You are very welcome, sir!"},{"t":"sd","text":"They salute.","id":147},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["So, so, there!","Aches contract and starve your supple joints!","That there should be small love 'mongst these sweet knaves,","And all this courtesy! The strain of man's bred out","Into baboon and monkey."],"id":148,"m":"There, look at that! May aches shrink and wither your bending joints! To think there's so little love among these charming rogues, and all this politeness! The human race has degenerated into baboons and monkeys."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["Sir, you have sav'd my longing, and I feed","Most hungerly on your sight."],"id":149,"m":"Sir, you have satisfied my longing to see you, and I feast hungrily on the sight of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Right welcome, sir!","Ere we depart we'll share a bounteous time","In different pleasures. Pray you, let us in."],"id":150,"m":"You are very welcome, sir! Before we part we'll share a fine time with all kinds of pleasures. Please, let's go in."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but APEMANTUS.","id":151},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter two LORDS.","id":152},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["What time o' day is't, Apemantus?"],"id":153,"m":"What time of day is it, Apemantus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Time to be honest."],"id":154,"m":"Time to be honest."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["That time serves still."],"id":155,"m":"That's always the right time."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["The more accursed thou that still omitt'st it."],"id":156,"m":"Then you are all the more damned for always missing it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Thou art going to Lord Timon's feast."],"id":157,"m":"You're going to Lord Timon's feast."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Ay; to see meat fill knaves and wine heat fools."],"id":158,"m":"Yes, to see food fill rogues and wine heat up fools."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Fare thee well, fare thee well."],"id":159,"m":"Goodbye, goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Thou art a fool to bid me farewell twice."],"id":160,"m":"You're a fool to say goodbye to me twice."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Why, Apemantus?"],"id":161,"m":"Why, Apemantus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Shouldst have kept one to thyself, for I mean to give thee none."],"id":162,"m":"You should have kept one for yourself, because I'm not going to give you any."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Hang thyself!"],"id":163,"m":"Go hang yourself!"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["No, I will do nothing at thy bidding: make thy requests to thy","friend."],"id":164,"m":"No, I won't do anything you tell me to. Make your requests to your friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Away, unpeaceable dog! or I'll spurn thee hence."],"id":165,"m":"Go away, you quarrelsome dog, or I'll kick you out of here."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["I will fly, like a dog, the heels of an ass."],"id":166,"m":"I'll run from the heels of an ass, as a dog does."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":167},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["He's opposite to humanity. Come, shall we in,","And taste Lord Timon's bounty? he outgoes","The very heart of kindness."],"id":168,"m":"He's the enemy of all humanity. Come, shall we go in and enjoy Lord Timon's generosity? He goes beyond kindness itself."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["He pours it out; Plutus, the god of gold,","Is but his steward: no meed but he repays","Sevenfold above itself; no gift to him","But breeds the giver a return exceeding","All use of quittance."],"id":169,"m":"He pours it out. Plutus, the god of wealth, is only his steward. He repays every favour seven times over, and nobody gives him a gift without getting back far more than any normal repayment."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["The noblest mind he carries","That ever govern'd man."],"id":170,"m":"He has the noblest mind that ever ruled a man."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Long may he live in fortunes! Shall we in?"],"id":171,"m":"May he live long and prosper! Shall we go in?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["I'll keep you company."],"id":172,"m":"I'll come with you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":173},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"Athens. A banqueting room in Timon's house.","id":174},{"t":"sd","text":"Hautboys playing loud music. A great banquet served in; FLAVIUS and Others attending: then enter LORD TIMON, ALCIBIADES, Lords, and Senators, VENTIDIUS and Attendants. Then comes, dropping after all, APEMANTUS, discontentedly, like himself.","id":175},{"t":"sp","who":"VENTIDIUS","lines":["Most honour'd Timon,","It hath pleas'd the gods to remember my father's age,","And call him to long peace.","He is gone happy, and has left me rich:","Then, as in grateful virtue I am bound","To your free heart, I do return those talents,","Doubled with thanks and service, from whose help","I deriv'd liberty."],"id":176,"m":"Most honoured Timon, the gods have been pleased to remember my father's old age and call him to his long rest. He has died happy and left me rich. So, since gratitude binds me to your generous heart, I return those talents that bought my freedom, doubled, with my thanks and service."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["O! by no means,","Honest Ventidius: you mistake my love;","I gave it freely ever; and there's none","Can truly say he gives, if he receives:","If our betters play at that game, we must not dare","To imitate them; faults that are rich are fair."],"id":177,"m":"Oh, certainly not, honest Ventidius. You misunderstand my love. I always gave freely, and nobody can truly say he gives if he takes something back. If our superiors play that game, we must not dare copy them. Rich men's faults are always called fair."},{"t":"sp","who":"VENTIDIUS","lines":["A noble spirit."],"id":178,"m":"What a noble spirit."},{"t":"sd","text":"They all stand ceremoniously looking on TIMON.","id":179},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Nay, my lords, ceremony was but devis'd at first","To set a gloss on faint deeds, hollow welcomes,","Recanting goodness, sorry ere 'tis shown;","But where there is true friendship there needs none.","Pray, sit; more welcome are ye to my fortunes","Than my fortunes to me."],"id":180,"m":"No, my lords, ceremony was first invented to put a shine on half-hearted deeds, empty welcomes, and kindness that is regretted even before it is shown. Where there is true friendship, none is needed. Please, sit down. You are more welcome to my wealth than my wealth is to me."},{"t":"sd","text":"They sit.","id":181},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["My lord, we always have confess'd it."],"id":182,"m":"My lord, we have always admitted it."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Ho, ho! confess'd it; hang'd it, have you not?"],"id":183,"m":"Ha! Admitted it? You'll be hanged for it, won't you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["O! Apemantus, you are welcome."],"id":184,"m":"Oh, Apemantus, you are welcome."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["No,","You shall not make me welcome:","I come to have thee thrust me out of doors."],"id":185,"m":"No, you won't make me welcome. I've come so that you can throw me out."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Fie! thou'rt a churl; ye've got a humour there","Does not become a man; 'tis much to blame.","They say, my lords, Ira furor brevis est;","But yond man is ever angry.","Go, let him have a table by himself;","For he does neither affect company,","Nor is he fit for it, indeed."],"id":186,"m":"For shame, you're a boor. You have a mood that doesn't suit a man; it's very wrong. They say, my lords, \"anger is a short madness\", but that man is always angry. Go, give him a table of his own, because he doesn't like company and isn't fit for it either."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Let me stay at thine apperil, Timon:","I come to observe; I give thee warning on't."],"id":187,"m":"Let me stay at your own risk, Timon. I've come to watch. I'm warning you."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["I take no heed of thee; thou'rt an Athenian, therefore, welcome.","I myself would have no power; prithee; let my meat make thee","silent."],"id":188,"m":"I pay no attention to you. You're an Athenian, so you're welcome. I don't want the power to silence you myself; please, let my food silence you."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["I scorn thy meat; 't'would choke me, for I should","Ne'er flatter thee. O you gods! what a number","Of men eats Timon, and he sees 'em not!","It grieves me to see so many dip their meat","In one man's blood; and all the madness is,","He cheers them up too.","I wonder men dare trust themselves with men:","Methinks they should invite them without knives;","Good for their meat, and safer for their lives.","There's much example for 't; the fellow that","Sits next him now, parts bread with him, pledges","The breath of him in a divided draught,","Is the readiest man to kill him: 't has been prov'd.","If I were a huge man, I should fear to drink at meals;","Lest they should spy my wind-pipe's dangerous notes:","Great men should drink with harness on their throats."],"id":189,"m":"I despise your food. It would choke me, because I would never flatter you to earn it. Oh you gods, how many men are eating Timon, and he doesn't see it! It upsets me to see so many dip their bread in one man's blood, and the maddest part of it is that he encourages them. I'm amazed that men dare trust themselves with other men. I think they should invite guests without knives: it would be good for the food and safer for their lives. There are plenty of examples. The fellow sitting next to him now, sharing his bread and drinking his health from the same cup, is the man readiest to kill him. It has been proved before. If I were a great man I'd be afraid to drink at meals, in case they spotted where my windpipe was weakest. Great men should drink with armour on their throats."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["My lord, in heart; and let the health go round."],"id":190,"m":"(Drinking a toast) My lord, with all my heart, and let the toast go round."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Let it flow this way, my good lord."],"id":191,"m":"Let it come this way, my good lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Flow this way! A brave fellow! he keeps his tides well. Those","healths will make thee and thy state look ill, Timon.","Here's that which is too weak to be a sinner,","Honest water, which ne'er left man i' the mire:","This and my food are equals; there's no odds:","Feasts are too proud to give thanks to the gods.","~Immortal gods, I crave no pelf;","~I pray for no man but myself.","~Grant I may never prove so fond","~To trust man on his oath or bond;","~Or a harlot for her weeping;","~Or a dog that seems a-sleeping;","~Or a keeper with my freedom;","~Or my friends, if I should need 'em.","Amen. So fall to't.","Rich men sin, and I eat root."],"id":192,"m":"\"Come this way\"! What a fine fellow! He knows how to catch the tide. Those toasts will make you and your fortune look sick, Timon. Here's something too weak to make anyone sin: honest water, which never left a man in the mud. This and my food are equal partners. Feasts are too proud to thank the gods. (Saying grace) Immortal gods, I don't ask for money. I pray for nobody but myself. Grant that I may never be so foolish as to trust a man's oath or his signed promise, or a prostitute's tears, or a dog that seems to be asleep, or a jailer with my freedom, or my friends if I should need them. Amen. So, tuck in. Rich men sin, and I eat roots."},{"t":"sd","text":"Eats and drinks.","id":193},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","cont":1,"lines":["Much good dich thy good heart, Apemantus!"],"id":194,"m":"Much good may it do your good heart, Apemantus!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Captain Alcibiades, your heart's in the field now."],"id":195,"m":"Captain Alcibiades, your heart is on the battlefield now."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["My heart is ever at your service, my lord."],"id":196,"m":"My heart is always at your service, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["You had rather be at a breakfast of enemies than a dinner of","friends."],"id":197,"m":"You'd rather be at a breakfast of enemies than a dinner of friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["So they were bleeding-new, my lord, there's no meat","like 'em: I could wish my best friend at such a feast."],"id":198,"m":"As long as they were freshly bleeding, my lord, there's no meat like them. I could wish my best friend at a feast like that."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["'Would all those flatterers were thine enemies then, that","then thou mightst kill 'em, and bid me to 'em."],"id":199,"m":"Then I wish all those flatterers were your enemies, so that you could kill them and invite me to eat them."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Might we but have that happiness, my lord, that you","would once use our hearts, whereby we might express some part of","our zeals, we should think ourselves for ever perfect."],"id":200,"m":"If only we might have the happiness, my lord, of your using our hearts some day, so that we could show some part of our devotion, we would think ourselves perfectly content forever."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["O, no doubt, my good friends, but the gods themselves have","provided that I shall have much help from you: how had you been","my friends else? why have you that charitable title from","thousands, did not you chiefly belong to my heart? I have told","more of you to myself than you can with modesty speak in your own","behalf; and thus far I confirm you. O you gods! think I, what","need we have any friends if we should ne'er have need of 'em?","they were the most needless creatures living, should we ne'er","have use for 'em; and would most resemble sweet instruments hung","up in cases, that keep their sounds to themselves. Why, I have","often wished myself poorer that I might come nearer to you. We","are born to do benefits; and what better or properer can we call","our own than the riches of our friends? O! what a precious","comfort 'tis to have so many, like brothers, commanding one","another's fortunes! O joy! e'en made away ere it can be born.","Mine eyes cannot hold out water, methinks: to forget their","faults, I drink to you."],"id":201,"m":"Oh, I'm sure, my good friends, that the gods themselves have arranged for me to have a lot of help from you. How else would you be my friends? Why else would you, out of thousands, be called friends, unless you belonged above all to my heart? I have said more good of you to myself than you could modestly say of yourselves, and to that extent I'm sure of you. \"Oh you gods,\" I think, \"what need would we have of any friends if we never needed them? They would be the most useless creatures alive if we never had any use for them. They would be like sweet instruments hung up in their cases, keeping their music to themselves.\" Why, I have often wished I were poorer so that I could come closer to you. We are born to do good to others, and what can we more truly call our own than our friends' riches? Oh, what a precious comfort it is to have so many friends who, like brothers, can command each other's fortunes! Oh joy, spilling over before it can even be born! My eyes can't hold back their tears. To cover up their weakness, I drink to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Thou weepest to make them drink, Timon."],"id":202,"m":"You're crying so you can make them drink, Timon."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Joy had the like conception in our eyes,","And, at that instant like a babe, sprung up."],"id":203,"m":"Joy was conceived in our eyes the same way, and at that moment was born like a baby: in tears."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Ho, ho! I laugh to think that babe a bastard."],"id":204,"m":"Ha! It makes me laugh to think that baby is a bastard."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD LORD","lines":["I promise you, my lord, you mov'd me much."],"id":205,"m":"I promise you, my lord, you moved me deeply."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Much!"],"id":206,"m":"Deeply!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Tucket sounded.","id":207},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["What means that trump?"],"id":208,"m":"What does that trumpet mean?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a SERVANT.","id":209},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","cont":1,"lines":["How now!"],"id":210,"m":"What is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Please you, my lord, there are certain ladies most desirous of","admittance."],"id":211,"m":"If you please, my lord, there are some ladies who very much want to come in."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Ladies? What are their wills?"],"id":212,"m":"Ladies? What do they want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["There comes with them a forerunner, my lord, which bears","that office, to signify their pleasures."],"id":213,"m":"They have sent a messenger ahead of them, my lord, whose job is to explain what they wish."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["I pray, let them be admitted."],"id":214,"m":"Please, let them in."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CUPID.","id":215},{"t":"sp","who":"CUPID","lines":["Hail to thee, worthy Timon; and to all","That of his bounties taste! The five best Senses","Acknowledge thee their patron; and come freely","To gratulate thy plenteous bosom. Th' Ear,","Taste, Touch, Smell, pleas'd from thy table rise;","They only now come but to feast thine eyes."],"id":216,"m":"Greetings to you, worthy Timon, and to everyone who enjoys his generosity! The five senses acknowledge you as their patron and come of their own accord to praise your generous heart. Hearing, taste, touch and smell rise satisfied from your table. Now they have only come to feast your eyes."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["They are welcome all; let 'em have kind admittance:","Music, make their welcome!"],"id":217,"m":"They are all welcome. Let them in kindly. Music, welcome them!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit CUPID.","id":218},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["You see, my lord, how ample you're belov'd."],"id":219,"m":"You see, my lord, how widely you are loved."},{"t":"sd","text":"Music. Re-enter CUPID, with a masque of LADIES as Amazons, with lutes in their hands, dancing and playing.","id":220},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Hoy-day! what a sweep of vanity comes this way:","They dance! they are mad women.","Like madness is the glory of this life,","As this pomp shows to a little oil and root.","We make ourselves fools to disport ourselves;","And spend our flatteries to drink those men","Upon whose age we void it up again,","With poisonous spite and envy.","Who lives that's not depraved or depraves?","Who dies that bears not one spurn to their graves","Of their friend's gift?","I should fear those that dance before me now","Would one day stamp upon me: it has been done:","Men shut their doors against a setting sun."],"id":221,"m":"Well, well! What a parade of vanity is coming this way! They're dancing! They're mad women. The glory of this life is just as mad as this display looks next to a meal of a little oil and roots. We make fools of ourselves to entertain ourselves, and waste our flattery toasting men whom, when they're old, we spit back out with poisonous spite and envy. Who lives who isn't corrupted or corrupting others? Who dies without taking to the grave some kick given by a friend? I'd be afraid that the women dancing in front of me now would one day stamp on me. It has happened before: men shut their doors on a setting sun."},{"t":"sd","text":"The LORDS rise from table, with much adoring of TIMON; and to show their loves, each singles out an Amazon, and all dance, men with women, a lofty strain or two to the hautboys, and cease.","id":222},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["You have done our pleasures much grace, fair ladies,","Set a fair fashion on our entertainment,","Which was not half so beautiful and kind;","You have added worth unto 't and lustre,","And entertain'd me with mine own device;","I am to thank you for 't."],"id":223,"m":"You have greatly honoured our pleasures, fair ladies. You've decorated our entertainment, which wasn't half so beautiful and charming before. You've given it worth and shine, and entertained me with my own idea. I must thank you for it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LADY","lines":["My lord, you take us even at the best."],"id":224,"m":"My lord, you take us at our very best."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Faith, for the worst is filthy; and would not hold taking, I","doubt me."],"id":225,"m":"Yes, because the worst of them is filthy, and wouldn't bear taking, I suspect."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Ladies, there is an idle banquet","Attends you; please you to dispose yourselves."],"id":226,"m":"Ladies, a light dessert is waiting for you. Please help yourselves."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL LADIES","lines":["Most thankfully, my lord."],"id":227,"m":"With many thanks, my lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt CUPID and LADIES.","id":228},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Flavius!"],"id":229,"m":"Flavius!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["My lord!"],"id":230,"m":"My lord!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["The little casket bring me hither."],"id":231,"m":"Bring me the little jewel box."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["Yes, my lord. [Aside] More jewels yet!","There is no crossing him in 's humour;","Else I should tell him well, i' faith, I should,","When all's spent, he'd be cross'd then, an he could.","'Tis pity bounty had not eyes behind,","That man might ne'er be wretched for his mind."],"id":232,"m":"Yes, my lord. (Aside) More jewels! There's no stopping him when he's in this mood. Otherwise I would tell him plainly, I really would. When it's all spent, he'd want to be stopped then, if he could. It's a pity generosity doesn't have eyes in the back of its head, so that a man would never be ruined by his own good nature."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":233},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Where be our men?"],"id":234,"m":"Where are our servants?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Here, my lord, in readiness."],"id":235,"m":"Here, my lord, ready."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Our horses!"],"id":236,"m":"Our horses!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter FLAVIUS, with the casket.","id":237},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["O, my friends! I have one word to say to you;","Look you, my good lord,","I must entreat you, honour me so much","As to advance this jewel; accept it and wear it,","Kind my lord."],"id":238,"m":"Oh, my friends, I have one more word to say to you. Look, my good lord, I must ask you to do me the honour of improving this jewel by accepting it and wearing it, my kind lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["I am so far already in your gifts—"],"id":239,"m":"I already owe you so much in gifts..."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL LORDS","lines":["So are we all."],"id":240,"m":"So do we all."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a SERVANT.","id":241},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["My lord, there are certain nobles of the Senate","Newly alighted and come to visit you."],"id":242,"m":"My lord, some noblemen of the Senate have just dismounted and have come to visit you."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["They are fairly welcome."],"id":243,"m":"They are very welcome."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["I beseech your honour,","Vouchsafe me a word; it does concern you near."],"id":244,"m":"I beg your honour, allow me a word. It concerns you closely."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Near! why then, another time I'll hear thee.","I prithee let's be provided to show them entertainment."],"id":245,"m":"Closely? Then I'll hear you some other time. Please, let's get ready to entertain them."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["[Aside] I scarce know how."],"id":246,"m":"(Aside) I hardly know how."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter another SERVANT.","id":247},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["May it please your honour, Lord Lucius,","Out of his free love, hath presented to you","Four milk-white horses, trapp'd in silver."],"id":248,"m":"If it please your honour, Lord Lucius, out of pure friendship, has sent you four milk-white horses, harnessed in silver."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["I shall accept them fairly; let the presents","Be worthily entertain'd."],"id":249,"m":"I'll accept them gladly. See that the gifts are properly looked after."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a third SERVANT.","id":250},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","cont":1,"lines":["How now! What news?"],"id":251,"m":"What is it? What news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["Please you, my lord, that honourable gentleman, Lord Lucullus,","entreats your company to-morrow to hunt with him, and has sent","your honour two brace of greyhounds."],"id":252,"m":"If you please, my lord, that honourable gentleman Lord Lucullus asks you to hunt with him tomorrow, and has sent your honour two pairs of greyhounds."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["I'll hunt with him; and let them be receiv'd,","Not without fair reward."],"id":253,"m":"I'll hunt with him. Receive the dogs, and give a generous reward for them."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["[Aside] What will this come to?","He commands us to provide, and give great gifts,","And all out of an empty coffer;","Nor will he know his purse, or yield me this,","To show him what a beggar his heart is,","Being of no power to make his wishes good.","His promises fly so beyond his state","That what he speaks is all in debt; he owes","For every word: he is so kind that he now","Pays interest for 't; his land's put to their books.","Well, would I were gently put out of office","Before I were forc'd out!","Happier he that has no friend to feed","Than such that do e'en enemies exceed.","I bleed inwardly for my lord."],"id":254,"m":"(Aside) Where will this end? He orders us to provide feasts and give great gifts, all out of an empty money chest. He won't find out how much is in his purse, or let me show him what a beggar his heart has become, since it has no power to make its wishes come true. His promises go so far beyond his means that everything he says is in debt. He owes for every word. He is so kind that he now pays interest on his kindness. His lands are mortgaged in his creditors' account books. Well, I wish I could be gently retired from my job before I'm forced out of it! A man with no friends to feed is happier than one whose friends are worse than enemies. I'm bleeding inside for my lord."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":255},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["You do yourselves much wrong;","You bate too much of your own merits;","Here, my lord, a trifle of our love."],"id":256,"m":"You do yourselves an injustice; you underrate your own merits. Here, my lord, a small token of my love."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["With more than common thanks I will receive it."],"id":257,"m":"I accept it with more than ordinary thanks."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD LORD","lines":["O! he's the very soul of bounty!"],"id":258,"m":"Oh, he's the very soul of generosity!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["And now I remember, my lord, you gave","Good words the other day of a bay courser","I rode on: it is yours because you lik'd it."],"id":259,"m":"And now I remember, my lord, you spoke well the other day of a bay horse I was riding. It's yours, because you liked it."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD LORD","lines":["O! I beseech you, pardon me, my lord, in that."],"id":260,"m":"Oh, please excuse me from that, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["You may take my word, my lord: I know no man","Can justly praise but what he does affect:","I weigh my friend's affection with mine own.","I'll tell you true; I'll call to you."],"id":261,"m":"You can take my word for it, my lord: I know no man truly praises anything unless he wants it. I value my friend's desire as I value my own. I'm telling you the truth. I'll call on you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL LORDS","lines":["O! none so welcome!"],"id":262,"m":"Oh, nobody would be so welcome!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["I take all and your several visitations","So kind to heart, 'tis not enough to give;","Methinks, I could deal kingdoms to my friends,","And ne'er be weary. Alcibiades,","Thou art a soldier, therefore seldom rich;","It comes in charity to thee; for all thy living","Is 'mongst the dead, and all the lands thou hast","Lie in a pitch'd field."],"id":263,"m":"I take all your visits so kindly to heart that giving isn't enough. I feel I could hand out kingdoms to my friends and never grow tired. Alcibiades, you're a soldier, and so seldom rich. It's charity to give to you, because your whole income comes from the dead, and all the land you own is battlefields."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["Ay, defil'd land, my lord."],"id":264,"m":"Yes, land soiled with blood, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["We are so virtuously bound,—"],"id":265,"m":"We are so deeply obliged to you..."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["And so am I to you."],"id":266,"m":"And so am I to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["So infinitely endear'd,—"],"id":267,"m":"So endlessly devoted..."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["All to you. Lights, more lights!"],"id":268,"m":"I'm entirely yours. Lights, more lights!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["The best of happiness,","Honour, and fortunes, keep with you, Lord Timon!"],"id":269,"m":"May the greatest happiness, honour and fortune stay with you, Lord Timon!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Ready for his friends."],"id":270,"m":"And always ready for his friends."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt ALCIBIADES, Lords, etc.","id":271},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["What a coil's here!","Serving of becks and jutting out of bums!","I doubt whether their legs be worth the sums","That are given for 'em. Friendship's full of dregs:","Methinks, false hearts should never have sound legs.","Thus honest fools lay out their wealth on curtsies."],"id":272,"m":"What a fuss there is here! Bowing and scraping and sticking out of bottoms! I doubt whether their bending legs are worth the sums paid for them. Friendship is full of dregs. It seems to me that false hearts should never have healthy legs. That's how honest fools spend their wealth on bows."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Now, Apemantus, if thou wert not sullen,","I would be good to thee."],"id":273,"m":"Now, Apemantus, if you weren't so sulky, I would be generous to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["No, I'll nothing; for if I should be bribed too, there","would be none left to rail upon thee, and then thou wouldst sin","the faster. Thou givest so long, Timon, I fear me thou wilt give","away thyself in paper shortly: What needs these feasts, pomps,","and vain-glories?"],"id":274,"m":"No, I'll take nothing. If I were bribed too, there would be nobody left to criticise you, and then you would sin all the faster. You give so much, Timon, that I'm afraid you'll soon give yourself away in IOUs. What's the need for these feasts, this pomp and empty show?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Nay, an you begin to rail on society once, I am sworn not to","give regard to you. Farewell; and come with better music."],"id":275,"m":"No, if you're going to start attacking good company, I've sworn not to listen to you. Goodbye, and come back with a better tune."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":276},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["So: Thou wilt not hear me now; thou shalt not then;","I'll lock thy heaven from thee.","O! that men's ears should be","To counsel deaf, but not to flattery!"],"id":277,"m":"So, you won't hear me now. Then you shan't hear me later, when you need to. I'll lock your heaven away from you. Oh, that men's ears should be deaf to good advice but not to flattery!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":278},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"Athens. A senator's house.","id":279},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter A SENATOR, with papers in his hand.","id":280},{"t":"sp","who":"SENATOR","lines":["And late, five thousand: to Varro and to Isidore","He owes nine thousand; besides my former sum,","Which makes it five-and-twenty. Still in motion","Of raging waste! It cannot hold; it will not.","If I want gold, steal but a beggar's dog","And give it Timon, why, the dog coins gold;","If I would sell my horse, and buy twenty more","Better than he, why, give my horse to Timon,","Ask nothing, give it him, it foals me straight,","And able horses. No porter at his gate,","But rather one that smiles and still invites","All that pass by. It cannot hold; no reason","Can found his state in safety. Caphis, ho!","Caphis, I say!"],"id":281,"m":"And recently five thousand. He owes Varro and Isidore nine thousand, besides the sum I lent him before, which makes it twenty-five thousand. And still he goes on wasting madly! It can't last, it won't. If I want gold, I need only steal a beggar's dog and give it to Timon, and the dog turns into gold. If I wanted to sell my horse and buy twenty better ones, I need only give my horse to Timon, asking nothing, and straight away it gives birth to fine grown horses for me. There's no porter at his gate to keep people out, only one who smiles and invites in everyone who passes. It can't last. No reasoning can find his fortune safe. Caphis, hey! Caphis, I say!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CAPHIS.","id":282},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPHIS","lines":["Here, sir; what is your pleasure?"],"id":283,"m":"Here, sir. What do you want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SENATOR","lines":["Get on your cloak, and haste you to Lord Timon;","Importune him for my moneys; be not ceas'd","With slight denial, nor then silenc'd when—","'Commend me to your master'—and the cap","Plays in the right hand, thus;—but tell him,","My uses cry to me; I must serve my turn","Out of mine own; his days and times are past,","And my reliances on his fracted dates","Have smit my credit: I love and honour him,","But must not break my back to heal his finger;","Immediate are my needs, and my relief","Must not be toss'd and turn'd to me in words,","But find supply immediate. Get you gone:","Put on a most importunate aspect,","A visage of demand; for I do fear,","When every feather sticks in his own wing,","Lord Timon will be left a naked gull,","Which flashes now a phoenix. Get you gone."],"id":284,"m":"Put on your cloak and hurry to Lord Timon. Press him for my money. Don't be put off by a feeble refusal, or silenced when he says \"Give my regards to your master\" while twirling his hat in his right hand, like this. Tell him my needs are calling for it; I have to serve my own turn out of my own money. His payment dates have passed, and by relying on his broken promises I've damaged my own credit. I love and honour him, but I can't break my own back to heal his finger. My needs are urgent, and my relief mustn't be tossed back to me in words; it must come at once. Go. Put on a most demanding face, the look of a man asking for what he's owed. I'm afraid that when every borrowed feather is back in its own bird's wing, Lord Timon, who now shines like a phoenix, will be left a naked, plucked fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPHIS","lines":["I go, sir."],"id":285,"m":"I'm going, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SENATOR","lines":["Take the bonds along with you,","And have the dates in compt."],"id":286,"m":"Take the bonds with you, and have the dates worked out."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPHIS","lines":["I will, sir."],"id":287,"m":"I will, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SENATOR","lines":["Go."],"id":288,"m":"Go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":289},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"II","loc":"Athens. Before Timon's house.","id":290},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter FLAVIUS, with many bills in his hand.","id":291},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["No care, no stop! So senseless of expense,","That he will neither know how to maintain it,","Nor cease his flow of riot: takes no account","How things go from him, nor resumes no care","Of what is to continue: never mind","Was to be so unwise, to be so kind.","What shall be done? He will not hear, till feel:","I must be round with him. Now he comes from hunting.","Fie, fie, fie, fie!"],"id":292,"m":"No caution, no stopping! He's so unaware of what he spends that he neither knows how to pay for it nor will stop his flood of extravagance. He takes no notice of how things slip away from him, and gives no thought to how he'll carry on. Never was a mind so unwise as to be so kind. What can be done? He won't listen until he feels the pain. I must be blunt with him. Here he comes back from hunting. Oh dear, oh dear!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter CAPHIS, and the SERVANTS Of ISIDORE and VARRO.","id":293},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPHIS","lines":["Good even, Varro. What! You come for money?"],"id":294,"m":"Good evening, Varro. What, have you come for money?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VARRO'S SERVANT","lines":["Is't not your business too?"],"id":295,"m":"Isn't that your business too?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPHIS","lines":["It is: and yours too, Isidore?"],"id":296,"m":"It is. And yours too, Isidore?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISIDORE'S SERVANT","lines":["It is so."],"id":297,"m":"It is."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPHIS","lines":["Would we were all discharg'd!"],"id":298,"m":"I wish we were all paid off!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VARRO'S SERVANT","lines":["I fear it."],"id":299,"m":"I doubt it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPHIS","lines":["Here comes the lord!"],"id":300,"m":"Here comes the lord!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter TIMON, ALCIBIADES, and Lords, etc.","id":301},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["So soon as dinner's done, we'll forth again.","My Alcibiades. With me? what is your will?"],"id":302,"m":"As soon as dinner's over, we'll go out again, my Alcibiades. Do you want me? What do you want?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPHIS","lines":["My lord, here is a note of certain dues."],"id":303,"m":"My lord, here's a list of certain debts."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Dues! Whence are you?"],"id":304,"m":"Debts! Where are you from?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPHIS","lines":["Of Athens here, my lord."],"id":305,"m":"From here in Athens, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Go to my steward."],"id":306,"m":"Go to my steward."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPHIS","lines":["Please it your lordship, he hath put me off","To the succession of new days this month:","My master is awak'd by great occasion","To call upon his own; and humbly prays you","That with your other noble parts you'll suit","In giving him his right."],"id":307,"m":"If it please your lordship, he has put me off from day to day all this month. My master is pressed by great need to call in what's his, and humbly begs you to pay him what he's owed, as befits your other noble qualities."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Mine honest friend,","I prithee, but repair to me next morning."],"id":308,"m":"My honest friend, please just come back to me tomorrow morning."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPHIS","lines":["Nay, good my lord,—"],"id":309,"m":"No, my good lord..."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Contain thyself, good friend."],"id":310,"m":"Control yourself, good friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"VARRO'S SERVANT","lines":["One Varro's servant, my good lord,—"],"id":311,"m":"I'm Varro's servant, my good lord..."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISIDORE'S SERVANT","lines":["From Isidore; he humbly prays your speedy payment."],"id":312,"m":"I'm from Isidore. He humbly asks you for quick payment."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPHIS","lines":["If you did know, my lord, my master's wants,—"],"id":313,"m":"If you knew, my lord, how much my master needs it..."},{"t":"sp","who":"VARRO'S SERVANT","lines":["'Twas due on forfeiture, my lord, six weeks and past."],"id":314,"m":"It was due on penalty, my lord, more than six weeks ago."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISIDORE'S SERVANT","lines":["Your steward puts me off, my lord; and","I am sent expressly to your lordship."],"id":315,"m":"Your steward keeps putting me off, my lord, and I have been sent directly to your lordship."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Give me breath.","I do beseech you, good my lords, keep on;","I'll wait upon you instantly."],"id":316,"m":"Give me room to breathe. Please, my good lords, go on ahead. I'll join you in a moment."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt ALCIBIADES and LORDS.","id":317},{"t":"sd","text":"To FLAVIUS.","id":318},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","cont":1,"lines":["Come hither: pray you,","How goes the world, that I am thus encounter'd","With clamorous demands of date-broke bonds,","And the detention of long-since-due debts,","Against my honour?"],"id":319,"m":"Come here. Please tell me, what's going on, that I'm attacked like this with noisy demands for bonds past their date and debts long overdue, to the harm of my honour?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["Please you, gentlemen,","The time is unagreeable to this business:","Your importunacy cease till after dinner,","That I may make his lordship understand","Wherefore you are not paid."],"id":320,"m":"If you please, gentlemen, this isn't a good time for this business. Stop pressing until after dinner, so that I can explain to his lordship why you haven't been paid."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Do so, my friends.","See them well entertain'd."],"id":321,"m":"Do that, my friends. (To Flavius) See that they're well looked after."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":322},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["Pray, draw near."],"id":323,"m":"Please, come this way."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":324},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter APEMANTUS and FOOL.","id":325},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPHIS","lines":["Stay, stay; here comes the fool with Apemantus:","Let's ha' some sport with 'em."],"id":326,"m":"Wait, wait. Here comes the Fool with Apemantus. Let's have some fun with them."},{"t":"sp","who":"VARRO'S SERVANT","lines":["Hang him, he'll abuse us!"],"id":327,"m":"Hang him, he'll insult us!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ISIDORE'S SERVANT","lines":["A plague upon him, dog!"],"id":328,"m":"A plague on him, the dog!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VARRO'S SERVANT","lines":["How dost, fool?"],"id":329,"m":"How are you, fool?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Dost dialogue with thy shadow?"],"id":330,"m":"Are you talking to your own shadow?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VARRO'S SERVANT","lines":["I speak not to thee."],"id":331,"m":"I'm not speaking to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["No; 'tis to thyself. [To the FOOL]","Come away."],"id":332,"m":"No, you're speaking to yourself. (To the Fool) Come on."},{"t":"sp","who":"ISIDORE'S SERVANT","lines":["[To VARRO'S SERVANT] There's the fool hangs on your back already."],"id":333,"m":"(To Varro's Servant) There, the fool is hanging on your back already."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["No, thou stand'st single; thou'rt not on him yet."],"id":334,"m":"No, you're standing alone. You haven't climbed onto him yet."},{"t":"sp","who":"CAPHIS","lines":["Where's the fool now?"],"id":335,"m":"Where's the fool now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["He last asked the question. Poor rogues and usurers'","men! bawds between gold and want!"],"id":336,"m":"The last one who asked a question. Poor rogues and moneylenders' servants! Pimps between gold and need!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL SERVANTS","lines":["What are we, Apemantus?"],"id":337,"m":"What are we, Apemantus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Asses."],"id":338,"m":"Asses."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL SERVANTS","lines":["Why?"],"id":339,"m":"Why?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["That you ask me what you are, and do not know yourselves. Speak","to 'em, fool."],"id":340,"m":"Because you ask me what you are, and don't know yourselves. Talk to them, fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOOL","lines":["How do you, gentlemen?"],"id":341,"m":"How are you, gentlemen?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL SERVANTS","lines":["Gramercies, good fool. How does your mistress?"],"id":342,"m":"Thank you, good fool. How's your mistress?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FOOL","lines":["She's e'en setting on water to scald such chickens as you","are. Would we could see you at Corinth!"],"id":343,"m":"She's just putting water on to boil, to scald chickens like you. I wish we could see you at Corinth!"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Good! gramercy."],"id":344,"m":"Good! Thank you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter PAGE.","id":345},{"t":"sp","who":"FOOL","lines":["Look you, here comes my mistress' page."],"id":346,"m":"Look, here comes my mistress's page."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["[To the FOOL] Why, how now, Captain! what do you in this wise company? How dost","thou, Apemantus?"],"id":347,"m":"(To the Fool) Well, hello, captain! What are you doing in this wise company? How are you, Apemantus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Would I had a rod in my mouth, that I might answer thee","profitably."],"id":348,"m":"I wish I had a cane in my mouth, so I could give you a useful answer."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Prithee, Apemantus, read me the superscription of these","letters: I know not which is which."],"id":349,"m":"Please, Apemantus, read me the addresses on these letters. I don't know which is which."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Canst not read?"],"id":350,"m":"Can't you read?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["No."],"id":351,"m":"No."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["There will little learning die, then, that day thou art","hanged. This is to Lord Timon; this to Alcibiades. Go; thou wast","born a bastard, and thou'lt die a bawd."],"id":352,"m":"Then there won't be much learning lost on the day you're hanged. This one is to Lord Timon, this one to Alcibiades. Go. You were born a bastard, and you'll die a pimp."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Thou wast whelped a dog, and thou shalt famish a dog's death.","Answer not; I am gone."],"id":353,"m":"You were born a puppy, and you'll starve to death like a dog. Don't answer. I've gone."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit PAGE.","id":354},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["E'en so thou outrunn'st grace.—","Fool, I will go with you to Lord Timon's."],"id":355,"m":"In just the same way you run away from grace. Fool, I'll go with you to Lord Timon's."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOOL","lines":["Will you leave me there?"],"id":356,"m":"Will you leave me there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["If Timon stay at home. You three serve three usurers?"],"id":357,"m":"If Timon stays at home. You three serve three moneylenders?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL SERVANTS","lines":["Ay; would they served us!"],"id":358,"m":"Yes. We wish they served us!"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["So would I, as good a trick as ever hangman served thief."],"id":359,"m":"So do I, the same way the hangman serves a thief."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOOL","lines":["Are you three usurers' men?"],"id":360,"m":"Are you three moneylenders' men?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL SERVANTS","lines":["Ay, fool."],"id":361,"m":"Yes, fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOOL","lines":["I think no usurer but has a fool to his servant: my mistress","is one, and I am her fool. When men come to borrow of your","masters, they approach sadly, and go away merry; but they enter","my mistress' house merrily, and go away sadly: the reason of","this?"],"id":362,"m":"I think every moneylender has a fool as his servant. My mistress is one, and I'm her fool. When men come to borrow from your masters, they arrive sad and go away happy. But they come into my mistress's house happy and go away sad. What's the reason for that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VARRO'S SERVANT","lines":["I could render one."],"id":363,"m":"I could give one."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Do it, then, that we may account thee a whoremaster and a","knave; which notwithstanding, thou shalt be no less esteemed."],"id":364,"m":"Do it, then, so we can count you a whoremonger and a rogue. Even so, you won't be thought any less of."},{"t":"sp","who":"VARRO'S SERVANT","lines":["What is a whoremaster, fool?"],"id":365,"m":"What's a whoremonger, fool?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FOOL","lines":["A fool in good clothes, and something like thee. 'Tis a","spirit: sometime 't appears like a lord; sometime like a lawyer;","sometime like a philosopher, with two stones more than's","artificial one. He is very often like a knight; and generally,","in all shapes that man goes up and down in from fourscore to","thirteen, this spirit walks in."],"id":366,"m":"A fool in good clothes, something like you. It's a spirit. Sometimes it looks like a lord, sometimes like a lawyer, sometimes like a philosopher with two stones besides his philosopher's stone. Very often it looks like a knight. In general, this spirit walks around in every shape that men take, from eighty to thirteen."},{"t":"sp","who":"VARRO'S SERVANT","lines":["Thou art not altogether a fool."],"id":367,"m":"You're not entirely a fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOOL","lines":["Nor thou altogether a wise man:","as much foolery as I have, so much wit thou lackest."],"id":368,"m":"And you're not entirely a wise man. Whatever foolery I have, you lack that much sense."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["That answer might have become Apemantus."],"id":369,"m":"That answer might have come from Apemantus."},{"t":"sp","who":"VARRO'S SERVANT","lines":["Aside, aside; here comes Lord Timon."],"id":370,"m":"Step aside, step aside. Here comes Lord Timon."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter TIMON and FLAVIUS.","id":371},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Come with me, fool, come."],"id":372,"m":"Come with me, fool, come."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOOL","lines":["I do not always follow lover, elder brother, and woman;","sometime the philosopher."],"id":373,"m":"I don't always follow a lover, an elder brother and a woman. Sometimes I follow the philosopher."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt APEMANTUS and FOOL.","id":374},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["Pray you walk near: I'll speak with you anon."],"id":375,"m":"Please wait nearby. I'll speak to you soon."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt SERVANTS.","id":376},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["You make me marvel: wherefore, ere this time,","Had you not fully laid my state before me,","That I might so have rated my expense","As I had leave of means?"],"id":377,"m":"You amaze me. Why didn't you set out my financial position fully before now, so that I could have matched my spending to what I could afford?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["You would not hear me,","At many leisures I propos'd."],"id":378,"m":"You wouldn't listen to me, at many free moments when I tried."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Go to:","Perchance some single vantages you took,","When my indisposition put you back;","And that unaptness made your minister","Thus to excuse yourself."],"id":379,"m":"Come on. Perhaps you seized on a few odd moments when I wasn't in the mood and turned you away, and now you use that unwillingness to excuse yourself."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["O my good lord!","At many times I brought in my accounts,","Laid them before you; you would throw them off,","And say you found them in mine honesty.","When for some trifling present you have bid me","Return so much, I have shook my head, and wept;","Yea, 'gainst the authority of manners, pray'd you","To hold your hand more close: I did endure","Not seldom, nor no slight checks, when I have","Prompted you in the ebb of your estate","And your great flow of debts. My loved lord,","Though you hear now, too late, yet now's a time,","The greatest of your having lacks a half","To pay your present debts."],"id":380,"m":"Oh, my good lord! Many times I brought in my accounts and laid them in front of you. You would push them away and say you trusted my honesty. When you ordered me to give back a large sum in return for some trivial gift, I shook my head and wept. Yes, against all proper manners, I begged you to keep a tighter hold on your money. I often had to put up with harsh rebukes when I reminded you of your shrinking fortune and your swelling debts. My dear lord, though you're listening now, too late, now is the time to hear it: the whole of what you own is not enough to pay half your present debts."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Let all my land be sold."],"id":381,"m":"Sell all my land."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["'Tis all engag'd, some forfeited and gone;","And what remains will hardly stop the mouth","Of present dues; the future comes apace:","What shall defend the interim? and at length","How goes our reckoning?"],"id":382,"m":"It's all mortgaged, some of it forfeited and gone, and what's left will hardly stop the mouths of the debts already due. Future payments are coming fast. What will see us through until then? And in the end, how will our accounts stand?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["To Lacedaemon did my land extend."],"id":383,"m":"My lands stretched as far as Sparta."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["O my good lord! the world is but a word;","Were it all yours to give it in a breath,","How quickly were it gone!"],"id":384,"m":"Oh, my good lord, the world is only a word. If it were all yours, and you could give it away in one breath, how quickly it would be gone!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["You tell me true."],"id":385,"m":"You're right."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["If you suspect my husbandry or falsehood,","Call me before the exactest auditors","And set me on the proof. So the gods bless me,","When all our offices have been oppress'd","With riotous feeders, when our vaults have wept","With drunken spilth of wine, when every room","Hath blaz'd with lights and bray'd with minstrelsy,","I have retir'd me to a wasteful cock,","And set mine eyes at flow."],"id":386,"m":"If you suspect my management or my honesty, call me before the strictest auditors and make me prove myself. As the gods bless me, when all our kitchens and pantries were crammed with greedy guests, when our cellars wept with spilled drunken wine, when every room blazed with lights and roared with music, I went off alone to a running tap and let my own tears run too."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Prithee, no more."],"id":387,"m":"Please, no more."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["Heavens! have I said, the bounty of this lord!","How many prodigal bits have slaves and peasants","This night englutted! Who is not Timon's?","What heart, head, sword, force, means, but is Lord Timon's?","Great Timon, noble, worthy, royal Timon!'","Ah! when the means are gone that buy this praise,","The breath is gone whereof this praise is made:","Feast-won, fast-lost; one cloud of winter showers,","These flies are couch'd."],"id":388,"m":"\"Heavens,\" people said, \"how generous this lord is! How many lavish mouthfuls have slaves and peasants gobbled up tonight! Who doesn't belong to Timon? What heart, head, sword, strength or wealth isn't Lord Timon's? Great Timon, noble, worthy, royal Timon!\" Ah, when the money that buys this praise is gone, the breath that makes the praise is gone too. Won by a feast, lost by a fast. One cloud of winter showers and these flies hide away."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Come, sermon me no further;","No villainous bounty yet hath pass'd my heart;","Unwisely, not ignobly, have I given.","Why dost thou weep? Canst thou the conscience lack,","To think I shall lack friends? Secure thy heart;","If I would broach the vessels of my love,","And try the argument of hearts by borrowing,","Men and men's fortunes could I frankly use","As I can bid thee speak."],"id":389,"m":"Come, don't preach at me any more. My heart has never yet given anything for a wicked purpose. I have given unwisely, but not shamefully. Why are you crying? Do you really have so little faith as to think I'll lack friends? Set your heart at rest. If I open the casks of my friends' love and test their hearts by borrowing, I could use men and their fortunes as freely as I can tell you to speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["Assurance bless your thoughts!"],"id":390,"m":"May your confidence prove true!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["And, in some sort, these wants of mine are crown'd","That I account them blessings; for by these","Shall I try friends. You shall perceive how you","Mistake my fortunes; I am wealthy in my friends.","Within there! Flaminius! Servilius!"],"id":391,"m":"And in a way these needs of mine are a crown, because I count them as blessings. By them I'll test my friends. You'll see how wrong you are about my fortune. I'm rich in my friends. Hey, in there! Flaminius! Servilius!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter FLAMINIUS, SERVILIUS, and other Servants.","id":392},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANTS","lines":["My lord! my lord!"],"id":393,"m":"My lord! My lord!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["I will dispatch you severally: you to Lord Lucius; to Lord","Lucullus you: I hunted with his honour to-day; you, to","Sempronius. Commend me to their loves; and I am proud, say, that","my occasions have found time to use them toward a supply of","money: let the request be fifty talents."],"id":394,"m":"I'll send you each on a separate errand. You go to Lord Lucius. You to Lord Lucullus; I went hunting with his honour today. You go to Sempronius. Give them my love, and say that I'm proud that my needs have given me a chance to use them for a loan of money. Ask for fifty talents."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAMINIUS","lines":["As you have said, my lord."],"id":395,"m":"As you say, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["[Aside] Lord Lucius and Lucullus? hum!"],"id":396,"m":"(Aside) Lord Lucius and Lucullus? Hmm!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["[To another Servant] Go you, sir, to the senators,—","Of whom, even to the state's best health, I have","Deserv'd this hearing,—Bid 'em send o' the instant","A thousand talents to me."],"id":397,"m":"(To another Servant) You, sir, go to the senators, whom I have served well enough, even for the good of the state, to deserve a hearing. Tell them to send me a thousand talents at once."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["I have been bold,—","For that I knew it the most general way,—","To them to use your signet and your name;","But they do shake their heads, and I am here","No richer in return."],"id":398,"m":"I was bold enough, since I knew it was the usual way, to use your seal and your name with them already. But they shook their heads, and I came back no richer."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Is't true? can't be?"],"id":399,"m":"Is that true? Can it be?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["They answer, in a joint and corporate voice,","That now they are at fall, want treasure, cannot","Do what they would; are sorry; you are honourable;","But yet they could have wish'd; they know not;","Something hath been amiss; a noble nature","May catch a wrench; would all were well; 'tis pity;","And so, intending other serious matters,","After distasteful looks, and these hard fractions,","With certain half-caps and cold-moving nods,","They froze me into silence."],"id":400,"m":"They reply, all together in one voice, that just now they're at a low point, short of money, can't do what they would like; they're sorry; you're honourable; but still they could have wished; they don't know; something has gone wrong; a noble nature can slip; they wish all were well; it's a pity. And so, pretending to have other important business, with sour looks and these broken-off phrases, with half-lifted hats and cold nods, they froze me into silence."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["You gods, reward them!","Prithee, man, look cheerly. These old fellows","Have their ingratitude in them hereditary;","Their blood is cak'd, 'tis cold, it seldom flows;","'Tis lack of kindly warmth they are not kind;","And nature, as it grows again toward earth,","Is fashion'd for the journey, dull and heavy.","[To a Servant] Go to Ventidius.—[To Flavius]","Prithee, be not sad,","Thou art true and honest; ingenuously I speak,","No blame belongs to thee.—[To Servant] Ventidius lately","Buried his father; by whose death he's stepp'd","Into a great estate. When he was poor,","Imprison'd and in scarcity of friends,","I clear'd him with five talents; greet him from me,","Bid him suppose some good necessity","Touches his friend, which craves to be remember'd","With those five talents."],"id":401,"m":"May the gods repay them! Please, man, cheer up. These old men have ingratitude in their blood by inheritance. Their blood is clotted and cold; it hardly flows. They aren't kind because they lack natural warmth. And human nature, as it grows back towards the earth, becomes dull and heavy for the journey to the grave. (To a Servant) Go to Ventidius. (To Flavius) Please, don't be sad. You're loyal and honest. I say it frankly: you're not to blame. (To the Servant) Ventidius has just buried his father, and by his death he has come into a great fortune. When he was poor, in prison and short of friends, I freed him with five talents. Greet him for me. Tell him to imagine that some real need has touched his friend, which asks to be remembered with those five talents."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Servant.","id":402},{"t":"sd","text":"To Flavius.","id":403},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","cont":1,"lines":["That had, give't these fellows","To whom 'tis instant due. Ne'er speak, or think","That Timon's fortunes 'mong his friends can sink."],"id":404,"m":"(To Flavius) When you have it, give it to these men who must be paid at once. Never say or think that Timon's fortune can sink while he is among his friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["I would I could not think it:","That thought is bounty's foe;","Being free itself, it thinks all others so."],"id":405,"m":"I wish I couldn't think it. That thought is the enemy of generosity. Being generous itself, it thinks everyone else is too."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":406},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"Athens. A room in Lucullus' house.","id":407},{"t":"sd","text":"FLAMINIUS waiting.","id":408},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a SERVANT to him.","id":409},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["I have told my lord of you; he is coming down to you."],"id":410,"m":"I've told my lord about you. He's coming down to see you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAMINIUS","lines":["I thank you, sir."],"id":411,"m":"Thank you, sir."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter LUCULLUS.","id":412},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Here's my lord."],"id":413,"m":"Here's my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCULLUS","lines":["[Aside] One of Lord Timon's men! a gift, I warrant. Why, this","hits right; I dreamt of a silver basin and ewer to-night.","Flaminius, honest Flaminius, you are very respectively","welcome, sir. Fill me some wine."],"id":414,"m":"(Aside) One of Lord Timon's men! A present, I bet. Why, this fits nicely: I dreamed of a silver basin and jug last night. (To Flaminius) Flaminius, honest Flaminius, you're most respectfully welcome, sir. (To the Servant) Pour me some wine."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit SERVANT.","id":415},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCULLUS","cont":1,"lines":["And how does that honourable, complete, freehearted","gentleman of Athens, thy very bountiful good lord and master?"],"id":416,"m":"And how is that honourable, perfect, generous gentleman of Athens, your very bountiful good lord and master?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAMINIUS","lines":["His health is well, sir."],"id":417,"m":"He's in good health, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCULLUS","lines":["I am right glad that his health is well, sir. And what","hast thou there under thy cloak, pretty Flaminius?"],"id":418,"m":"I'm very glad he's in good health, sir. And what have you got there under your cloak, pretty Flaminius?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAMINIUS","lines":["Faith, nothing but an empty box, sir; which in my lord's","behalf, I come to entreat your honour to supply; who, having","great and instant occasion to use fifty talents, hath sent to","your lordship to furnish him, nothing doubting your present","assistance therein."],"id":419,"m":"Honestly, nothing but an empty box, sir, which I've come on my lord's behalf to beg your honour to fill. He has a great and urgent need for fifty talents and has sent to your lordship to supply him, not doubting your immediate help."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCULLUS","lines":["La, la, la, la! 'Nothing doubting,' says he? Alas, good","lord! a noble gentleman 'tis, if he would not keep so good a","house. Many a time and often I ha' dined with him, and told him","on't; and come again to supper to him, of purpose to have him","spend less; and yet he would embrace no counsel, take no warning","by my coming. Every man has his fault, and honesty is his. I ha'","told him on't, but I could ne'er get him from it."],"id":420,"m":"La, la, la, la! \"Not doubting,\" he says? Oh dear, good lord! He's a noble gentleman, if only he didn't keep such an extravagant house. Time and again I've dined with him and told him so, and come back to supper with him on purpose to get him to spend less. But he wouldn't take any advice or any warning from my visits. Every man has his fault, and his is honesty. I've told him so, but I could never get him to change."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter SERVANT with wine.","id":421},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Please your lordship, here is the wine."],"id":422,"m":"If it please your lordship, here's the wine."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCULLUS","lines":["Flaminius, I have noted thee always wise. Here's to thee."],"id":423,"m":"Flaminius, I've always found you sensible. Here's to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAMINIUS","lines":["Your lordship speaks your pleasure."],"id":424,"m":"Your lordship is pleased to say so."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCULLUS","lines":["I have observed thee always for a towardly prompt spirit,","give thee thy due, and one that knows what belongs to reason, and","canst use the time well, if the time use thee well: good parts in","thee. [To SERVANT]—Get you gone, sirrah.—"],"id":425,"m":"I've always seen you as a willing, quick-witted fellow, to give you your due, and one who knows what is reasonable and can make good use of an opportunity, if the opportunity treats you well. You have good qualities. (To the Servant) Off you go, fellow."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit SERVANT.","id":426},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCULLUS","cont":1,"lines":["Draw nearer, honest Flaminius. Thy lord's a bountiful gentleman;","but thou art wise, and thou know'st well enough, although thou","comest to me, that this is no time to lend money, especially upon","bare friendship without security. Here's three solidares for","thee: good boy, wink at me, and say thou sawest me not. Fare thee","well."],"id":427,"m":"Come closer, honest Flaminius. Your lord is a generous gentleman, but you're sensible, and you know well enough, even though you've come to me, that this is no time to lend money, especially on mere friendship without any security. Here are three coins for you. Good boy, turn a blind eye and say you didn't see me. Goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAMINIUS","lines":["Is't possible the world should so much differ,","And we alive that liv'd? Fly, damned baseness,","To him that worships thee."],"id":428,"m":"Is it possible that the world can change so much while we who lived in it are still alive? Fly, damned meanness, back to the man who worships you!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Throwing the money away.","id":429},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCULLUS","lines":["Ha! now I see thou art a fool, and fit for thy master."],"id":430,"m":"Ha! Now I see you're a fool, a fit servant for your master."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":431},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAMINIUS","lines":["May these add to the number that may scald thee!","Let molten coin be thy damnation,","Thou disease of a friend, and not himself!","Has friendship such a faint and milky heart","It turns in less than two nights? O you gods!","I feel my master's passion! This slave unto his honour","Has my lord's meat in him:","Why should it thrive and turn to nutriment","When he is turn'd to poison?","O! may diseases only work upon't!","And when he's sick to death, let not that part of nature","Which my lord paid for, be of any power","To expel sickness, but prolong his hour."],"id":432,"m":"May these coins be among those that boil you in hell! May molten money be your damnation, you sickness pretending to be a friend! Has friendship such a weak and milky heart that it turns sour in less than two nights? Oh you gods! I feel my master's anger. This slave to his own rank still has my lord's food inside him. Why should it do him good and nourish him, when he himself has turned to poison? Oh, may it only feed diseases! And when he's sick to death, may the part of his body that my lord paid for have no power to drive out the sickness, but only drag out his dying."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":433},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"Athens. A public place.","id":434},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucius, with three STRANGERS.","id":435},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Who, the Lord Timon? he is my very good friend, and an","honourable gentleman."],"id":436,"m":"Who, Lord Timon? He's a very good friend of mine and an honourable gentleman."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST STRANGER","lines":["We know him for no less, though we are but strangers to him. But","I can tell you one thing, my lord, and which I hear from common","rumours: now Lord Timon's happy hours are done and past, and his","estate shrinks from him."],"id":437,"m":"We know he is, though we're only strangers to him. But I can tell you one thing, my lord, which I hear from common gossip: Lord Timon's happy times are over, and his fortune is shrinking away."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Fie, no, do not believe it; he cannot want for money."],"id":438,"m":"Nonsense, don't believe it. He can't be short of money."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND STRANGER","lines":["But believe you this, my lord, that, not long ago, one of his men","was with the Lord Lucullus to borrow so many talents, nay, urged","extremely for't, and showed what necessity belonged to't, and yet","was denied."],"id":439,"m":"But believe this, my lord: not long ago one of his men went to Lord Lucullus to borrow a number of talents, and pressed him hard for them, and explained how badly they were needed, and still he was refused."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["How!"],"id":440,"m":"What!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND STRANGER","lines":["I tell you, denied, my lord."],"id":441,"m":"I tell you, refused, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["What a strange case was that! now, before the gods, I am","ashamed on't. Denied that honourable man! there was very little","honour showed in't. For my own part, I must needs confess, I have","received some small kindnesses from him, as money, plate, jewels,","and such like trifles, nothing comparing to his; yet, had he","mistook him, and sent to me, I should ne'er have denied his","occasion so many talents."],"id":442,"m":"What a strange thing! Now, by the gods, I'm ashamed of it. To refuse that honourable man! There was very little honour shown in that. For my own part, I must admit I've received a few small kindnesses from him, like money, silver, jewels and trifles of that sort, nothing compared with what Lucullus got. Still, if he had passed over Lucullus and sent to me, I would never have refused him so many talents in his need."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter SERVILIUS.","id":443},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVILIUS","lines":["See, by good hap, yonder's my lord; I have sweat to see","his honour. [To LUCIUS] My honoured lord!"],"id":444,"m":"Look, by good luck there's my lord. I've sweated to find his honour. (To Lucius) My honoured lord!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Servilius! you are kindly met, sir. Fare thee well: commend","me to thy honourable virtuous lord, my very exquisite friend."],"id":445,"m":"Servilius! Good to see you, sir. Goodbye. Give my regards to your honourable, virtuous lord, my most excellent friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVILIUS","lines":["May it please your honour, my lord hath sent—"],"id":446,"m":"If it please your honour, my lord has sent..."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Ha! What has he sent? I am so much endeared to that lord;","he's ever sending: how shall I thank him, thinkest thou? And what","has he sent now?"],"id":447,"m":"Ha! What has he sent? I'm so much in that lord's debt; he's always sending me things. How do you think I can thank him? And what has he sent now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVILIUS","lines":["Has only sent his present occasion now, my lord;","requesting your lordship to supply his instant use with so many","talents."],"id":448,"m":"He has only sent you his present need, my lord, asking your lordship to supply him at once with a number of talents."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["I know his lordship is but merry with me;","He cannot want fifty-five hundred talents."],"id":449,"m":"I know his lordship is only joking with me. He can't be short of fifty-five hundred talents."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVILIUS","lines":["But in the mean time he wants less, my lord.","If his occasion were not virtuous,","I should not urge it half so faithfully."],"id":450,"m":"But just now he needs much less, my lord. If his need weren't an honest one, I wouldn't press it half so earnestly."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["Dost thou speak seriously, Servilius?"],"id":451,"m":"Are you serious, Servilius?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVILIUS","lines":["Upon my soul, 'tis true, sir."],"id":452,"m":"On my soul, it's true, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["What a wicked beast was I to disfurnish myself against such","a good time, when I might ha' shown myself honourable! how","unluckily it happened, that I should purchase the day before for","a little part, and undo a great deal of honour! Servilius, now,","before the gods, I am not able to do; the more beast, I say; I","was sending to use Lord Timon myself, these gentlemen can","witness; but I would not, for the wealth of Athens, I had done it","now. Commend me bountifully to his good lordship; and I hope his","honour will conceive the fairest of me, because I have no power","to be kin: and tell him this from me, I count it one of my","greatest afflictions say, that I cannot pleasure such an","honourable gentleman. Good Servilius, will you befriend me so far","as to use mine own words to him?"],"id":453,"m":"What a wicked beast I was to leave myself without money just when I could have shown myself honourable! How unlucky that only the day before I made a purchase that brought me a little profit and lost me a great deal of honour! Servilius, now, by the gods, I'm not able to do it. All the more beast me, I say! I was about to send to Lord Timon to borrow from him myself, as these gentlemen can witness. But I wouldn't have done it now for all the wealth of Athens. Give my warmest regards to his good lordship. I hope his honour will think the best of me, since I have no power to be kind. And tell him this from me: say I count it one of my greatest sorrows that I can't oblige such an honourable gentleman. Good Servilius, will you do me the favour of telling him in my own words?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVILIUS","lines":["Yes, sir, I shall."],"id":454,"m":"Yes, sir, I will."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","lines":["I'll look you out a good turn, Servilius."],"id":455,"m":"I'll find a good turn to do for you, Servilius."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit SERVILIUS.","id":456},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS","cont":1,"lines":["True, as you said, Timon is shrunk indeed;","And he that's once denied will hardly speed."],"id":457,"m":"It's true, as you said: Timon really has come down in the world. And a man who has been refused once will hardly succeed elsewhere."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":458},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST STRANGER","lines":["Do you observe this, Hostilius?"],"id":459,"m":"Do you see this, Hostilius?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND STRANGER","lines":["Ay, too well."],"id":460,"m":"Yes, all too well."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST STRANGER","lines":["Why, this is the world's soul; and just of the same piece","Is every flatterer's spirit. Who can call him","His friend that dips in the same dish? for, in","My knowing, Timon has been this lord's father,","And kept his credit with his purse,","Supported his estate; nay, Timon's money","Has paid his men their wages: He ne'er drinks","But Timon's silver treads upon his lip;","And yet, O! see the monstrousness of man,","When he looks out in an ungrateful shape,","He does deny him, in respect of his,","What charitable men afford to beggars."],"id":461,"m":"Why, this is the spirit of the world, and every flatterer's spirit is cut from the same cloth. Who can call a man who eats from the same dish his friend? As far as I know, Timon has been a father to this lord. He kept up his credit with his own purse and supported his estate. Yes, Timon's money has paid his servants' wages. He never drinks without Timon's silver touching his lips. And yet, oh, see how monstrous man is when he shows his ungrateful side! Compared with what he has, he refuses Timon what charitable men give to beggars."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD STRANGER","lines":["Religion groans at it."],"id":462,"m":"Religion groans at it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST STRANGER","lines":["For mine own part,","I never tasted Timon in my life,","Nor came any of his bounties over me","To mark me for his friend; yet I protest,","For his right noble mind, illustrious virtue,","And honourable carriage,","Had his necessity made use of me,","I would have put my wealth into donation,","And the best half should have return'd to him,","So much I love his heart. But, I perceive,","Men must learn now with pity to dispense;","For policy sits above conscience."],"id":463,"m":"For my own part, I've never enjoyed anything of Timon's in my life. None of his gifts ever came my way to make me his friend. Yet I swear, for his truly noble mind, his shining virtue and his honourable behaviour, if his need had turned to me, I would have treated my wealth as a gift and given the better half back to him, I love his heart so much. But I see that men must now learn to do without pity, because self-interest now ranks above conscience."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":464},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"III","loc":"Athens. A room in Sempronius' house.","id":465},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter SEMPRONIUS and a SERVANT of TIMON'S.","id":466},{"t":"sp","who":"SEMPRONIUS","lines":["Must he needs trouble me in't? Hum! 'bove all others?","He might have tried Lord Lucius, or Lucullus;","And now Ventidius is wealthy too,","Whom he redeem'd from prison: all these","Owe their estates unto him."],"id":467,"m":"Must he trouble me with this? Hmm! Me before everyone else? He might have tried Lord Lucius, or Lucullus. And now Ventidius is rich too, whom he bought out of prison. All of these owe their fortunes to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["My lord,","They have all been touch'd and found base metal, for","They have all denied him."],"id":468,"m":"My lord, they have all been tested like coins and found to be base metal, because they have all refused him."},{"t":"sp","who":"SEMPRONIUS","lines":["How! have they denied him?","Has Ventidius and Lucullus denied him?","And does he send to me? Three? Hum!","It shows but little love or judgment in him:","Must I be his last refuge? His friends, like physicians,","Thrice give him over; must I take the cure upon me?","Has much disgrac'd me in't; I'm angry at him,","That might have known my place. I see no sense for't,","But his occasions might have woo'd me first;","For, in my conscience, I was the first man","That e'er received gift from him:","And does he think so backwardly of me now,","That I'll requite it last? No:","So it may prove an argument of laughter","To the rest, and I 'mongst lords be thought a fool.","I had rather than the worth of thrice the sum,","Had sent to me first, but for my mind's sake;","I'd such a courage to do him good. But now return,","And with their faint reply this answer join;","Who bates mine honour shall not know my coin."],"id":469,"m":"What! Have they refused him? Have Ventidius and Lucullus refused him? And now he sends to me? Three of them? Hmm! It shows very little love or judgement in him. Must I be his last resort? His friends, like doctors, have given him up three times over. Must I take on the cure? He has insulted me badly by this. I'm angry with him; he should have known my rank. I can see no reason why his needs shouldn't have come to me first, because, honestly, I was the first man who ever received a gift from him. And does he think so little of me now that I'll be the last to repay it? No. That would make me a laughing stock among the others, and I'd be thought a fool among lords. I'd rather he had sent to me first, just for my own sake, than be given three times the sum, I was so eager to help him. But now go back, and add this answer to their feeble replies: whoever lowers my honour won't see my money."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":470},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Excellent! Your lordship's a goodly villain. The devil","knew not what he did when he made man politic; he crossed","himself by't: and I cannot think but, in the end the villainies","of man will set him clear. How fairly this lord strives to appear","foul! takes virtuous copies to be wicked, like those that under","hot ardent zeal would set whole realms on fire:","Of such a nature is his politic love.","This was my lord's best hope; now all are fled","Save only the gods. Now his friends are dead,","Doors, that were ne'er acquainted with their wards","Many a bounteous year, must be employ'd","Now to guard sure their master:","And this is all a liberal course allows:","Who cannot keep his wealth must keep his house."],"id":471,"m":"Excellent! Your lordship is a fine villain. The devil didn't know what he was doing when he made man crafty; he got in his own way by it. I can't help thinking that in the end man's villainy will make the devil look innocent. How cleverly this lord tries to look foul! He uses virtuous examples to be wicked, like those who, under a show of burning religious zeal, would set whole kingdoms on fire. His crafty love is of that kind. He was my lord's best hope. Now everyone has run away except the gods. Now that his friends are dead, doors that never knew their bolts for many generous years must be used to guard their master safely. This is all that a generous life leaves him: a man who can't keep his wealth must keep to his house to avoid arrest."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":472},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"IV","loc":"Athens. A hall in Timon's house.","id":473},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter two Servants of VARRO, and the Servant of LUCIUS, meeting TITUS, HORTENSIUS, and other Servants to TIMON's Creditors, waiting his coming out.","id":474},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST VARRO'S SERVANT","lines":["Well met; good morrow, Titus and Hortensius."],"id":475,"m":"Well met. Good morning, Titus and Hortensius."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["The like to you, kind Varro."],"id":476,"m":"The same to you, kind Varro."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIUS","lines":["Lucius! What! do we meet together!"],"id":477,"m":"Lucius! What, are we all meeting here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS' SERVANT","lines":["Ay, and I think one business does command us all; for mine is","money."],"id":478,"m":"Yes, and I think one errand brings us all, because mine is money."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["So is theirs and ours."],"id":479,"m":"So is theirs and ours."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter PHILOTUS.","id":480},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS' SERVANT","lines":["And Sir Philotus too!"],"id":481,"m":"And Sir Philotus too!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILOTUS","lines":["Good day at once."],"id":482,"m":"Good day to you all."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS' SERVANT","lines":["Welcome, good brother.","What do you think the hour?"],"id":483,"m":"Welcome, good brother. What time do you think it is?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILOTUS","lines":["Labouring for nine."],"id":484,"m":"Getting on for nine."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS' SERVANT","lines":["So much?"],"id":485,"m":"As late as that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILOTUS","lines":["Is not my lord seen yet?"],"id":486,"m":"Hasn't my lord been seen yet?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS' SERVANT","lines":["Not yet."],"id":487,"m":"Not yet."},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILOTUS","lines":["I wonder on't; he was wont to shine at seven."],"id":488,"m":"I'm surprised. He used to shine out at seven."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS' SERVANT","lines":["Ay, but the days are waxed shorter with him:","You must consider that a prodigal course","Is like the sun's, but not, like his, recoverable.","I fear,","'Tis deepest winter in Lord Timon's purse;","That is, one may reach deep enough, and yet","Find little."],"id":489,"m":"Yes, but his days have grown shorter. You must remember that a spendthrift's course is like the sun's, but, unlike the sun's, it can't come round again. I'm afraid it's deepest winter in Lord Timon's purse. That is, you can reach deep into it and still find little."},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILOTUS","lines":["I am of your fear for that."],"id":490,"m":"I share your fear on that."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["I'll show you how to observe a strange event.","Your lord sends now for money."],"id":491,"m":"I'll show you something strange. Your lord is now sending to Timon for money."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIUS","lines":["Most true, he does."],"id":492,"m":"Quite true, he is."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["And he wears jewels now of Timon's gift,","For which I wait for money."],"id":493,"m":"And he's wearing jewels that Timon gave him, which I'm waiting to be paid for."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIUS","lines":["It is against my heart."],"id":494,"m":"It goes against my heart."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS' SERVANT","lines":["Mark, how strange it shows,","Timon in this should pay more than he owes:","And e'en as if your lord should wear rich jewels,","And send for money for 'em."],"id":495,"m":"Look how strange it seems: in this way Timon will pay more than he owes. It's just as if your lord should wear rich jewels and send to Timon for money to pay for them."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIUS","lines":["I'm weary of this charge, the gods can witness:","I know my lord hath spent of Timon's wealth,","And now ingratitude makes it worse than stealth."],"id":496,"m":"I'm sick of this errand, as the gods are my witness. I know my lord has spent Timon's wealth, and now his ingratitude makes it worse than theft."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST VARRO'S SERVANT","lines":["Yes, mine's three thousand crowns; what's yours?"],"id":497,"m":"Yes, mine is three thousand crowns. What's yours?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS' SERVANT","lines":["Five thousand mine."],"id":498,"m":"Five thousand mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST VARRO'S SERVANT","lines":["'Tis much deep: and it should seem by the sum,","Your master's confidence was above mine;","Else, surely, his had equall'd."],"id":499,"m":"That's a lot. And it seems from the size of the sum that your master trusted Timon more than mine did. Otherwise, surely, his loan would have been as big."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter FLAMINIUS.","id":500},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["One of Lord Timon's men."],"id":501,"m":"One of Lord Timon's men."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS' SERVANT","lines":["Flaminius! Sir, a word. Pray, is my lord ready to","come forth?"],"id":502,"m":"Flaminius! Sir, a word. Please, is my lord ready to come out?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAMINIUS","lines":["No, indeed, he is not."],"id":503,"m":"No, indeed, he isn't."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["We attend his lordship; pray, signify so much."],"id":504,"m":"We're waiting for his lordship. Please tell him so."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAMINIUS","lines":["I need not tell him that; he knows you are too diligent."],"id":505,"m":"I don't need to tell him that. He knows you are all too eager."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit FLAMINIUS.","id":506},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter FLAVIUS, in a cloak, muffled.","id":507},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS' SERVANT","lines":["Ha! is not that his steward muffled so?","He goes away in a cloud: call him, call him."],"id":508,"m":"Ha! Isn't that his steward, wrapped up like that? He's slipping away in a cloud. Call him, call him."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Do you hear, sir?"],"id":509,"m":"Do you hear, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND VARRO'S SERVANT","lines":["By your leave, sir."],"id":510,"m":"Excuse me, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["What do you ask of me, my friend?"],"id":511,"m":"What do you want from me, my friend?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["We wait for certain money here, sir."],"id":512,"m":"We're waiting here for some money that's certain, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["Ay,","If money were as certain as your waiting,","'Twere sure enough.","Why then preferr'd you not your sums and bills,","When your false masters eat of my lord's meat?","Then they could smile and fawn upon his debts,","And take down the interest into their gluttonous maws.","You do yourselves but wrong to stir me up;","Let me pass quietly:","Believe't, my lord and I have made an end;","I have no more to reckon, he to spend."],"id":513,"m":"Yes. If money were as certain as your waiting, it would be sure enough. Why didn't you hand in your sums and bills when your false masters were eating my lord's food? Then they could smile and fawn on his debts and swallow the interest into their greedy stomachs. You only harm yourselves by provoking me. Let me pass in peace. Believe me, my lord and I have finished: I have no more to count, and he has no more to spend."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS' SERVANT","lines":["Ay, but this answer will not serve."],"id":514,"m":"Yes, but that answer won't serve."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["If 'twill not serve, 'tis not so base as you;","For you serve knaves."],"id":515,"m":"If it won't serve, it's not as low as you, because you serve rogues."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":516},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST VARRO'S SERVANT","lines":["How! what does his cashiered worship mutter?"],"id":517,"m":"What! What is his dismissed worship muttering?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND VARRO'S SERVANT","lines":["No matter what; he's poor, and that's revenge enough. Who can","speak broader than he that has no house to put his head in? such","may rail against great buildings."],"id":518,"m":"Never mind what. He's poor, and that's revenge enough. Who can speak more freely than a man who has no house to shelter in? Men like that can rail against great buildings."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter SERVILIUS.","id":519},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["O, here's Servilius; now we shall know some answer."],"id":520,"m":"Oh, here's Servilius. Now we'll get some answer."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVILIUS","lines":["If I might beseech you, gentlemen, to repair some other","hour, I should derive much from't; for, take't of my soul, my","lord leans wondrously to discontent. His comfortable temper has","forsook him; he's much out of health, and keeps his chamber."],"id":521,"m":"If I might beg you, gentlemen, to come back at some other time, I'd be most grateful. For, believe me on my soul, my lord is terribly inclined to gloom. His cheerful temper has deserted him. He's very unwell and keeps to his room."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS' SERVANT","lines":["Many do keep their chambers are not sick:","And, if it be so far beyond his health,","Methinks he should the sooner pay his debts,","And make a clear way to the gods."],"id":522,"m":"Plenty of people keep to their rooms who aren't sick. And if he's as ill as that, I think he should pay his debts all the sooner and clear his way to the gods."},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVILIUS","lines":["Good gods!"],"id":523,"m":"Good gods!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["We cannot take this for answer, sir."],"id":524,"m":"We can't accept this as an answer, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAMINIUS","lines":["[Within] Servilius, help! my lord! my lord!"],"id":525,"m":"(Offstage) Servilius, help! My lord! My lord!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter TIMON, in a rage; FLAMINIUS following.","id":526},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["What! are my doors oppos'd against my passage?","Have I been ever free, and must my house","Be my retentive enemy, my gaol?","The place which I have feasted, does it now,","Like all mankind, show me an iron heart?"],"id":527,"m":"What! Are my own doors barred against me? Have I always been free, and must my house now be my enemy that holds me in, my prison? Does the place where I have feasted others now show me an iron heart, like all mankind?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS' SERVANT","lines":["Put in now, Titus."],"id":528,"m":"Go on, now, Titus."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["My lord, here is my bill."],"id":529,"m":"My lord, here's my bill."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS' SERVANT","lines":["Here's mine."],"id":530,"m":"Here's mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIUS","lines":["And mine, my lord."],"id":531,"m":"And mine, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTH VARRO'S SERVANTS","lines":["And ours, my lord."],"id":532,"m":"And ours, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILOTUS","lines":["All our bills."],"id":533,"m":"All our bills."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Knock me down with 'em: cleave me to the girdle."],"id":534,"m":"Knock me down with them. Split me in two to the waist."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS' SERVANT","lines":["Alas, my lord—"],"id":535,"m":"Oh dear, my lord..."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Cut my heart in sums."],"id":536,"m":"Cut my heart into sums."},{"t":"sp","who":"TITUS","lines":["Mine, fifty talents."],"id":537,"m":"Mine is fifty talents."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Tell out my blood."],"id":538,"m":"Count out my blood."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCIUS' SERVANT","lines":["Five thousand crowns, my lord."],"id":539,"m":"Five thousand crowns, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Five thousand drops pays that. What yours? and yours?"],"id":540,"m":"Five thousand drops will pay that. What's yours? And yours?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST VARRO'S SERVANT","lines":["My lord—"],"id":541,"m":"My lord..."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND VARRO'S SERVANT","lines":["My lord—"],"id":542,"m":"My lord..."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Tear me, take me; and the gods fall upon you!"],"id":543,"m":"Tear me apart, take me, and may the gods fall on you!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":544},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIUS","lines":["Faith, I perceive our masters may throw their caps at their","money: these debts may well be called desperate ones, for a","madman owes 'em."],"id":545,"m":"Honestly, I think our masters can say goodbye to their money. These debts can rightly be called desperate ones, because a madman owes them."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":546},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter TIMON and FLAVIUS.","id":547},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["They have e'en put my breath from me, the slaves.","Creditors? devils!"],"id":548,"m":"The slaves have almost knocked the breath out of me. Creditors? Devils!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["My dear lord—"],"id":549,"m":"My dear lord..."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["What if it should be so?"],"id":550,"m":"What if I did this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAMINIUS","lines":["My lord—"],"id":551,"m":"My lord..."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["I'll have it so. My steward!"],"id":552,"m":"I'll do it. My steward!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["Here, my lord."],"id":553,"m":"Here, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["So fitly! Go, bid all my friends again:","Lucius, Lucullus, and Sempronius; all:","I'll once more feast the rascals."],"id":554,"m":"Just in time! Go and invite all my friends again: Lucius, Lucullus and Sempronius, all of them. I'll feast the rascals once more."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["O my lord!","You only speak from your distracted soul;","There is not so much left to furnish out","A moderate table."],"id":555,"m":"Oh, my lord! You only speak like that because your mind is disturbed. There isn't enough left to set out a modest table."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Be it not in thy care: go.","I charge thee, invite them all: let in the tide","Of knaves once more; my cook and I'll provide."],"id":556,"m":"Don't you worry about that. Go. I order you to invite them all. Let the tide of rogues in once more. My cook and I will provide."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":557},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"V","loc":"Athens. The Senate House. The Senate sitting.","id":558},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["My lord, you have my voice to it: the fault's","Bloody. 'tis necessary he should die;","Nothing emboldens sin so much as mercy."],"id":559,"m":"My lord, you have my vote for it. The crime was bloody, and he must die. Nothing encourages sin so much as mercy."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SENATOR","lines":["Most true; the law shall bruise him."],"id":560,"m":"Very true. The law shall crush him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter ALCIBIADES, attended.","id":561},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["Honour, health, and compassion to the senate!"],"id":562,"m":"Honour, health and compassion to the Senate!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Now, captain."],"id":563,"m":"Well, captain?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["I am a humble suitor to your virtues;","For pity is the virtue of the law,","And none but tyrants use it cruelly.","It pleases time and fortune to lie heavy","Upon a friend of mine, who, in hot blood","Hath stepp'd into the law, which is past depth","To those that without heed do plunge into't.","He is a man, setting his fate aside,","Of comely virtues;","Nor did he soil the fact with cowardice,—","An honour in him which buys out his fault,—","But, with a noble fury and fair spirit,","Seeing his reputation touch'd to death,","He did oppose his foe;","And with such sober and unnoted passion","He did behave his anger, ere 'twas spent,","As if he had but prov'd an argument."],"id":564,"m":"I come humbly to appeal to your virtues, because pity is the virtue of the law, and only tyrants use the law cruelly. Time and fortune have chosen to weigh heavily on a friend of mine who, in the heat of anger, has fallen foul of the law, which is too deep for anyone who plunges into it carelessly. Apart from this misfortune, he is a man of fine qualities. He did not stain the deed with cowardice, an honour that pays for his fault. With noble anger and a fair spirit, seeing his reputation wounded to the death, he stood up to his enemy. And he kept his anger so controlled and quiet, until it was spent, that it was as if he had only been arguing a point."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["You undergo too strict a paradox,","Striving to make an ugly deed look fair:","Your words have took such pains as if they labour'd","To bring manslaughter into form, and set","Quarrelling upon the head of valour; which indeed","Is valour misbegot, and came into the world","When sects and factions were newly born.","He's truly valiant that can wisely suffer","The worst that man can breathe, and make his wrongs","his outsides, to wear them like his raiment, carelessly,","And ne'er prefer his injuries to his heart,","To bring it into danger.","If wrongs be evils, and enforce us kill,","What folly 'tis to hazard life for ill!"],"id":565,"m":"You are defending too strained a contradiction, trying to make an ugly deed look fair. Your words have worked so hard that it seems they are trying to make manslaughter respectable and to treat quarrelling as a form of courage. That kind of courage is a bastard, born into the world when parties and factions first began. The truly brave man is one who can wisely bear the worst insults men can speak, and treat his wrongs as outward things, wearing them carelessly like his clothes, never taking his injuries to heart and so putting it in danger. If wrongs are evils that force us to kill, what folly it is to risk our lives for evil!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["My lord,—"],"id":566,"m":"My lord..."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["You cannot make gross sins look clear;","To revenge is no valour, but to bear."],"id":567,"m":"You can't make gross sins look innocent. Courage isn't taking revenge; it's enduring."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["My lords, then, under favour, pardon me,","If I speak like a captain.","Why do fond men expose themselves to battle,","And not endure all threats? sleep upon't,","And let the foes quietly cut their throats","Without repugnancy? If there be","Such valour in the bearing, what make we","Abroad? why, then, women are more valiant","That stay at home, if bearing carry it,","And the ass more captain than the lion, the felon","Loaden with irons wiser than the judge,","If wisdom be in suffering. O my lords!","As you are great, be pitifully good:","Who cannot condemn rashness in cold blood?","To kill, I grant, is sin's extremest gust;","But, in defence, by mercy, 'tis most just.","To be in anger is impiety;","But who is man that is not angry?","Weigh but the crime with this."],"id":568,"m":"Then, my lords, with your permission, forgive me if I speak like a soldier. Why do foolish men expose themselves in battle, instead of putting up with all threats, sleeping on them, and letting their enemies quietly cut their throats without resisting? If there is such courage in enduring, what are we doing fighting abroad? Then women who stay at home are braver, if enduring wins the prize, and the ass is a better captain than the lion, and the criminal loaded with chains is wiser than the judge, if wisdom lies in suffering. Oh my lords, since you are great, be good and merciful. Who can't condemn rashness when his own blood is cold? I grant that killing is the most extreme taste of sin, but when it is done in self-defence it is, in mercy, entirely just. Anger is ungodly, but what man is never angry? Just weigh the crime with that in mind."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SENATOR","lines":["You breathe in vain."],"id":569,"m":"You're wasting your breath."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["In vain! his service done","At Lacedaemon and Byzantium","Were a sufficient briber for his life."],"id":570,"m":"Wasting my breath! The service he did at Sparta and Byzantium should be enough to buy his life."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["What's that?"],"id":571,"m":"What's that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["I say, my lords, has done fair service,","And slain in fight many of your enemies.","How full of valour did he bear himself","In the last conflict, and made plenteous wounds!"],"id":572,"m":"I say, my lords, he has served you well and killed many of your enemies in battle. How bravely he behaved in the last fight, and how many wounds he gave!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SENATOR","lines":["He has made too much plenty with 'em;","He's a sworn rioter; he has a sin that often","Drowns him and takes his valour prisoner;","If there were no foes, that were enough","To overcome him; in that beastly fury","He has been known to commit outrages","And cherish factions; 'tis inferr'd to us,","His days are foul and his drink dangerous."],"id":573,"m":"He has made too many wounds. He's a hardened brawler. He has a sin that often drowns him and takes his courage prisoner. If there were no enemies, that alone would be enough to beat him. In that beastly drunken fury he has been known to commit outrages and stir up factions. We are told that his days are foul and his drinking dangerous."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["He dies."],"id":574,"m":"He dies."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["Hard fate! he might have died in war.","My lords, if not for any parts in him,—","Though his right arm might purchase his own time,","And be in debt to none,—yet, more to move you,","Take my deserts to his, and join 'em both;","And, for I know your reverend ages love","Security, I'll pawn my victories, all","My honour to you, upon his good returns.","If by this crime he owes the law his life,","Why, let the war receive't in valiant gore;","For law is strict, and war is nothing more."],"id":575,"m":"A cruel fate! He could have died in war. My lords, if not for any qualities of his own, though his right arm could buy him the rest of his life and owe nobody anything, then, to move you more, add my services to his and join them together. And because I know your reverend ages love security, I'll pledge all my victories and all my honour to you that he will repay you well. If he owes the law his life for this crime, let war take it in brave bloodshed, because war is just as strict as law."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["We are for law; he dies: urge it no more,","On height of our displeasure. Friend or brother,","He forfeits his own blood that spills another."],"id":576,"m":"We stand for the law. He dies. Don't press it any further, or face our greatest displeasure. Friend or brother, a man who spills another's blood loses his own."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["Must it be so? it must not be. My lords,","I do beseech you, know me."],"id":577,"m":"Must it be so? It must not. My lords, I beg you, remember who I am."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SENATOR","lines":["How!"],"id":578,"m":"What!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["Call me to your remembrances."],"id":579,"m":"Call me to mind."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SENATOR","lines":["What!"],"id":580,"m":"What!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["I cannot think but your age has forgot me;","It could not else be I should prove so base,","To sue, and be denied such common grace.","My wounds ache at you."],"id":581,"m":"I can only think that your old age has forgotten me. Otherwise it couldn't be that I am treated so lowly as to beg and be refused such an ordinary favour. My wounds ache at the sight of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Do you dare our anger?","'Tis in few words, but spacious in effect;","We banish thee for ever."],"id":582,"m":"Do you dare provoke our anger? It's said in a few words, but its effect is wide: we banish you forever."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["Banish me!","Banish your dotage; banish usury,","That makes the Senate ugly."],"id":583,"m":"Banish me! Banish your senility. Banish moneylending, which makes the Senate ugly."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["If, after two days' shine, Athens contain thee,","Attend our weightier judgment. And, not to swell our spirit,","He shall be executed presently."],"id":584,"m":"If Athens still holds you after two days, expect a heavier sentence. And, so as not to inflame our anger further, your friend will be executed at once."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt SENATORS.","id":585},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["Now the gods keep you old enough; that you may live","Only in bone, that none may look on you!","I'm worse than mad: I have kept back their foes,","While they have told their money and let out","Their coin upon large interest; I myself","Rich only in large hurts: all those for this?","Is this the balsam that the usuring senate","Pours into captains' wounds? Banishment!","It comes not ill; I hate not to be banish'd;","It is a cause worthy my spleen and fury,","That I may strike at Athens. I'll cheer up","My discontented troops, and lay for hearts.","'Tis honour with most lands to be at odds;","Soldiers should brook as little wrongs as gods."],"id":586,"m":"Now may the gods keep you alive until you are old enough to be nothing but bones, so that no one can bear to look at you! I'm worse than mad. I've kept their enemies away while they counted their money and lent it out at high interest, and I myself am rich only in great wounds. All that for this? Is this the healing ointment that the moneylending Senate pours into soldiers' wounds? Banishment! It doesn't come badly. I don't mind being banished. It's a cause worthy of my rage and fury, so that I can strike at Athens. I'll cheer up my discontented troops and win their hearts. It's an honour to be at war with most countries. Soldiers should put up with wrongs as little as gods do."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":587},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"VI","loc":"Athens. A banqueting room in Timon's house.","id":588},{"t":"sd","text":"Music. Tables set out: Servants attending. Enter divers LORDS, SENATORS, and Others, at several doors.","id":589},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["The good time of day to you, sir."],"id":590,"m":"Good day to you, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["I also wish it to you. I think this honourable lord","did but try us this other day."],"id":591,"m":"The same to you. I think this honourable lord was only testing us the other day."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Upon that were my thoughts tiring when we encountered:","I hope it is not so low with him as he made it seem in the trial","of his several friends."],"id":592,"m":"That's just what I was thinking about when we met. I hope he isn't as badly off as he made himself seem when he tested his various friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["It should not be, by the persuasion of his new feasting."],"id":593,"m":"He can't be, judging by this new feast."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["I should think so: he hath sent me an earnest inviting,","which many my near occasions did urge me to put off; but he hath","conjured me beyond them, and I must needs appear."],"id":594,"m":"I should think so. He sent me an urgent invitation, which many pressing affairs of mine made me want to refuse, but he begged me so hard that I had to come."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["In like manner was I in debt to my importunate business, but he","would not hear my excuse. I am sorry, when he sent to borrow of","me, that my provision was out."],"id":595,"m":"In the same way I had urgent business to attend to, but he wouldn't accept my excuse. I'm sorry that when he sent to borrow from me my money had run out."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["I am sick of that grief too, as I understand how all things go."],"id":596,"m":"I'm sick with the same regret, now that I understand how things are."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Every man here's so. What would he have borrowed you?"],"id":597,"m":"Everyone here is. How much did he want to borrow from you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["A thousand pieces."],"id":598,"m":"A thousand gold pieces."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["A thousand pieces!"],"id":599,"m":"A thousand gold pieces!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["What of you?"],"id":600,"m":"How much from you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["He sent to me, sir—here he comes."],"id":601,"m":"He sent to me, sir... here he comes."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter TIMON and Attendants.","id":602},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["With all my heart, gentlemen both; And how fare you?"],"id":603,"m":"Welcome with all my heart, both of you, gentlemen. And how are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Ever at the best, hearing well of your lordship."],"id":604,"m":"Always at our best when we hear your lordship is well."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["The swallow follows not summer more willing than we","your lordship."],"id":605,"m":"The swallow doesn't follow summer more willingly than we follow your lordship."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["[Aside] Nor more willingly leaves winter; such summer-birds","are men. Gentlemen, our dinner will not recompense this long","stay: feast your ears with the music awhile, if they will fare so","harshly o' the trumpet's sound; we shall to't presently."],"id":606,"m":"(Aside) Nor leave winter more willingly. Men are summer birds like that. (Aloud) Gentlemen, our dinner won't make up for this long wait. Feast your ears on the music for a while, if they can stand such a harsh diet as the trumpet's sound. We'll eat in a moment."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["I hope it remains not unkindly with your lordship that","I return'd you an empty messenger."],"id":607,"m":"I hope your lordship doesn't still hold it against me that I sent your messenger back empty-handed."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["O! sir, let it not trouble you."],"id":608,"m":"Oh, sir, don't let it trouble you."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["My noble lord,—"],"id":609,"m":"My noble lord..."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Ah! my good friend, what cheer?"],"id":610,"m":"Ah, my good friend, how are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["My most honourable lord, I am e'en sick of shame, that","when your lordship this other day sent to me I was so","unfortunate a beggar."],"id":611,"m":"My most honourable lord, I'm sick with shame that when your lordship sent to me the other day I was so unluckily without money."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Think not on't, sir."],"id":612,"m":"Don't think about it, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["If you had sent but two hours before,—"],"id":613,"m":"If you had only sent two hours earlier..."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Let it not cumber your better remembrance."],"id":614,"m":"Don't let it burden your better memories."},{"t":"sd","text":"The banquet brought in.","id":615},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","cont":1,"lines":["Come, bring in all together."],"id":616,"m":"Come, bring it all in together."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["All covered dishes!"],"id":617,"m":"All covered dishes!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Royal cheer, I warrant you."],"id":618,"m":"A royal feast, I'm sure."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD LORD","lines":["Doubt not that, if money and the season can yield it."],"id":619,"m":"No doubt of that, if money and the season can provide it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["How do you? What's the news?"],"id":620,"m":"How are you? What's the news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD LORD","lines":["Alcibiades is banished: hear you of it?"],"id":621,"m":"Alcibiades has been banished. Have you heard?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST AND SECOND LORDS","lines":["Alcibiades banished!"],"id":622,"m":"Alcibiades banished!"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD LORD","lines":["'Tis so, be sure of it."],"id":623,"m":"It's true, you can be sure of it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["How? how?"],"id":624,"m":"What? How?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["I pray you, upon what?"],"id":625,"m":"Please tell me, for what?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["My worthy friends, will you draw near?"],"id":626,"m":"My worthy friends, will you come to the table?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD LORD","lines":["I'll tell you more anon. Here's a noble feast toward."],"id":627,"m":"I'll tell you more later. There's a fine feast coming."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["This is the old man still."],"id":628,"m":"He's the same old Timon still."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD LORD","lines":["Will't hold? will't hold?"],"id":629,"m":"Will it last? Will it last?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["It does; but time will—and so—"],"id":630,"m":"It does for now, but time will... and so..."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD LORD","lines":["I do conceive."],"id":631,"m":"I understand."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Each man to his stool with that spur as he would to the lip","of his mistress; your diet shall be in all places alike. Make not","a city feast of it, to let the meat cool ere we can agree upon","the first place: sit, sit. The gods require our thanks.—","You great benefactors sprinkle our society with thankfulness.","For your own gifts make yourselves praised: but reserve still to","give, lest your deities be despised. Lend to each man enough,","that one need not lend to another; for, were your god-heads to","borrow of men, men would forsake the gods. Make the meat be","beloved more than the man that gives it. Let no assembly of","twenty be without a score of villains: if there sit twelve women","at the table, let a dozen of them be as they are. The rest of","your foes, O gods! the senators of Athens, together with the","common lag of people, what is amiss in them, you gods, make","suitable for destruction. For these my present friends, as they","are to me nothing, so in nothing bless them, and to nothing are","they welcome.","Uncover, dogs, and lap."],"id":632,"m":"Every man go to his seat as eagerly as he would go to kiss his mistress. The food will be the same everywhere. Don't make a formal city banquet of it, letting the food go cold while we argue about who takes the top place. Sit, sit. The gods require our thanks. (Saying grace) You great benefactors, sprinkle our company with thankfulness. Make yourselves praised for your own gifts, but always keep something back to give, in case your godhead is despised. Lend each man enough so that no one need lend to another, because if you gods had to borrow from men, men would abandon the gods. Make the food more loved than the man who gives it. Let no gathering of twenty be without a score of villains. If twelve women sit at the table, let a dozen of them be what they are. As for the rest of your enemies, oh gods, the senators of Athens together with the common rabble, whatever is wrong in them, you gods, make it fit for destruction. As for my present friends here, since they are nothing to me, bless them with nothing, and to nothing they are welcome. Uncover the dishes, dogs, and lap it up."},{"t":"sd","text":"The dishes uncovered are full of warm water.","id":633},{"t":"sp","who":"SOME LORDS","lines":["What does his lordship mean?"],"id":634,"m":"What does his lordship mean?"},{"t":"sp","who":"OTHER LORDS","lines":["I know not."],"id":635,"m":"I don't know."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["May you a better feast never behold,","You knot of mouth-friends! smoke and lukewarm water","Is your perfection. This is Timon's last;","Who, stuck and spangled with your flatteries,","Washes it off, and sprinkles in your faces"],"id":636,"m":"May you never see a better feast, you gang of friends in words only! Steam and lukewarm water are your perfect likeness. This is Timon's last feast. Smeared and spangled with your flattery, he washes it off and sprinkles in your faces"},{"t":"sd","text":"Throwing the water in their faces.","id":637},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","cont":1,"lines":["Your reeking villainy. Live loath'd, and long,","Most smiling, smooth, detested parasites,","Courteous destroyers, affable wolves, meek bears,","You fools of fortune, trencher-friends, time's flies,","Cap and knee slaves, vapours, and minute-jacks!","Of man and beast the infinite malady","Crust you quite o'er! What, dost thou go?","Soft! take thy physic first,—thou too,—and thou;—","Stay, I will lend thee money, borrow none."],"id":638,"m":"your stinking villainy. May you live hated, and live long, you smiling, smooth, loathed parasites, polite destroyers, friendly wolves, gentle bears, you fools of fortune, friends of the dinner plate, fair-weather flies, bowing and kneeling slaves, puffs of air, clockwork puppets! May every disease of man and beast cover you completely with scabs! What, are you going? Wait! Take your medicine first, and you too, and you. Stay, I'll lend you money, not borrow any."},{"t":"sd","text":"Throws the dishes at them.","id":639},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","cont":1,"lines":["What, all in motion? Henceforth be no feast,","Whereat a villain's not a welcome guest.","Burn, house! sink Athens! henceforth hated be","Of Timon man and all humanity!"],"id":640,"m":"What, all running away? From now on let there be no feast where a villain is not a welcome guest. Burn, house! Sink, Athens! From now on let man and all humanity be hated by Timon!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":641},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter the LORDS, SENATORS, and &c.","id":642},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["How now, my lords!"],"id":643,"m":"Well, my lords!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Know you the quality of Lord Timon's fury?"],"id":644,"m":"Do you understand what Lord Timon's fury is about?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD LORD","lines":["Push! did you see my cap?"],"id":645,"m":"Bah! Have you seen my hat?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FOURTH LORD","lines":["I have lost my gown."],"id":646,"m":"I've lost my gown."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["He's but a mad lord, and nought but humour sways him.","He gave me a jewel th' other day, and now he has beat it out of","my hat: did you see my jewel?"],"id":647,"m":"He's just a mad lord, ruled by nothing but his moods. He gave me a jewel the other day, and now he has knocked it out of my hat. Have you seen my jewel?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD LORD","lines":["Did you see my cap?"],"id":648,"m":"Have you seen my hat?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Here 'tis."],"id":649,"m":"Here it is."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOURTH LORD","lines":["Here lies my gown."],"id":650,"m":"Here's my gown."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST LORD","lines":["Let's make no stay."],"id":651,"m":"Let's not stay."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND LORD","lines":["Lord Timon's mad."],"id":652,"m":"Lord Timon is mad."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD LORD","lines":["I feel't upon my bones."],"id":653,"m":"I can feel it in my bones."},{"t":"sp","who":"FOURTH LORD","lines":["One day he gives us diamonds, next day stones."],"id":654,"m":"One day he gives us diamonds, the next day stones."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":655},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"Outside the walls of Athens.","id":656},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter TIMON.","id":657},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Let me look back upon thee. O thou wall,","That girdles in those wolves, dive in the earth,","And fence not Athens! Matrons, turn incontinent!","Obedience fail in children! slaves and fools,","Pluck the grave wrinkled senate from the bench,","And minister in their steads! To general filths","Convert, o' the instant, green virginity.","Do't in your parents' eyes! Bankrupts, hold fast;","Rather than render back, out with your knives,","And cut your trusters' throats. Bound servants, steal,—","Large-handed robbers your grave masters are,","And pill by law. Maid, to thy master's bed;","Thy mistress is o' the brothel! Son of sixteen,","Pluck the lin'd crutch from thy old limping sire,","With it beat out his brains! Piety, and fear,","Religion to the gods, peace, justice, truth,","Domestic awe, night-rest and neighbourhood,","Instruction, manners, mysteries and trades,","Degrees, observances, customs and laws,","Decline to your confounding contraries,","And let confusion live! Plagues incident to men,","Your potent and infectious fevers heap","On Athens, ripe for stroke! Thou cold sciatica,","Cripple our senators, that their limbs may halt","As lamely as their manners! Lust and liberty","Creep in the minds and marrows of our youth,","That 'gainst the stream of virtue they may strive,","And drown themselves in riot! Itches, blains,","Sow all the Athenian bosoms, and their crop","Be general leprosy! Breath infect breath,","That their society, as their friendship, may","Be merely poison! Nothing I'll bear from thee","But nakedness, thou detestable town!","Take thou that too, with multiplying bans!","Timon will to the woods; where he shall find","Th' unkindest beast more kinder than mankind.","The gods confound—hear me, you good gods all—","The Athenians both within and out that wall!","And grant, as Timon grows, his hate may grow","To the whole race of mankind, high and low!","Amen."],"id":658,"m":"Let me look back at you. Oh you wall that circles those wolves, sink into the earth and stop protecting Athens! Wives, become unchaste! Children, stop obeying! Slaves and fools, drag the grave, wrinkled senators from their bench and rule in their place! Let young virgins turn at once into common whores; do it in front of your parents! Bankrupts, hold on to what you have; rather than pay it back, take out your knives and cut your creditors' throats! Bound servants, steal: your grave masters are big-handed robbers who plunder by law. Maid, go to your master's bed; your mistress is in the brothel! Son of sixteen, snatch the padded crutch from your old limping father and beat his brains out with it! Piety and fear, reverence for the gods, peace, justice, truth, respect within the home, night's rest and neighbourliness, teaching, manners, crafts and trades, ranks, ceremonies, customs and laws, collapse into your destructive opposites, and let chaos live! Plagues that afflict men, pile your powerful, infectious fevers on Athens, which is ripe to be struck down! Cold sciatica, cripple our senators, so that their limbs limp as lamely as their morals! Let lust and licence creep into the minds and bones of our young men, so that they swim against the stream of virtue and drown in debauchery! Itches and blisters, be sown in every Athenian breast, and let the harvest be leprosy for all! Let breath infect breath, so that their company, like their friendship, is nothing but poison! I'll take nothing from you but my nakedness, you hateful town! Take that too, with curses multiplied! Timon will go to the woods, where he'll find the cruellest beast kinder than mankind. May the gods destroy (hear me, all you good gods) the Athenians both inside and outside that wall! And grant that, as Timon grows older, his hatred may grow towards the whole human race, high and low! Amen."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":659},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"Athens. A room in Timon's house.","id":660},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter FLAVIUS, with two or three SERVANTS.","id":661},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["Hear you, Master Steward! where's our master?","Are we undone? cast off? nothing remaining?"],"id":662,"m":"Listen, Master Steward! Where's our master? Are we ruined? Thrown out? Is nothing left?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["Alack! my fellows, what should I say to you?","Let me be recorded by the righteous gods,","I am as poor as you."],"id":663,"m":"Alas, my friends, what can I say to you? Let the righteous gods record it: I'm as poor as you are."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["Such a house broke!","So noble a master fall'n! All gone! and not","One friend to take his fortune by the arm","And go along with him!"],"id":664,"m":"Such a great house broken! So noble a master fallen! All gone! And not one friend to take his fortune by the arm and go along with him!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["As we do turn our backs","From our companion, thrown into his grave,","So his familiars to his buried fortunes","Slink all away, leave their false vows with him,","Like empty purses pick'd; and his poor self,","A dedicated beggar to the air,","With his disease of all-shunn'd poverty,","Walks, like contempt, alone. More of our fellows."],"id":665,"m":"Just as we turn our backs on a companion once he's thrown into his grave, so his close friends all slink away from his buried fortune, leaving their false vows with him like empty, picked purses. And he himself, a beggar given over to the open air, with his disease of poverty that everyone avoids, walks alone, like scorn itself. More of our fellows."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter other SERVANTS.","id":666},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["All broken implements of a ruin'd house."],"id":667,"m":"All broken tools of a ruined house."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["Yet do our hearts wear Timon's livery,","That see I by our faces; we are fellows still,","Serving alike in sorrow. Leak'd is our bark,","And we, poor mates, stand on the dying deck,","Hearing the surges threat: we must all part","Into this sea of air."],"id":668,"m":"Yet our hearts still wear Timon's uniform; I can see that in our faces. We are still fellows, serving together in sorrow. Our ship has sprung a leak, and we poor crewmen stand on the sinking deck, hearing the waves threaten. We must all part into this sea of air."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["Good fellows all,","The latest of my wealth I'll share amongst you.","Wherever we shall meet, for Timon's sake","Let's yet be fellows; let's shake our heads, and say,","As 'twere a knell unto our master's fortune,","'We have seen better days.' Let each take some;"],"id":669,"m":"Good fellows all, I'll share the last of my money among you. Wherever we meet, for Timon's sake, let's still be friends. Let's shake our heads and say, as if it were a funeral bell for our master's fortune, \"We have seen better days.\" Each of you take some."},{"t":"sd","text":"Giving them money.","id":670},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","cont":1,"lines":["Nay, put out all your hands. Not one word more:","Thus part we rich in sorrow, parting poor."],"id":671,"m":"No, hold out all your hands. Not another word. So we part rich in sorrow, though poor."},{"t":"sd","text":"They embrace, and part several ways.","id":672},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","cont":1,"lines":["O! the fierce wretchedness that glory brings us.","Who would not wish to be from wealth exempt,","Since riches point to misery and contempt?","Who would be so mock'd with glory? or so live,","But in a dream of friendship?","To have his pomp, and all what state compounds","But only painted, like his varnish'd friends?","Poor honest lord! brought low by his own heart,","Undone by goodness. Strange, unusual blood,","When man's worst sin is he does too much good!","Who then dares to be half so kind agen?","For bounty, that makes gods, does still mar men.","My dearest lord, bless'd, to be most accurs'd,","Rich, only to be wretched, thy great fortunes","Are made thy chief afflictions. Alas! kind lord,","He's flung in rage from this ingrateful seat","Of monstrous friends;","Nor has he with him to supply his life,","Or that which can command it.","I'll follow and enquire him out:","I'll ever serve his mind with my best will;","Whilst I have gold, I'll be his steward still."],"id":673,"m":"Oh, the terrible misery that greatness brings us! Who wouldn't wish to be free of wealth, since riches lead to misery and contempt? Who would want to be fooled by glory like this, or to live only in a dream of friendship, to have his splendour and everything that makes up high rank only painted on, like his varnished friends? Poor honest lord! Brought low by his own heart, ruined by goodness. What a strange, unnatural temperament, when a man's worst sin is doing too much good! Who will dare to be half so kind again? Generosity, which makes gods, still ruins men. My dearest lord, blessed only to be cursed the more, rich only to be wretched, your great fortune has become your chief affliction. Alas, kind lord! He has rushed off in a rage from this ungrateful home of monstrous friends, and he has nothing with him to keep himself alive, or money to buy it. I'll follow and find out where he is. I'll always serve his wishes with all my goodwill. As long as I have gold, I'll still be his steward."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":674},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"III","loc":"Woods and caves near the sea-shore. Before Timon's cave.","id":675},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter TIMON from the Cave.","id":676},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["O blessed breeding sun! draw from the earth","Rotten humidity; below thy sister's orb","Infect the air! Twinn'd brothers of one womb,","Whose procreation, residence and birth,","Scarce is dividant, touch them with several fortunes;","The greater scorns the lesser: not nature,","To whom all sores lay siege, can bear great fortune,","But by contempt of nature.","Raise me this beggar, and deny't that lord;","The senator shall bear contempt hereditary,","The beggar native honour.","It is the pasture lards the rother's sides,","The want that makes him lean. Who dares, who dares,","In purity of manhood stand upright,","And say, 'This man's a flatterer'? if one be,","So are they all; for every grize of fortune","Is smooth'd by that below: the learned pate","Ducks to the golden fool: all is oblique;","There's nothing level in our cursed natures","But direct villainy. Therefore, be abhorr'd","All feasts, societies, and throngs of men!","His semblable, yea, himself, Timon disdains:","Destruction fang mankind! Earth, yield me roots!"],"id":677,"m":"Oh blessed, life-giving sun, draw rotten damp up from the earth and infect the air below the moon! Take twin brothers from one womb, whose conception, life in the womb and birth can hardly be told apart, and give them different fortunes: the greater will scorn the lesser. Human nature, besieged by every ill, can't bear great fortune without despising its own kind. Raise this beggar up and take that lord's wealth away: the senator will be born to contempt, the beggar to honour. It's the pasture that fattens the ox's sides, and the lack of it that makes him thin. Who dares, who dares to stand up straight in pure manliness and say \"This man is a flatterer\"? If one is, they all are, because every step on fortune's stair is smoothed by the one below. The learned head bows to the golden fool. Everything is crooked. There's nothing straight in our cursed natures except outright villainy. So let all feasts, gatherings and crowds of men be hated! Timon despises his fellow man, yes, even himself. May destruction seize mankind in its teeth! Earth, give me roots!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Digging.","id":678},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","cont":1,"lines":["Who seeks for better of thee, sauce his palate","With thy most operant poison! What is here?","Gold! yellow, glittering, precious gold! No, gods,","I am no idle votarist. Roots, you clear heavens!","Thus much of this will make black white, foul fair,","Wrong right, base noble, old young, coward valiant.","Ha! you gods, why this? What this, you gods? Why, this","Will lug your priests and servants from your sides,","Pluck stout men's pillows from below their head:","This yellow slave","Will knit and break religions; bless the accurs'd,","Make the hoar leprosy ador'd; place thieves,","And give them title, knee, and approbation,","With senators on the bench; this is it","That makes the wappen'd widow wed again;","She, whom the spital-house and ulcerous sores","Would cast the gorge at, this embalms and spices","To the April day again. Come, damned earth,","Thou common whore of mankind, that putt'st odds","Among the rout of nations, I will make thee","Do thy right nature.—[March afar off]","Ha! a drum? thou'rt quick,","But yet I'll bury thee: thou'lt go, strong thief,","When gouty keepers of thee cannot stand:","Nay, stay thou out for earnest."],"id":679,"m":"Whoever wants something better from you, may you season his taste with your strongest poison! What's this? Gold! Yellow, glittering, precious gold! No, gods, I'm not a half-hearted worshipper: I asked for roots, you clear heavens! This much of it will make black white, foul fair, wrong right, low noble, old young, coward brave. Ha, you gods! Why this? What's this for, you gods? Why, this will drag your priests and servants away from your side and pull pillows from under the heads of strong men who are still alive. This yellow slave will tie religions together and break them apart, bless the cursed, make white leprosy adored, place thieves among senators on the bench and give them titles, bowing and approval. This is what makes the worn-out widow marry again. Her, whom a hospital full of ulcerous patients would vomit at the sight of, this perfumes and spices back to the freshness of an April day. Come, damned earth, you common whore of mankind who sets nations quarrelling, I will make you do what your nature is. (Marching heard in the distance) Ha! A drum? You're lively, but I'll bury you all the same. You'll walk about, strong thief, when your gouty keepers can't stand. No, stay out here as a down payment."},{"t":"sd","text":"Keeping some gold.","id":680},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter ALCIBIADES, with drum and fife, in warlike manner; PHRYNIA and TIMANDRA.","id":681},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["What art thou there? speak."],"id":682,"m":"What are you there? Speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["A beast, as thou art. The canker gnaw thy heart,","For showing me again the eyes of man!"],"id":683,"m":"A beast, as you are. May a cancer eat your heart for showing me the eyes of man again!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["What is thy name? Is man so hateful to thee,","That art thyself a man?"],"id":684,"m":"What's your name? Is man so hateful to you, when you're a man yourself?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["I am Misanthropos, and hate mankind.","For thy part, I do wish thou wert a dog,","That I might love thee something."],"id":685,"m":"I am Misanthropos, the man-hater, and I hate mankind. As for you, I wish you were a dog, so that I could love you a little."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["I know thee well,","But in thy fortunes am unlearn'd and strange."],"id":686,"m":"I know you well, but I know nothing about what has happened to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["I know thee too; and more than that I know thee","I not desire to know. Follow thy drum;","With man's blood paint the ground, gules, gules;","Religious canons, civil laws are cruel;","Then what should war be? This fell whore of thine","Hath in her more destruction than thy sword","For all her cherubin look."],"id":687,"m":"I know you too, and I don't want to know you any better than that. Follow your drum. Paint the ground red, red, with man's blood. The church's rules and the civil laws are cruel, so what should war be? This deadly whore of yours carries more destruction in her than your sword, for all her angelic looks."},{"t":"sp","who":"PHRYNIA","lines":["Thy lips rot off!"],"id":688,"m":"May your lips rot off!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["I will not kiss thee; then the rot returns","To thine own lips again."],"id":689,"m":"I won't kiss you, so the rot goes back to your own lips."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["How came the noble Timon to this change?"],"id":690,"m":"How did the noble Timon come to change like this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["As the moon does, by wanting light to give:","But then renew I could not like the moon;","There were no suns to borrow of."],"id":691,"m":"As the moon does, by having no more light to give. But I couldn't renew myself like the moon: there were no suns to borrow light from."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["Noble Timon,","What friendship may I do thee?"],"id":692,"m":"Noble Timon, what act of friendship can I do for you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["None, but to maintain my opinion."],"id":693,"m":"None, except to support my opinion."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["What is it, Timon?"],"id":694,"m":"What is it, Timon?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Promise me friendship, but perform none: if thou wilt not","promise, the gods plague thee, for thou art man! If thou dost","perform, confound thee, for thou art a man!"],"id":695,"m":"Promise me friendship, but don't carry it out. If you won't promise, may the gods plague you, because you're a man! If you do carry it out, may they destroy you, because you're a man!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["I have heard in some sort of thy miseries."],"id":696,"m":"I have heard something of your misfortunes."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Thou saw'st them when I had prosperity."],"id":697,"m":"You saw them when I was prosperous."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["I see them now; then was a blessed time."],"id":698,"m":"I see them now. Then was a blessed time."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["As thine is now, held with a brace of harlots."],"id":699,"m":"As yours is now, held fast by a pair of harlots."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMANDRA","lines":["Is this the Athenian minion whom the world","Voic'd so regardfully?"],"id":700,"m":"Is this the Athenian favourite the world spoke of so respectfully?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Art thou Timandra?"],"id":701,"m":"Are you Timandra?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMANDRA","lines":["Yes."],"id":702,"m":"Yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Be a whore still; they love thee not that use thee;","Give them diseases, leaving with thee their lust.","Make use of thy salt hours; season the slaves","For tubs and baths; bring down rose-cheeked youth","To the tub-fast and the diet."],"id":703,"m":"Stay a whore. The men who use you don't love you. Give them diseases in return for the lust they leave with you. Make the most of your lecherous hours. Prepare the slaves for the sweating tubs and baths that cure the pox. Bring rosy-cheeked young men down to the tub, the fasting and the diet."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMANDRA","lines":["Hang thee, monster!"],"id":704,"m":"Go hang, you monster!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["Pardon him, sweet Timandra, for his wits","Are drown'd and lost in his calamities.","I have but little gold of late, brave Timon,","The want whereof doth daily make revolt","In my penurious band: I have heard, and griev'd","How cursed Athens, mindless of thy worth,","Forgetting thy great deeds, when neighbour states,","But for thy sword and fortune, trod upon them,—"],"id":705,"m":"Forgive him, sweet Timandra, because his mind is drowned and lost in his misfortunes. I've had little gold lately, brave Timon, and the lack of it causes mutiny every day in my poor army. I have heard, and grieved, how cursed Athens, forgetting your worth and your great deeds, when neighbouring states would have trampled on it but for your sword and your wealth..."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["I prithee, beat thy drum, and get thee gone."],"id":706,"m":"Please, beat your drum and go away."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["I am thy friend, and pity thee, dear Timon."],"id":707,"m":"I'm your friend, and I pity you, dear Timon."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["How dost thou pity him whom thou dost trouble?","I had rather be alone."],"id":708,"m":"How can you pity a man you are pestering? I'd rather be alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["Why, fare thee well:","Here is some gold for thee."],"id":709,"m":"Well, goodbye. Here's some gold for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Keep it, I cannot eat it."],"id":710,"m":"Keep it. I can't eat it."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["When I have laid proud Athens on a heap,—"],"id":711,"m":"When I have reduced proud Athens to a heap of rubble..."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Warr'st thou 'gainst Athens?"],"id":712,"m":"Are you making war on Athens?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["Ay, Timon, and have cause."],"id":713,"m":"Yes, Timon, and I have good reason."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["The gods confound them all in thy conquest;","And thee after, when thou hast conquer'd!"],"id":714,"m":"May the gods destroy them all through your conquest, and destroy you afterwards, when you have conquered!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["Why me, Timon?"],"id":715,"m":"Why me, Timon?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["That, by killing of villains,","Thou wast born to conquer my country.","Put up thy gold: go on,—here's gold,—go on;","Be as a planetary plague, when Jove","Will o'er some high-vic'd city hang his poison","In the sick air: let not thy sword skip one.","Pity not honour'd age for his white beard;","He is an usurer. Strike me the counterfeit matron;","It is her habit only that is honest,","Herself's a bawd. Let not the virgin's cheek","Make soft thy trenchant sword; for those milk paps","That through the window-bars bore at men's eyes,","Are not within the leaf of pity writ,","But set them down horrible traitors. Spare not the babe,","Whose dimpled smiles from fools exhaust their mercy;","Think it a bastard, whom the oracle","Hath doubtfully pronounc'd thy throat shall cut,","And mince it sans remorse. Swear against objects;","Put armour on thine ears and on thine eyes,","Whose proof nor yells of mothers, maids, nor babes,","Nor sight of priests in holy vestments bleeding,","Shall pierce a jot. There's gold to pay thy soldiers:","Make large confusion; and, thy fury spent,","Confounded be thyself! Speak not, be gone."],"id":716,"m":"Because you were born to conquer my country by killing villains. Put away your gold. Go on; here's gold; go on. Be like a plague sent by the planets, when Jove hangs his poison in the sick air over some city deep in vice. Don't let your sword skip a single person. Don't pity old age for its white beard; he's a moneylender. Strike the respectable-looking matron; only her clothes are honest, she herself is a bawd. Don't let the virgin's cheek soften your sharp sword, because those milky breasts that peep at men through the window bars aren't written on pity's page. Count them as dreadful traitors. Don't spare the baby, whose dimpled smiles drain fools of their mercy. Think of it as a bastard whom the oracle has darkly prophesied will cut your throat, and chop it up without remorse. Swear yourself against all pitiful sights. Put armour on your ears and eyes so strong that neither the screams of mothers, girls and babies, nor the sight of priests bleeding in their holy robes, can pierce it at all. Here's gold to pay your soldiers. Cause great destruction, and when your fury is spent, may you be destroyed yourself! Don't speak. Go."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["Hast thou gold yet? I'll take the gold thou giv'st me,","Not all thy counsel."],"id":717,"m":"Have you still got gold? I'll take the gold you give me, but not all your advice."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Dost thou, or dost thou not, heaven's curse upon thee!"],"id":718,"m":"Whether you do or not, heaven's curse on you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PHRYNIA AND TIMANDRA","lines":["Give us some gold, good Timon:","Hast thou more?"],"id":719,"m":"Give us some gold, good Timon. Have you got more?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Enough to make a whore forswear her trade,","And to make whores a bawd. Hold up, you sluts,","Your aprons mountant: you are not oathable,","Although, I know, you'll swear, terribly swear","Into strong shudders and to heavenly agues,","The immortal gods that hear you, spare your oaths,","I'll trust to your conditions: be whores still;","And he whose pious breath seeks to convert you,","Be strong in whore, allure him, burn him up;","Let your close fire predominate his smoke,","And be no turncoats: yet may your pains, six months,","Be quite contrary: and thatch your poor thin roofs","With burdens of the dead; some that were hang'd,","No matter; wear them, betray with them: whore still;","Paint till a horse may mire upon your face:","A pox of wrinkles!"],"id":720,"m":"Enough to make a whore give up her trade, and to make whores into bawds. Hold up your aprons, you sluts. You can't be trusted to swear an oath, though I know you'll swear, swear terribly enough to make the immortal gods who hear you shudder and shake with fever. Spare your oaths; I'll trust your natures. Stay whores. And if some preacher's pious breath tries to convert you, be strong in whoring, seduce him and burn him up. Let your secret fire overpower his smoky sermon, and don't change sides. Yet for six months of the year may your efforts go the other way, suffering the pox yourselves. Thatch your thin, balding heads with wigs taken from the dead, some of them hanged men, no matter; wear them and deceive with them. Stay whores. Paint your faces so thick that a horse could get stuck in the mud on them. A pox on wrinkles!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PHRYNIA AND TIMANDRA","lines":["Well, more gold. What then?","Believe't that we'll do anything for gold."],"id":721,"m":"Well, more gold. What then? Believe us, we'll do anything for gold."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Consumptions sow","In hollow bones of man; strike their sharp shins,","And mar men's spurring. Crack the lawyer's voice,","That he may never more false title plead,","Nor sound his quillets shrilly; hoar the flamen,","That scolds against the quality of flesh,","And not believes himself: down with the nose,","Down with it flat; take the bridge quite away","Of him that, his particular to foresee,","Smells from the general weal: make curl'd-pate ruffians bald,","And let the unscarr'd braggarts of the war","Derive some pain from you: plague all,","That your activity may defeat and quell","The source of all erection. There's more gold;","Do you damn others, and let this damn you,","And ditches grave you all!"],"id":722,"m":"Sow wasting diseases in men's hollow bones. Strike their sharp shins and stop them riding. Crack the lawyer's voice so that he can never again plead a false claim or squeal out his quibbles. Make the priest hoarse, who preaches against the flesh and doesn't believe himself. Bring down the nose, flatten it, take the bridge right off the nose of the man who sniffs out his own private gain and ignores the common good. Make curly-haired thugs bald, and let the boastful soldiers who come home unscarred from war get some pain from you. Infect everyone, so that your work may destroy and kill the source of all erection. Here's more gold. You damn others, and let this damn you, and may ditches be your graves!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PHRYNIA AND TIMANDRA","lines":["More counsel with more money, bounteous Timon."],"id":723,"m":"More advice, with more money, generous Timon."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["More whore, more mischief first; I have given you earnest."],"id":724,"m":"More whoring and more mischief first. I've given you a down payment."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["Strike up the drum towards Athens! Farewell, Timon:","If I thrive well, I'll visit thee again."],"id":725,"m":"Beat the drum and march towards Athens! Goodbye, Timon. If I do well, I'll visit you again."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["If I hope well, I'll never see thee more."],"id":726,"m":"If my hopes come true, I'll never see you again."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["I never did thee harm."],"id":727,"m":"I never did you any harm."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Yes, thou spok'st well of me."],"id":728,"m":"Yes, you did. You spoke well of me."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["Call'st thou that harm?"],"id":729,"m":"Do you call that harm?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Men daily find it. Get thee away, and take","Thy beagles with thee."],"id":730,"m":"Men find it so every day. Get out, and take your hounds with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["We but offend him. Strike!"],"id":731,"m":"We're only offending him. Strike up the drum!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Drum beats. Exeunt all but TIMON.","id":732},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["That nature, being sick of man's unkindness,","Should yet be hungry! Common mother, thou,"],"id":733,"m":"To think that nature, sick of man's unkindness, should still be hungry! You, common mother of us all,"},{"t":"sd","text":"Digging.","id":734},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","cont":1,"lines":["Whose womb unmeasurable, and infinite breast,","Teems, and feeds all; whose self-same mettle,","Whereof thy proud child, arrogant man, is puff'd,","Engenders the black toad and adder blue,","The gilded newt and eyeless venom'd worm,","With all the abhorred births below crisp heaven","Whereon Hyperion's quickening fire doth shine;","Yield him, who all thy human sons doth hate,","From forth thy plenteous bosom, one poor root!","Ensear thy fertile and conceptious womb,","Let it no more bring out ingrateful man!","Go great with tigers, dragons, wolves, and bears;","Teem with new monsters, whom thy upward face","Hath to the marbled mansion all above","Never presented! O! a root; dear thanks:","Dry up thy marrows, vines and plough-torn leas;","Whereof ingrateful man, with liquorish draughts","And morsels unctuous, greases his pure mind,","That from it all consideration slips!"],"id":735,"m":"whose immeasurable womb and endless breast give birth to everything and feed everything, whose same material, from which your proud child, arrogant man, is puffed up, also breeds the black toad and the blue adder, the golden newt and the blind poisonous snake, with all the hateful creatures under the bright sky that the sun's life-giving fire shines on, give the man who hates all your human sons one poor root from your plentiful breast! Dry up your fertile, conceiving womb. Let it never again produce ungrateful man! Be pregnant with tigers, dragons, wolves and bears. Breed new monsters that your upturned face has never shown to the marble palace of heaven above! Oh, a root! Thank you dearly. Dry up your rich juices, your vines and ploughed fields, from which ungrateful man, with his sweet drinks and greasy food, smears over his clear mind so that all good sense slips out of it!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter APEMANTUS.","id":736},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","cont":1,"lines":["More man! Plague! plague!"],"id":737,"m":"More men! Plague! Plague!"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["I was directed hither: men report","Thou dost affect my manners, and dost use them."],"id":738,"m":"I was told to come here. People say you are copying my manners and imitating me."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["'Tis, then, because thou dost not keep a dog","Whom I would imitate: consumption catch thee!"],"id":739,"m":"That's because you don't keep a dog that I could imitate instead. May you catch a wasting disease!"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["This is in thee a nature but infected;","A poor unmanly melancholy sprung","From change of fortune. Why this spade, this place?","This slave-like habit? and these looks of care?","Thy flatterers yet wear silk, drink wine, lie soft,","Hug their diseas'd perfumes, and have forgot","That ever Timon was. Shame not these woods","By putting on the cunning of a carper.","Be thou a flatterer now, and seek to thrive","By that which has undone thee: hinge thy knee","And let his very breath, whom thou'lt observe","Blow off thy cap; praise his most vicious strain,","And call it excellent. Thou wast told thus;","Thou gav'st thine ears, like tapsters that bade welcome,","To knaves and all approachers: 'tis most just","That thou turn rascal; hadst thou wealth again,","Rascals should have't. Do not assume my likeness."],"id":740,"m":"In you this is only an infected nature, a feeble, unmanly gloom that comes from your change of fortune. Why this spade, this place, these slave's clothes, these worried looks? Your flatterers still wear silk, drink wine, sleep soft, hug their diseased perfumed women, and have forgotten that Timon ever existed. Don't shame these woods by putting on the airs of a fault-finder. Be a flatterer yourself now, and try to prosper by the very thing that has ruined you. Bend your knee as if it had a hinge, and let the mere breath of the man you court blow your cap off. Praise his worst vice and call it excellent. That's how they spoke to you. You lent your ears, like innkeepers welcoming everyone, to rogues and anyone who came along. It's only right that you should turn rascal. If you had wealth again, rascals would get it. Don't try to look like me."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Were I like thee I'd throw away myself."],"id":741,"m":"If I were like you, I'd throw myself away."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Thou hast cast away thyself, being like thyself;","A madman so long, now a fool. What! think'st","That the bleak air, thy boisterous chamberlain,","Will put thy shirt on warm? will these moss'd trees,","That have outliv'd the eagle, page thy heels","And skip when thou point'st out? will the cold brook,","Candied with ice, caudle thy morning taste","To cure thy o'ernight's surfeit? Call the creatures","Whose naked natures live in all the spite","Of wreakful heaven, whose bare unhoused trunks,","To the conflicting elements expos'd,","Answer mere nature; bid them flatter thee;","O! thou shalt find—"],"id":742,"m":"You threw yourself away by being like yourself: a madman for so long, now a fool. What, do you think the bitter wind, your rough servant, will warm your shirt for you? Will these mossy trees, older than the eagle, follow at your heels like pages and jump when you point? Will the cold stream, iced over like sugar, make you a warm drink in the morning to cure last night's excess? Call the creatures whose naked bodies live through all the spite of avenging heaven, whose bare, homeless bodies are exposed to the warring elements and live by nature alone. Tell them to flatter you. Oh, you'll find..."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["A fool of thee. Depart."],"id":743,"m":"That you're a fool. Go."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["I love thee better now than e'er I did."],"id":744,"m":"I love you more now than I ever did."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["I hate thee worse."],"id":745,"m":"I hate you more."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Why?"],"id":746,"m":"Why?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Thou flatter'st misery."],"id":747,"m":"You flatter misery."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["I flatter not, but say thou art a caitiff."],"id":748,"m":"I don't flatter. I say you are a wretch."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Why dost thou seek me out?"],"id":749,"m":"Why do you come looking for me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["To vex thee."],"id":750,"m":"To annoy you."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Always a villain's office or a fool's.","Dost please thyself in't?"],"id":751,"m":"That's always the job of a villain or a fool. Do you enjoy it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Ay."],"id":752,"m":"Yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["What! a knave too?"],"id":753,"m":"What, a rogue as well?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["If thou didst put this sour-cold habit on","To castigate thy pride, 'twere well; but thou","Dost it enforcedly; thou'dst courtier be again","Wert thou not beggar. Willing misery","Outlives incertain pomp, is crown'd before;","The one is filling still, never complete;","The other, at high wish: best state, contentless,","Hath a distracted and most wretched being,","Worse than the worst, content.","Thou shouldst desire to die, being miserable."],"id":754,"m":"If you had put on this harsh, cold way of life to punish your pride, it would be good. But you do it because you're forced to. You'd be a courtier again if you weren't a beggar. Poverty that is chosen outlives uncertain splendour and wins its crown sooner. One is always filling up and never full; the other has all it wishes. The best position, without contentment, is a troubled and wretched existence, worse than the worst position with contentment. Since you're miserable, you ought to want to die."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Not by his breath that is more miserable.","Thou art a slave, whom Fortune's tender arm","With favour never clasp'd, but bred a dog.","Hadst thou, like us from our first swath, proceeded","The sweet degrees that this brief world affords","To such as may the passive drugs of it","Freely command, thou wouldst have plung'd thyself","In general riot; melted down thy youth","In different beds of lust; and never learn'd","The icy precepts of respect, but follow'd","The sugar'd game before thee. But myself,","Who had the world as my confectionary,","The mouths, the tongues, the eyes, and hearts of men","At duty, more than I could frame employment,","That numberless upon me stuck as leaves","Do on the oak, have with one winter's brush","Fell from their boughs, and left me open, bare","For every storm that blows; I, to bear this,","That never knew but better, is some burden:","Thy nature did commence in sufferance, time","Hath made thee hard in't. Why shouldst thou hate men?","They never flatter'd thee: what hast thou given?","If thou wilt curse, thy father, that poor rag,","Must be thy subject; who in spite put stuff","To some she-beggar and compounded thee","Poor rogue hereditary. Hence! be gone!","If thou hadst not been born the worst of men,","Thou hadst been a knave and flatterer."],"id":755,"m":"Not on the advice of someone more miserable than me. You're a slave whom Fortune never held lovingly in her arms, but bred as a dog. If you had gone, as I did from my baby clothes, through all the sweet stages this short life offers to those who can freely command its willing pleasures, you'd have thrown yourself into general excess, melted your youth away in different beds of lust, and never learned the icy rules of caution, but chased the sugary prey in front of you. But I, who had the world as my sweet shop, with the mouths, tongues, eyes and hearts of men at my service, more than I could find work for, who had them sticking to me in countless numbers as leaves do to the oak, have seen them fall from the branches at one brush of winter, leaving me open and bare to every storm that blows. For me, who never knew anything but better, it's a heavy burden to bear. Your life began in suffering, and time has hardened you to it. Why should you hate men? They never flattered you. What have you ever given? If you want to curse, it must be your father, that poor rag, who out of spite lay with some beggar woman and made you a born poor rogue. Get out! Go! If you hadn't been born the lowest of men, you'd have been a rogue and a flatterer."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Art thou proud yet?"],"id":756,"m":"Are you still proud?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Ay, that I am not thee."],"id":757,"m":"Yes, proud that I'm not you."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["I, that I was","No prodigal."],"id":758,"m":"And I'm proud that I was never a spendthrift."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["I, that I am one now;","Were all the wealth I have shut up in thee,","I'd give thee leave to hang it. Get thee gone.","That the whole life of Athens were in this!","Thus would I eat it."],"id":759,"m":"And I'm proud that I am one now. If all the wealth I have were locked up in you, I'd give you permission to hang yourself. Go away. I wish the whole life of Athens were in this root! I'd eat it like this."},{"t":"sd","text":"Eating a root.","id":760},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Here; I will mend thy feast."],"id":761,"m":"Here, I'll improve your feast."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["First mend my company, take away thyself."],"id":762,"m":"First improve my company: take yourself away."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["So I shall mend mine own, by the lack of thine."],"id":763,"m":"Then I'll improve my own company by losing yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["'Tis not well mended so, it is but botch'd.","If not, I would it were."],"id":764,"m":"That isn't improving it, it's just patching it badly. If it isn't, I wish it were."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["What wouldst thou have to Athens?"],"id":765,"m":"What message do you want sent to Athens?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Thee thither in a whirlwind. If thou wilt,","Tell them there I have gold; look, so I have."],"id":766,"m":"You, sent there in a whirlwind. If you like, tell them I have gold. Look, I do."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Here is no use for gold."],"id":767,"m":"There's no use for gold here."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["The best and truest;","For here it sleeps and does no hired harm."],"id":768,"m":"It's the best and truest use, because here it sleeps and does no harm for hire."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Where liest o' nights, Timon?"],"id":769,"m":"Where do you sleep at night, Timon?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Under that's above me.","Where feed'st thou o' days, Apemantus?"],"id":770,"m":"Under what's above me. Where do you eat by day, Apemantus?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Where my stomach finds meat; or rather, where I eat it."],"id":771,"m":"Wherever my stomach finds food, or rather, wherever I eat it."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Would poison were obedient and knew my mind!"],"id":772,"m":"I wish poison were obedient and knew what I wanted!"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Where wouldst thou send it?"],"id":773,"m":"Where would you send it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["To sauce thy dishes."],"id":774,"m":"To flavour your food."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["The middle of humanity thou never knewest, but the","extremity of both ends. When thou wast in thy gilt and thy","perfume, they mock'd thee for too much curiosity; in thy rags","thou know'st none, but art despised for the contrary. There's a","medlar for thee; eat it."],"id":775,"m":"You never knew the middle way of life, only the extremes at both ends. When you were all gilt and perfume, people mocked you for being too fussy. In your rags you have no fussiness at all, and you're despised for the opposite. Here's a medlar for you; eat it."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["On what I hate I feed not."],"id":776,"m":"I don't eat what I hate."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Dost hate a medlar?"],"id":777,"m":"Do you hate a medlar?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Ay, though it look like thee."],"id":778,"m":"Yes, even though it looks like you."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["An thou hadst hated medlars sooner, thou shouldst have loved","thyself better now. What man didst thou ever know unthrift","that was beloved after his means?"],"id":779,"m":"If you had hated meddlers sooner, you'd love yourself more now. What spendthrift did you ever know who was loved once his money was gone?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Who, without those means thou talkest of, didst thou ever","know beloved?"],"id":780,"m":"And who did you ever know who was loved without the money you're talking about?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Myself."],"id":781,"m":"Myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["I understand thee; thou hadst some means to keep a dog."],"id":782,"m":"I understand you: you had enough money to keep a dog that loved you."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["What things in the world canst thou nearest compare to","thy flatterers?"],"id":783,"m":"What things in the world can you compare most closely to your flatterers?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Women nearest; but men, men are the things themselves. What","wouldst thou do with the world, Apemantus, if it lay in thy","power?"],"id":784,"m":"Women, most closely. But men, men are the very things themselves. What would you do with the world, Apemantus, if you had power over it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Give it the beasts, to be rid of the men."],"id":785,"m":"Give it to the beasts, to be rid of the men."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Wouldst thou have thyself fall in the confusion of men, and","remain a beast with the beasts?"],"id":786,"m":"Would you let yourself fall with the ruin of men, and stay on as a beast among the beasts?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Ay, Timon."],"id":787,"m":"Yes, Timon."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["A beastly ambition, which the gods grant thee to attain to.","If thou wert the lion, the fox would beguile thee; if thou wert","the lamb, the fox would eat thee; if thou wert the fox, the lion","would suspect thee, when peradventure, thou wert accused by the","ass; if thou wert the ass, thy dulness would torment thee, and","still thou livedst but as a breakfast to the wolf; if thou wert","the wolf, thy greediness would afflict thee, and oft thou","shouldst hazard thy life for thy dinner; wert thou the unicorn,","pride and wrath would confound thee and make thine own self the","conquest of thy fury; wert thou a bear, thou wouldst be killed by","the horse; wert thou a horse, thou wouldst be seized by the","leopard; wert thou a leopard, thou wert german to the lion, and","the spots of thy kindred were jurors on thy life; all thy safety","were remotion, and thy defence absence. What beast couldst thou","be that were not subject to a beast? and what beast art thou","already, that seest not thy loss in transformation!"],"id":788,"m":"A beastly ambition, and may the gods let you achieve it. If you were the lion, the fox would trick you. If you were the lamb, the fox would eat you. If you were the fox, the lion would suspect you whenever, perhaps, the ass accused you. If you were the ass, your stupidity would torment you, and you'd still live only as the wolf's breakfast. If you were the wolf, your greed would plague you, and you'd often risk your life for your dinner. If you were the unicorn, pride and rage would ruin you and make you the victim of your own fury. If you were a bear, the horse would kill you. If you were a horse, the leopard would seize you. If you were a leopard, you'd be a cousin of the lion, and your relatives' spots would be the jury that condemned you. Your only safety would be distance, and your only defence absence. What beast could you be that wasn't prey to another beast? And what a beast you are already, not to see how much you'd lose by the change!"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["If thou couldst please me with speaking to me, thou","mightst have hit upon it here; the commonwealth of Athens is","become a forest of beasts."],"id":789,"m":"If you could ever please me by talking to me, you might have done it now. The state of Athens has become a forest of beasts."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["How has the ass broke the wall, that thou art out of the","city?"],"id":790,"m":"How did the ass break down the city wall, so that you're outside the city?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Yonder comes a poet and a painter: the plague of company","light upon thee! I will fear to catch it, and give way. When I","know not what else to do, I'll see thee again."],"id":791,"m":"Over there comes a poet and a painter. May the plague of company land on you! I'm afraid of catching it, so I'll get out of the way. When I don't know what else to do, I'll come and see you again."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["When there is nothing living but thee, thou shalt be","welcome. I had rather be a beggar's dog than Apemantus."],"id":792,"m":"When there's nothing alive but you, you'll be welcome. I'd rather be a beggar's dog than Apemantus."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Thou art the cap of all the fools alive."],"id":793,"m":"You're the king of all the fools alive."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Would thou wert clean enough to spit upon!"],"id":794,"m":"I wish you were clean enough to spit on!"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["A plague on thee! thou art too bad to curse!"],"id":795,"m":"A plague on you! You're too bad to curse!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["All villains that do stand by thee are pure."],"id":796,"m":"All villains are pure compared with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["There is no leprosy but what thou speak'st."],"id":797,"m":"There's no leprosy except what you speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["If I name thee,","I'll beat thee, but I should infect my hands."],"id":798,"m":"If I named you, that would be a curse. I'd beat you, but I'd infect my hands."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["I would my tongue could rot them off!"],"id":799,"m":"I wish my tongue could rot them off!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Away, thou issue of a mangy dog!","Choler does kill me that thou art alive;","I swound to see thee."],"id":800,"m":"Get away, you son of a mangy dog! It's killing me with anger that you're alive. I faint to see you."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Would thou wouldst burst!"],"id":801,"m":"I wish you'd burst!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Away,","Thou tedious rogue! I am sorry I shall lose","A stone by thee."],"id":802,"m":"Away, you tedious rogue! I'm sorry I'll waste a stone on you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Throws a stone at him.","id":803},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Beast!"],"id":804,"m":"Beast!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Slave!"],"id":805,"m":"Slave!"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Toad!"],"id":806,"m":"Toad!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Rogue, rogue, rogue!","I am sick of this false world, and will love nought","But even the mere necessities upon't.","Then, Timon, presently prepare thy grave;","Lie where the light foam of the sea may beat","Thy gravestone daily: make thine epitaph,","That death in me at others' lives may laugh."],"id":807,"m":"Rogue, rogue, rogue! I'm sick of this false world, and will love nothing in it but the bare necessities. Then, Timon, get your grave ready at once. Lie where the light sea foam can beat on your gravestone every day. Write your epitaph, so that in my death I can laugh at other people's lives."},{"t":"sd","text":"Looking on the gold.","id":808},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","cont":1,"lines":["O thou sweet king-killer, and dear divorce","'Twixt natural son and sire! thou bright defiler","Of Hymen's purest bed! thou valiant Mars!","Thou ever young, fresh, lov'd, and delicate wooer,","Whose blush doth thaw the consecrated snow","That lies on Dian's lap! thou visible god,","That solder'st close impossibilities,","And mak'st them kiss! that speak'st with every tongue","To every purpose! O thou touch of hearts!","Think, thy slave man rebels, and by thy virtue","Set them into confounding odds, that beasts","May have the world in empire!"],"id":809,"m":"Oh you sweet killer of kings, and dear divider of a natural son from his father! You bright defiler of the purest marriage bed! You brave Mars! You ever young, fresh, loved and delicate suitor, whose blush melts the holy snow that lies in Diana's lap! You visible god, who welds together things that could never meet and makes them kiss! You who speak every language for every purpose! Oh you touchstone of hearts! Imagine that man, your slave, is rebelling, and by your power set men fighting among themselves until they destroy each other, so that the beasts may rule the world!"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Would 'twere so:","But not till I am dead; I'll say thou'st gold:","Thou wilt be throng'd to shortly."],"id":810,"m":"I wish it were so, but not until I'm dead. I'll tell people you have gold. You'll soon be mobbed."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Throng'd to?"],"id":811,"m":"Mobbed?"},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Ay."],"id":812,"m":"Yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Thy back, I prithee."],"id":813,"m":"Show me your back, please."},{"t":"sp","who":"APEMANTUS","lines":["Live, and love thy misery!"],"id":814,"m":"Live, and love your misery!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Long live so, and so die!"],"id":815,"m":"Live long like that, and die like that!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit APEMANTUS.","id":816},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","cont":1,"lines":["I am quit.","More things like men! Eat, Timon, and abhor them."],"id":817,"m":"I'm rid of him. More things that look like men! Eat, Timon, and loathe them."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter BANDITTI.","id":818},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST BANDIT","lines":["Where should he have this gold? It is some poor","fragment, some slender ort of his remainder. The mere want of","gold, and the falling-from of his friends, drove him into this","melancholy."],"id":819,"m":"Where would he get this gold? It must be some poor scrap, some thin leftover of what he had. It was just the lack of gold and his friends' desertion that drove him into this gloom."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND BANDIT","lines":["It is noised he hath a mass of treasure."],"id":820,"m":"Word is he has a heap of treasure."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD BANDIT","lines":["Let us make the assay upon him: if he care not for't,","he will supply us easily; if he covetously reserve it, how","shall's get it?"],"id":821,"m":"Let's try him. If he doesn't care about it, he'll give it to us easily. If he greedily hoards it, how shall we get it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND BANDIT","lines":["True; for he bears it not about him, 'tis hid."],"id":822,"m":"True, because he doesn't carry it on him. It's hidden."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST BANDIT","lines":["Is not this he?"],"id":823,"m":"Isn't this him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL BANDITS","lines":["Where?"],"id":824,"m":"Where?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND BANDIT","lines":["'Tis his description."],"id":825,"m":"He fits the description."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD BANDIT","lines":["He; I know him."],"id":826,"m":"It's him. I know him."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL BANDITS","lines":["Save thee, Timon!"],"id":827,"m":"God save you, Timon!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Now, thieves?"],"id":828,"m":"Well, thieves?"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL BANDITS","lines":["Soldiers, not thieves."],"id":829,"m":"Soldiers, not thieves."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Both too, and women's sons."],"id":830,"m":"Both of those, and sons of women too."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL BANDITS","lines":["We are not thieves, but men that much do want."],"id":831,"m":"We're not thieves, but men in great need."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Your greatest want is, you want much of meat.","Why should you want? Behold, the earth hath roots;","Within this mile break forth a hundred springs;","The oaks bear mast, the briars scarlet hips;","The bounteous housewife, Nature, on each bush","Lays her full mess before you. Want! Why want?"],"id":832,"m":"Your greatest need is that you need a lot of food. Why should you be in need? Look, the earth has roots. Within a mile of here a hundred springs burst out. The oaks bear acorns, the briars red rosehips. Nature, the generous housewife, sets out a full meal for you on every bush. Need! Why are you in need?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST BANDIT","lines":["We cannot live on grass, on berries, water,","As beasts and birds and fishes."],"id":833,"m":"We can't live on grass, berries and water, like beasts and birds and fish."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Nor on the beasts themselves, the birds, and fishes;","You must eat men. Yet thanks I must you con","That you are thieves profess'd, that you work not","In holier shapes; for there is boundless theft","In limited professions. Rascal thieves,","Here's gold. Go, suck the subtle blood o' the grape","Till the high fever seethe your blood to froth,","And so scape hanging: trust not the physician;","His antidotes are poison, and he slays","More than you rob: take wealth and lives together;","Do villainy, do, since you protest to do't,","Like workmen. I'll example you with thievery:","The sun's a thief, and with his great attraction","Robs the vast sea; the moon's an arrant thief,","And her pale fire she snatches from the sun;","The sea's a thief, whose liquid surge resolves","The moon into salt tears; the earth's a thief,","That feeds and breeds by a composture stolen","From general excrement, each thing's a thief;","The laws, your curb and whip, in their rough power","Has uncheck'd theft. Love not yourselves; away!","Rob one another. There's more gold; cut throats;","All that you meet are thieves. To Athens go,","Break open shops; nothing can you steal","But thieves do lose it: steal no less for this","I give you; and gold confound you howsoe'er!","Amen."],"id":834,"m":"Nor on the beasts themselves, the birds and fish. You have to eat men. Still, I must thank you for being open, professional thieves and not working in holier disguises, because there is boundless theft in the respectable professions. Rascal thieves, here's gold. Go, suck the subtle blood of the grape until the high fever boils your blood to froth, and so escape hanging. Don't trust the doctor: his medicines are poison, and he kills more men than you rob. Take wealth and lives together. Do villainy, since you profess to do it, like true craftsmen. I'll give you examples of thieving. The sun is a thief, and with his great pull he robs the vast sea. The moon is an outright thief, and snatches her pale fire from the sun. The sea is a thief, whose liquid waves dissolve the moon into salt tears. The earth is a thief, which feeds and breeds on manure stolen from everything's excrement. Everything is a thief. The laws, your bridle and your whip, commit unchecked theft with their rough power. Don't love yourselves. Go! Rob one another. Here's more gold. Cut throats. Everyone you meet is a thief. Go to Athens, break open shops. You can steal nothing that a thief doesn't lose. Don't steal any less because of what I give you, and may gold destroy you anyway! Amen."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD BANDIT","lines":["Has almost charm'd me from my profession by","persuading me to it."],"id":835,"m":"He has almost charmed me out of my profession by urging me to follow it."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST BANDIT","lines":["'Tis in the malice of mankind that he thus advises","us; not to have us thrive in our mystery."],"id":836,"m":"It's out of hatred for mankind that he advises us like this, not so that we'll succeed in our trade."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND BANDIT","lines":["I'll believe him as an enemy, and give over my trade."],"id":837,"m":"I'll believe him as I would an enemy, and give up my trade."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST BANDIT","lines":["Let us first see peace in Athens. There is no time so","miserable but a man may be true."],"id":838,"m":"Let's wait until there's peace in Athens first. No time is so wretched that a man can't be honest."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt BANDITTI.","id":839},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter FLAVIUS.","id":840},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["O you gods!","Is yond despised and ruinous man my lord?","Full of decay and failing? O monument","And wonder of good deeds evilly bestow'd!","What an alteration of honour","Has desperate want made!","What viler thing upon the earth than friends","Who can bring noblest minds to basest ends!","How rarely does it meet with this time's guise,","When man was wish'd to love his enemies!","Grant I may ever love, and rather woo","Those that would mischief me than those that do!","He has caught me in his eye: I will present","My honest grief unto him; and, as my lord,","Still serve him with my life. My dearest master!"],"id":841,"m":"Oh you gods! Is that despised, ruined man over there my lord? So wasted and failing? Oh, monument and wonder of good deeds badly placed! What a change in an honourable man desperate poverty has made! What on earth is viler than friends who can bring the noblest minds to the lowest ends! How well this suits the fashion of our times, when man was told to love his enemies! Grant that I may always love, and rather court those who openly mean me harm than those who do me harm while pretending friendship! He has seen me. I'll show him my honest grief and still serve him as my lord with my life. My dearest master!"},{"t":"sd","text":"TIMON comes forward.","id":842},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Away! What art thou?"],"id":843,"m":"Get away! What are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["Have you forgot me, sir?"],"id":844,"m":"Have you forgotten me, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Why dost ask that? I have forgot all men;","Then, if thou grant'st thou'rt a man, I have forgot thee."],"id":845,"m":"Why do you ask that? I have forgotten all men. So, if you admit you're a man, I have forgotten you."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["An honest poor servant of yours."],"id":846,"m":"An honest, poor servant of yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Then I know thee not:","I never had honest man about me; ay all","I kept were knaves, to serve in meat to villains."],"id":847,"m":"Then I don't know you. I never had an honest man around me. All the men I kept were rogues who served food to villains."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["The gods are witness,","Ne'er did poor steward wear a truer grief","For his undone lord than mine eyes for you."],"id":848,"m":"The gods are my witness, no poor steward ever grieved more truly for his ruined lord than my eyes grieve for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["What! dost thou weep? Come nearer. Then I love thee,","Because thou art a woman, and disclaim'st","Flinty mankind, whose eyes do never give","But thorough lust and laughter. Pity's sleeping:","Strange times, that weep with laughing, not with weeping!"],"id":849,"m":"What, are you crying? Come closer. Then I love you, because you are a woman and disown hard-hearted mankind, whose eyes never water except from lust or laughter. Pity is asleep. Strange times, that weep from laughing, not from weeping!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["I beg of you to know me, good my lord,","To accept my grief, and whilst this poor wealth lasts","To entertain me as your steward still."],"id":850,"m":"I beg you to recognise me, my good lord, to accept my grief, and, while this little money lasts, to keep me on as your steward still."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Had I a steward","So true, so just, and now so comfortable?","It almost turns my dangerous nature mild.","Let me behold thy face. Surely, this man","Was born of woman.","Forgive my general and exceptless rashness,","You perpetual sober gods! I do proclaim","One honest man, mistake me not, but one;","No more, I pray, and he's a steward.","How fain would I have hated all mankind!","And thou redeem'st thyself: but all, save thee,","I fell with curses.","Methinks thou art more honest now than wise;","For, by oppressing and betraying me,","Thou mightst have sooner got another service:","For many so arrive at second masters","Upon their first lord's neck. But tell me true,—","For I must ever doubt, though ne'er so sure,—","Is not thy kindness subtle, covetous,","If not a usuring kindness and as rich men deal gifts,","Expecting in return, twenty for one?"],"id":851,"m":"Did I have a steward so loyal, so honest, and now such a comfort? It almost turns my savage nature mild. Let me look at your face. Surely this man was born of woman. Forgive my sweeping, exceptionless rashness, you eternally calm gods! I declare one honest man (don't mistake me, only one, no more, I beg you) and he is a steward. How gladly I would have hated all mankind! And you save yourself from it. But everyone except you I strike down with curses. I think you're more honest than wise now, because by oppressing and betraying me you could have found another job sooner. Many men climb to a second master over their first lord's neck. But tell me truly, for I must always doubt, however sure I am: isn't your kindness cunning and greedy, a moneylender's kindness, like rich men giving gifts and expecting twenty in return for one?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["No, my most worthy master, in whose breast","Doubt and suspect, alas! are plac'd too late!","You should have fear'd false times when you did feast;","Suspect still comes where an estate is least.","That which I show, heaven knows, is merely love,","Duty and zeal to your unmatched mind,","Care of your food and living; and, believe it,","My most honour'd lord,","For any benefit that points to me,","Either in hope or present, I'd exchange","For this one wish, that you had power and wealth","To requite me by making rich yourself."],"id":852,"m":"No, my most worthy master, in whose heart doubt and suspicion have, alas, been placed too late! You should have feared false times while you were feasting. Suspicion always comes when wealth is at its lowest. What I show, heaven knows, is pure love, duty and devotion to your unmatched mind, and care for your food and livelihood. And believe me, my most honoured lord, I would exchange any benefit to me, now or in the future, for this one wish: that you had the power and wealth to repay me by making yourself rich."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Look thee, 'tis so! Thou singly honest man,","Here, take: the gods, out of my misery,","Have sent thee treasure. Go, live rich and happy,","But thus condition'd: thou shalt build from men;","Hate all, curse all, show charity to none,","But let the famish'd flesh slide from the bone,","Ere thou relieve the beggar; give to dogs","What thou deny'st to men; let prisons swallow 'em,","Debts wither 'em to nothing; be men like blasted woods,","And may diseases lick up their false bloods!","And so, farewell and thrive."],"id":853,"m":"Look, it is so! You single honest man, here, take this. The gods have sent you treasure out of my misery. Go, live rich and happy, but on this condition: you shall build your house far from men. Hate everyone, curse everyone, show charity to no one, but let starving flesh slide off the bone before you help a beggar. Give to dogs what you refuse to men. Let prisons swallow them and debts wither them to nothing. Let men be like blighted woods, and may diseases lick up their false blood! And so, goodbye, and prosper."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["O! let me stay","And comfort you, my master."],"id":854,"m":"Oh, let me stay and comfort you, my master."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["If thou hatest curses,","Stay not; fly, whilst thou'rt bless'd and free:","Ne'er see thou man, and let me ne'er see thee."],"id":855,"m":"If you hate curses, don't stay. Run, while you're blessed and free. Never see man again, and let me never see you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt severally.","id":856},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"The woods. Before Timon's cave.","id":857},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter POET and PAINTER.","id":858},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["As I took note of the place, it cannot be far where he","abides."],"id":859,"m":"From what I noticed of the place, he can't be living far away."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["What's to be thought of him? Does the rumour hold for true that","he is so full of gold?"],"id":860,"m":"What should we think of him? Is the rumour true that he has so much gold?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["Certain. Alcibiades reports it; Phrynia and Timandra had","gold of him: he likewise enriched poor straggling soldiers with","great quantity. 'Tis said he gave unto his steward a mighty sum."],"id":861,"m":"Certainly. Alcibiades says so. Phrynia and Timandra got gold from him. He also made poor stray soldiers rich with a great quantity. They say he gave his steward a huge sum."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["Then this breaking of his has been but a try for his friends?"],"id":862,"m":"Then this bankruptcy of his was only a test of his friends?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["Nothing else. You shall see him a palm in Athens again,","and flourish with the highest. Therefore 'tis not amiss we tender","our loves to him in this supposed distress of his; it will show","honestly in us, and is very likely to load our purposes with what","they travail for, if it be just and true report that goes of his","having."],"id":863,"m":"Nothing else. You'll see him flourish like a palm tree in Athens again, among the highest. So it won't be a bad idea to offer him our love in this supposed hardship of his. It will look honest in us, and it's very likely to load our ambitions with what they're working for, if the reports of his wealth are true."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["What have you now to present unto him?"],"id":864,"m":"What have you got to give him now?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["Nothing at this time but my visitation; only, I will","promise him an excellent piece."],"id":865,"m":"Nothing at the moment except my visit. I'll just promise him an excellent painting."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["I must serve him so too, tell him of an intent that's coming","toward him."],"id":866,"m":"I must do the same with him: tell him about a work I'm planning for him."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["Good as the best. Promising is the very air o' the time;","it opens the eyes of expectation. Performance is ever the duller","for his act, and, but in the plainer and simpler kind of people,","the deed of saying is quite out of use. To promise is most","courtly and fashionable; performance is a kind of will or","testament which argues a great sickness in his judgment that","makes it."],"id":867,"m":"The best plan. Promising is the very fashion of the times; it opens the eyes of expectation. Doing is always duller once it's done, and, except among plainer and simpler people, keeping your word is quite out of fashion. Promising is most courtly and fashionable. Doing is like making a will: it shows the man who does it has a great sickness in his judgement."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter TIMON from his cave.","id":868},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["[Aside] Excellent workman! Thou canst not paint a man so bad","as is thyself."],"id":869,"m":"(Aside) Excellent workman! You can't paint a man as bad as you are yourself."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["I am thinking what I shall say I have provided for him. It","must be a personating of himself; a satire against the softness","of prosperity, with a discovery of the infinite flatteries that","follow youth and opulency."],"id":870,"m":"I'm thinking what I'll say I've prepared for him. It must be a portrait of himself: a satire against the softness of prosperity, exposing the endless flatteries that follow youth and wealth."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["[Aside] Must thou needs stand for a villain in thine own","work? Wilt thou whip thine own faults in other men? Do so, I have","gold for thee."],"id":871,"m":"(Aside) Must you play the villain in your own work? Will you whip your own faults in other men? Do so; I have gold for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["Nay, let's seek him;","Then do we sin against our own estate","When we may profit meet, and come too late."],"id":872,"m":"Come, let's look for him. We sin against our own interests if we come too late when we could meet with profit."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["True;","When the day serves, before black-corner'd night,","Find what thou want'st by free and offer'd light.","Come."],"id":873,"m":"True. While the day lasts, before black, secret night, find what you want by the free light on offer. Come."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["[Aside] I'll meet you at the turn. What a god's gold,","That he is worshipp'd in a baser temple","Than where swine feed!","'Tis thou that rigg'st the bark and plough'st the foam,","Settlest admired reverence in a slave.","To thee be worship! and thy saints for aye","Be crown'd with plagues, that thee alone obey!","Fit I meet them."],"id":874,"m":"(Aside) I'll meet you at the corner. What a god gold is, that he's worshipped in a temple lower than a pigsty! It's you that rigs the ship and ploughs the sea foam, and makes men admire and revere a slave. Worship to you! And may your saints, who obey you alone, be crowned with plagues forever! It's time I met them."},{"t":"sd","text":"Advancing from his cave.","id":875},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["Hail, worthy Timon!"],"id":876,"m":"Greetings, worthy Timon!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["Our late noble master!"],"id":877,"m":"Our former noble master!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Have I once liv'd to see two honest men?"],"id":878,"m":"Have I lived to see two honest men?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["Sir,","Having often of your open bounty tasted,","Hearing you were retir'd, your friends fall'n off,","Whose thankless natures—O abhorred spirits!","Not all the whips of heaven are large enough—","What! to you,","Whose star-like nobleness gave life and influence","To their whole being! I am rapt, and cannot cover","The monstrous bulk of this ingratitude","With any size of words."],"id":879,"m":"Sir, having often enjoyed your open generosity, and hearing that you had withdrawn and that your friends had deserted you, whose ungrateful natures (oh, hateful spirits! not all the whips of heaven are big enough) what, to you, whose starlike nobility gave life and power to their whole being! I'm overcome and cannot cover the monstrous size of this ingratitude with words of any size."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Let it go naked: men may see't the better.","You, that are honest, by being what you are,","Make them best seen and known."],"id":880,"m":"Let it go naked; men can see it better that way. You who are honest, by being what you are, show them up best."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["He and myself","Have travail'd in the great shower of your gifts,","And sweetly felt it."],"id":881,"m":"He and I have walked in the great shower of your gifts and felt it sweetly."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Ay, you are honest men."],"id":882,"m":"Yes, you're honest men."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["We are hither come to offer you our service."],"id":883,"m":"We have come here to offer you our service."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Most honest men! Why, how shall I requite you?","Can you eat roots, and drink cold water? No?"],"id":884,"m":"Most honest men! Now, how shall I repay you? Can you eat roots and drink cold water? No?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POET AND PAINTER","lines":["What we can do, we'll do, to do you service."],"id":885,"m":"Whatever we can do, we'll do, to serve you."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Ye're honest men! Ye've heard that I have gold;","I am sure you have. Speak truth; ye're honest men."],"id":886,"m":"You're honest men! You've heard that I have gold. I'm sure you have. Tell the truth; you're honest men."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["So it is said, my noble lord; but therefore","Came not my friend nor I."],"id":887,"m":"So people say, my noble lord, but that isn't why my friend and I came."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Good honest men! Thou draw'st a counterfeit","Best in all Athens. Thou'rt, indeed, the best;","Thou counterfeit'st most lively."],"id":888,"m":"Good honest men! You paint the best likeness in all Athens. You really are the best. You fake most lifelike."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["So, so, my lord."],"id":889,"m":"Fairly well, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["E'en so, sir, as I say."],"id":890,"m":"Just as I say, sir."},{"t":"sd","text":"To the POET.","id":891},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","cont":1,"lines":["And for thy fiction,","Why, thy verse swells with stuff so fine and smooth","That thou art even natural in thine art.","But for all this, my honest-natur'd friends,","I must needs say you have a little fault.","Marry, 'tis not monstrous in you; neither wish I","You take much pains to mend."],"id":892,"m":"And as for your fiction, why, your verse swells with matter so fine and smooth that your art is second nature to you. But for all this, my honest friends, I must say you have one small fault. It isn't monstrous in you, of course, and I don't want you to take much trouble to correct it."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET AND PAINTER","lines":["Beseech your honour","To make it known to us."],"id":893,"m":"We beg your honour to tell us what it is."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["You'll take it ill."],"id":894,"m":"You'll take it badly."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET AND PAINTER","lines":["Most thankfully, my lord."],"id":895,"m":"Most gratefully, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Will you indeed?"],"id":896,"m":"Will you really?"},{"t":"sp","who":"POET AND PAINTER","lines":["Doubt it not, worthy lord."],"id":897,"m":"Don't doubt it, worthy lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["There's never a one of you but trusts a knave","That mightily deceives you."],"id":898,"m":"Each one of you trusts a rogue who deceives you terribly."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET AND PAINTER","lines":["Do we, my lord?"],"id":899,"m":"Do we, my lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Ay, and you hear him cog, see him dissemble,","Know his gross patchery, love him, feed him,","Keep in your bosom; yet remain assur'd","That he's a made-up villain."],"id":900,"m":"Yes, and you hear him cheat, see him pretend, know all his crude deceit, and yet you love him, feed him, keep him close to your heart, while being quite sure he's a complete villain."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAINTER","lines":["I know not such, my lord."],"id":901,"m":"I don't know anyone like that, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET","lines":["Nor I."],"id":902,"m":"Nor do I."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Look you, I love you well; I'll give you gold,","Rid me these villains from your companies.","Hang them or stab them, drown them in a draught,","Confound them by some course, and come to me,","I'll give you gold enough."],"id":903,"m":"Look, I love you well. I'll give you gold if you rid your company of these villains. Hang them or stab them, drown them in a sewer, destroy them some way, and come to me. I'll give you plenty of gold."},{"t":"sp","who":"POET AND PAINTER","lines":["Name them, my lord; let's know them."],"id":904,"m":"Name them, my lord. Tell us who they are."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["You that way, and you this, but two in company;","Each man apart, all single and alone,","Yet an arch-villain keeps him company."],"id":905,"m":"You go that way, and you this way: still two of you together. Each of you on his own, single and alone, still has an arch-villain for company."},{"t":"sd","text":"To the PAINTER.","id":906},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","cont":1,"lines":["If, where thou art, two villains shall not be,","Come not near him."],"id":907,"m":"(To the Painter) If you don't want two villains where you are, don't go near him."},{"t":"sd","text":"To the POET.","id":908},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","cont":1,"lines":["If thou wouldst not reside","But where one villain is, then him abandon.","Hence! pack! there's gold; you came for gold, ye slaves."],"id":909,"m":"(To the Poet) If you won't live anywhere except where there's only one villain, then leave him. Get out! Clear off! There's gold. You came for gold, you slaves."},{"t":"sd","text":"To the PAINTER.","id":910},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","cont":1,"lines":["You have work for me; there's payment; hence!"],"id":911,"m":"(To the Painter) You did work for me; there's your payment. Go!"},{"t":"sd","text":"To the POET.","id":912},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","cont":1,"lines":["You are an alchemist; make gold of that.","Out, rascal dogs!"],"id":913,"m":"(To the Poet) You're an alchemist; make gold out of that. Out, you rascal dogs!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Beats them out and then returns to his cave.","id":914},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter FLAVIUS and two SENATORS.","id":915},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["It is vain that you would speak with Timon;","For he is set so only to himself","That nothing but himself, which looks like man,","Is friendly with him."],"id":916,"m":"It's no use trying to speak with Timon. He's so wrapped up in himself that nothing that looks like a man is friendly with him except himself."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Bring us to his cave.","It is our part and promise to the Athenians","To speak with Timon."],"id":917,"m":"Take us to his cave. We have promised the Athenians, and it's our duty, to speak with Timon."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SENATOR","lines":["At all times alike","Men are not still the same; 'twas time and griefs","That fram'd him thus. Time, with his fairer hand,","Offering the fortunes of his former days,","The former man may make him. Bring us to him,","And chance it as it may."],"id":918,"m":"Men aren't always the same at all times. It was time and griefs that made him like this. Time, with a kinder hand, offering him the fortunes of his earlier days, may make him the man he was. Take us to him, and let what happens happen."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["Here is his cave.","Peace and content be here! Lord Timon! Timon!","Look out, and speak to friends. The Athenians","By two of their most reverend Senate greet thee.","Speak to them, noble Timon."],"id":919,"m":"Here's his cave. Peace and contentment be here! Lord Timon! Timon! Come out and speak to friends. The Athenians greet you through two of their most respected senators. Speak to them, noble Timon."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter TIMON from his cave.","id":920},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Thou sun that comfort'st, burn! Speak and be hang'd!","For each true word, a blister! and each false","Be as a cauterizing to the root o' the tongue,","Consuming it with speaking!"],"id":921,"m":"You comforting sun, burn them! Speak and be hanged! For every true word you say, may you get a blister, and may every false word burn your tongue to the root, consuming it as you speak!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Worthy Timon,—"],"id":922,"m":"Worthy Timon..."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Of none but such as you, and you of Timon."],"id":923,"m":"Worthy of nobody but people like you, and you worthy of Timon."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["The senators of Athens greet thee, Timon."],"id":924,"m":"The senators of Athens greet you, Timon."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["I thank them; and would send them back the plague,","Could I but catch it for them."],"id":925,"m":"I thank them, and I'd send them back the plague, if I could catch it for them."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["O! forget","What we are sorry for ourselves in thee.","The senators with one consent of love","Entreat thee back to Athens, who have thought","On special dignities, which vacant lie","For thy best use and wearing."],"id":926,"m":"Oh, forget what we ourselves are sorry for having done to you. The senators, united in love, beg you to come back to Athens. They have set aside special honours, which lie vacant for you to use and wear."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SENATOR","lines":["They confess","Toward thee forgetfulness too general, gross;","Which now the public body, which doth seldom","Play the recanter, feeling in itself","A lack of Timon's aid, hath sense withal","Of it own fail, restraining aid to Timon,","And send forth us to make their sorrow'd render,","Together with a recompense more fruitful","Than their offence can weigh down by the dram;","Ay, even such heaps and sums of love and wealth","As shall to thee blot out what wrongs were theirs,","And write in thee the figures of their love,","Ever to read them thine."],"id":927,"m":"They confess they forgot you too widely and grossly. Now the state, which seldom takes back what it has done, feeling the lack of Timon's help, also recognises its own failure in withholding help from Timon. It has sent us to express its sorrow, together with a reward richer than their offence could outweigh by even a grain: yes, such heaps and sums of love and wealth as will wipe out the wrongs they did you and write their love in you in figures you can always read as yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["You witch me in it;","Surprise me to the very brink of tears.","Lend me a fool's heart and a woman's eyes,","And I'll beweep these comforts, worthy senators."],"id":928,"m":"You bewitch me with this. You surprise me to the very edge of tears. Lend me a fool's heart and a woman's eyes, and I'll weep over these comforts, worthy senators."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Therefore so please thee to return with us,","And of our Athens—thine and ours—to take","The captainship, thou shalt be met with thanks,","Allow'd with absolute power, and thy good name","Live with authority. So soon we shall drive back","Of Alcibiades the approaches wild,","Who, like a boar too savage, doth root up","His country's peace."],"id":929,"m":"So, if you will come back with us and take command of our Athens, yours and ours, you'll be met with thanks, given absolute power, and your good name will live with authority. Then we'll soon drive back the wild attacks of Alcibiades, who, like a savage boar, is rooting up his country's peace."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SENATOR","lines":["And shakes his threat'ning sword","Against the walls of Athens."],"id":930,"m":"And shakes his threatening sword at the walls of Athens."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Therefore, Timon,—"],"id":931,"m":"Therefore, Timon..."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Well, sir, I will. Therefore I will, sir, thus:","If Alcibiades kill my countrymen,","Let Alcibiades know this of Timon,","That Timon cares not. But if he sack fair Athens,","And take our goodly aged men by the beards,","Giving our holy virgins to the stain","Of contumelious, beastly, mad-brain'd war,","Then let him know, and tell him Timon speaks it,","In pity of our aged and our youth","I cannot choose but tell him that I care not,","And let him take't at worst; for their knives care not","While you have throats to answer. For myself,","There's not a whittle in the unruly camp","But I do prize it at my love before","The reverend'st throat in Athens. So I leave you","To the protection of the prosperous gods,","As thieves to keepers."],"id":932,"m":"Well, sir, I will. Therefore I will, sir, like this. If Alcibiades kills my countrymen, let Alcibiades know this from Timon: that Timon doesn't care. But if he sacks fair Athens, and takes our fine old men by their beards, and gives our holy virgins up to the stain of insulting, beastly, mad-brained war, then let him know, and tell him Timon says it, that out of pity for our old and our young I cannot help but tell him I don't care. Let him do his worst. Their knives don't care, as long as you have throats to meet them. As for me, there isn't a pocket-knife in that unruly camp that I don't value more than the most respected throat in Athens. So I leave you to the protection of the kindly gods, as thieves are left to their jailers."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["Stay not, all's in vain."],"id":933,"m":"Don't stay. It's all useless."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Why, I was writing of my epitaph;","It will be seen to-morrow. My long sickness","Of health and living now begins to mend,","And nothing brings me all things. Go, live still;","Be Alcibiades your plague, you his,","And last so long enough!"],"id":934,"m":"Why, I was writing my epitaph. It will be seen tomorrow. My long sickness of health and life is now beginning to get better, and nothing will give me everything. Go, live on. May Alcibiades be your plague and you his, and may it last long enough!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["We speak in vain."],"id":935,"m":"We're wasting our breath."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["But yet I love my country, and am not","One that rejoices in the common wrack,","As common bruit doth put it."],"id":936,"m":"But still I love my country, and I'm not a man who rejoices in its general ruin, as common rumour says."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["That's well spoke."],"id":937,"m":"That's well said."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Commend me to my loving countrymen,—"],"id":938,"m":"Give my regards to my loving countrymen..."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["These words become your lips as they pass through","them."],"id":939,"m":"These words suit your lips as they pass through them."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SENATOR","lines":["And enter in our ears like great triumphers","In their applauding gates."],"id":940,"m":"And they enter our ears like great conquerors through gates full of cheering crowds."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Commend me to them,","And tell them that, to ease them of their griefs,","Their fears of hostile strokes, their aches, losses,","Their pangs of love, with other incident throes","That nature's fragile vessel doth sustain","In life's uncertain voyage, I will some kindness do them:","I'll teach them to prevent wild Alcibiades' wrath."],"id":941,"m":"Give them my regards, and tell them that, to ease them of their griefs, their fears of enemy blows, their aches and losses, their pangs of love and the other troubles that nature's fragile ship must suffer on life's uncertain voyage, I will do them a kindness. I'll teach them how to escape wild Alcibiades' anger."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["I like this well; he will return again."],"id":942,"m":"I like this. He'll come back with us."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["I have a tree, which grows here in my close,","That mine own use invites me to cut down,","And shortly must I fell it. Tell my friends,","Tell Athens, in the sequence of degree","From high to low throughout, that whoso please","To stop affliction, let him take his haste,","Come hither, ere my tree hath felt the axe,","And hang himself. I pray you do my greeting."],"id":943,"m":"I have a tree growing here in my enclosure that I need to cut down for my own use, and I must fell it soon. Tell my friends, tell Athens, in order of rank from highest to lowest, that whoever wants to put an end to his suffering should hurry here, before my tree feels the axe, and hang himself. Please pass on my greeting."},{"t":"sp","who":"FLAVIUS","lines":["Trouble him no further; thus you still shall find him."],"id":944,"m":"Don't trouble him any more. This is how you'll always find him."},{"t":"sp","who":"TIMON","lines":["Come not to me again; but say to Athens","Timon hath made his everlasting mansion","Upon the beached verge of the salt flood,","Who once a day with his embossed froth","The turbulent surge shall cover. Thither come,","And let my gravestone be your oracle.","Lips, let sour words go by and language end:","What is amiss, plague and infection mend!","Graves only be men's works and death their gain!","Sun, hide thy beams! Timon hath done his reign."],"id":945,"m":"Don't come to me again, but tell Athens that Timon has made his everlasting home on the beach at the edge of the salt sea, which once a day the stormy waves will cover with their foam. Come there, and let my gravestone be your oracle. Lips, let bitter words pass and let speech end. Whatever is wrong, let plague and infection put it right! Let graves be men's only work, and death their only profit! Sun, hide your beams! Timon's reign is over."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit TIMON into his cave.","id":946},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["His discontents are unremovably","Coupled to nature."],"id":947,"m":"His discontent is permanently tied to his nature."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SENATOR","lines":["Our hope in him is dead. Let us return","And strain what other means is left unto us","In our dear peril."],"id":948,"m":"Our hope in him is dead. Let's go back and try whatever other means we have left in our great danger."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["It requires swift foot."],"id":949,"m":"It needs speed."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":950},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"Before the walls of Athens.","id":951},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter two other SENATORS with a MESSENGER.","id":952},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Thou hast painfully discover'd; are his files","As full as thy report?"],"id":953,"m":"Your news is painful. Are his ranks as full as you report?"},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["I have spoke the least.","Besides, his expedition promises","Present approach."],"id":954,"m":"I've understated it. And his speed promises he'll be here very soon."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SENATOR","lines":["We stand much hazard if they bring not Timon."],"id":955,"m":"We're in great danger if they don't bring Timon back."},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["I met a courier, one mine ancient friend,","Whom, though in general part we were oppos'd,","Yet our old love had a particular force,","And made us speak like friends. This man was riding","From Alcibiades to Timon's cave","With letters of entreaty, which imported","His fellowship i' the cause against your city,","In part for his sake mov'd."],"id":956,"m":"I met a messenger, an old friend of mine. Although we were on opposite sides in public, our old affection had a private power and made us talk like friends. This man was riding from Alcibiades to Timon's cave with letters begging him to join the campaign against your city, which was partly started for his sake."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the other SENATORS, from TIMON.","id":957},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Here come our brothers."],"id":958,"m":"Here come our colleagues."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SENATOR","lines":["No talk of Timon, nothing of him expect.","The enemies' drum is heard, and fearful scouring","Doth choke the air with dust. In, and prepare.","Ours is the fall, I fear; our foes the snare."],"id":959,"m":"Don't talk of Timon; expect nothing from him. The enemy's drum can be heard, and their terrifying raids are choking the air with dust. Inside, and prepare. I'm afraid the fall will be ours, and the trap our enemies'."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":960},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"III","loc":"The woods. Timon's cave, and a rough tomb.","id":961},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a SOLDIER in the woods, seeking TIMON.","id":962},{"t":"sp","who":"SOLDIER","lines":["By all description this should be the place.","Who's here? Speak, ho! No answer! What is this?","Timon is dead, who hath outstretch'd his span.","Some beast rear'd this; here does not live a man.","Dead, sure; and this his grave. What's on this tomb","I cannot read; the character I'll take with wax.","Our captain hath in every figure skill,","An ag'd interpreter, though young in days;","Before proud Athens he's set down by this,","Whose fall the mark of his ambition is."],"id":963,"m":"By every description this should be the place. Who's here? Hello, speak! No answer! What's this? Timon is dead; he has lived out his span. Some beast built this; no man lives here. Dead, certainly, and this is his grave. I can't read what's written on this tomb. I'll take an impression of the letters in wax. Our captain can read every kind of writing; he's an old interpreter, though young in years. By now he'll have laid siege to proud Athens, whose fall is the target of his ambition."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":964},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"IV","loc":"Before the walls of Athens.","id":965},{"t":"sd","text":"Trumpets sound. Enter ALCIBIADES with his powers.","id":966},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["Sound to this coward and lascivious town","Our terrible approach."],"id":967,"m":"Let the trumpets announce our terrible approach to this cowardly, lustful town."},{"t":"sd","text":"A parley sounded. The SENATORS appear upon the walls.","id":968},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","cont":1,"lines":["Till now you have gone on and fill'd the time","With all licentious measure, making your wills","The scope of justice; till now, myself, and such","As slept within the shadow of your power,","Have wander'd with our travers'd arms, and breath'd","Our sufferance vainly. Now the time is flush,","When crouching marrow, in the bearer strong,","Cries of itself, 'No more!' Now breathless wrong","Shall sit and pant in your great chairs of ease,","And pursy insolence shall break his wind","With fear and horrid flight."],"id":969,"m":"Until now you have gone on filling your time with every kind of licence, making your own wishes the limit of justice. Until now, I and those who lived in the shadow of your power have wandered with folded arms and breathed out our sufferings in vain. Now the time is ripe, when the crouching spirit, grown strong in the one who bears it, cries out of its own accord \"No more!\" Now exhausted wrong shall sit panting in your great easy chairs, and fat insolence shall lose its breath in fear and frantic flight."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Noble and young,","When thy first griefs were but a mere conceit,","Ere thou hadst power or we had cause of fear,","We sent to thee, to give thy rages balm,","To wipe out our ingratitude with loves","Above their quantity."],"id":970,"m":"Noble young man, when your first grievances were still only a thought, before you had power or we had reason to fear, we sent to you to soothe your anger and wipe out our ingratitude with greater love than the wrong deserved."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SENATOR","lines":["So did we woo","Transformed Timon to our city's love","By humble message and by promis'd means.","We were not all unkind, nor all deserve","The common stroke of war."],"id":971,"m":"In the same way we courted the transformed Timon back to our city's love with humble messages and promises of wealth. We were not all unkind, and we don't all deserve to be struck down together by war."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["These walls of ours","Were not erected by their hands from whom","You have receiv'd your griefs; nor are they such","That these great towers, trophies, and schools, should fall","For private faults in them."],"id":972,"m":"These walls of ours weren't built by the hands of the men who wronged you, and the wrongs aren't such that these great towers, monuments and schools should fall for the private faults of a few."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SENATOR","lines":["Nor are they living","Who were the motives that you first went out;","Shame, that they wanted cunning, in excess","Hath broke their hearts. March, noble lord,","Into our city with thy banners spread.","By decimation and a tithed death,—","If thy revenges hunger for that food","Which nature loathes,—take thou the destin'd tenth,","And by the hazard of the spotted die","Let die the spotted."],"id":973,"m":"And the men who first caused you to leave are no longer alive. Shame at their lack of foresight has broken their hearts. March into our city, noble lord, with your banners flying. If your revenge hungers for the food that nature hates, then kill one in every ten, take the tenth man fate assigns you, and by the chance of the spotted dice let the spotted, the guilty, die."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["All have not offended;","For those that were, it is not square to take,","On those that are, revenge: crimes, like lands,","Are not inherited. Then, dear countryman,","Bring in thy ranks, but leave without thy rage;","Spare thy Athenian cradle, and those kin","Which, in the bluster of thy wrath, must fall","With those that have offended. Like a shepherd","Approach the fold and cull th' infected forth,","But kill not all together."],"id":974,"m":"Not everyone has offended. It isn't fair to take revenge on those who are alive for the sake of those who are gone. Crimes, unlike lands, aren't inherited. So, dear countryman, bring in your soldiers but leave your anger outside. Spare your Athenian birthplace and those relatives who, in the storm of your anger, would fall along with the guilty. Like a shepherd, go into the fold and pick out the infected sheep, but don't kill them all together."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SENATOR","lines":["What thou wilt,","Thou rather shalt enforce it with thy smile","Than hew to 't with thy sword."],"id":975,"m":"Whatever you want, you'll achieve it more easily with your smile than by hacking your way to it with your sword."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SENATOR","lines":["Set but thy foot","Against our rampir'd gates and they shall ope,","So thou wilt send thy gentle heart before","To say thou'lt enter friendly."],"id":976,"m":"Just set your foot against our fortified gates and they'll open, as long as you send your gentle heart ahead to say you'll come in as a friend."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SENATOR","lines":["Throw thy glove,","Or any token of thine honour else,","That thou wilt use the wars as thy redress","And not as our confusion, all thy powers","Shall make their harbour in our town till we","Have seal'd thy full desire."],"id":977,"m":"Throw down your glove, or any other token of your honour, to show you'll use this war to set right your wrongs and not to destroy us, and all your forces shall be housed in our town until we have met all your demands."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["Then there's my glove;","Descend, and open your uncharged ports.","Those enemies of Timon's and mine own,","Whom you yourselves shall set out for reproof,","Fall, and no more. And, to atone your fears","With my more noble meaning, not a man","Shall pass his quarter or offend the stream","Of regular justice in your city's bounds,","But shall be render'd to your public laws","At heaviest answer."],"id":978,"m":"Then there's my glove. Come down and open your gates, which haven't been attacked. Only those enemies of Timon's and of mine whom you yourselves pick out for punishment shall die, and no more. And to settle your fears with my nobler intentions, not one of my men shall leave his quarters or break the course of ordinary justice within your city's bounds without being handed over to your public laws for the heaviest punishment."},{"t":"sp","who":"BOTH SENATORS","lines":["'Tis most nobly spoken."],"id":979,"m":"That is most nobly said."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["Descend, and keep your words."],"id":980,"m":"Come down, and keep your promises."},{"t":"sd","text":"The SENATORS descend and open the gates.","id":981},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a SOLDIER.","id":982},{"t":"sp","who":"SOLDIER","lines":["My noble General, Timon is dead;","Entomb'd upon the very hem o' the sea;","And on his gravestone this insculpture, which","With wax I brought away, whose soft impression","Interprets for my poor ignorance."],"id":983,"m":"My noble general, Timon is dead, buried on the very edge of the sea. And on his gravestone is this inscription. I've brought away an impression of it in soft wax, which can speak for my poor ignorance."},{"t":"sp","who":"ALCIBIADES","lines":["[Reads the epitaph] 'Here lies a wretched corse, of wretched soul bereft;","Seek not my name. A plague consume you wicked caitiffs left!","Here lie I, Timon, who alive all living men did hate.","Pass by, and curse thy fill; but pass, and stay not here thy gait.'","These well express in thee thy latter spirits.","Though thou abhorr'dst in us our human griefs,","Scorn'dst our brain's flow, and those our droplets which","From niggard nature fall, yet rich conceit","Taught thee to make vast Neptune weep for aye","On thy low grave, on faults forgiven. Dead","Is noble Timon, of whose memory","Hereafter more. Bring me into your city,","And I will use the olive with my sword;","Make war breed peace, make peace stint war, make each","Prescribe to other, as each other's leech.","Let our drums strike."],"id":984,"m":"(Reads the epitaph) \"Here lies a wretched body, robbed of its wretched soul. Do not look for my name. May a plague destroy you wicked wretches who are left! Here I lie, Timon, who hated all living men while I was alive. Pass by and curse as much as you like, but pass by, and do not stop here.\" These words express well your feelings at the end. Though you hated our human sorrows and scorned the tears that fall in small drops from stingy nature, your rich imagination taught you to make vast Neptune weep forever on your humble grave for faults forgiven. Noble Timon is dead, and of his memory we shall say more later. Take me into your city, and I will use the olive branch together with my sword. I will make war produce peace, and peace limit war, and make each the doctor of the other. Let our drums strike up."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":985}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"Timon","group":"Timon and his household","desc":"A rich Athenian lord who gives without limit: he pays Ventidius's debts, provides a dowry for his servant Lucilius, buys every poem, painting and jewel he is offered, and hands out horses and jewels at his feasts. He believes his friends are as generous as he is and will never let his steward show him the accounts. When his creditors call in his debts and every friend refuses him a loan, he invites them to a last banquet, serves them warm water and stones, and leaves Athens cursing it. In the woods he digs for roots, finds gold, and uses it to fund destruction: he pays Alcibiades to sack Athens, gives gold to whores and thieves to spread disease and theft. He calls himself Misanthropos, refuses the Senate's plea to save the city, and dies offstage in a grave by the sea that he has prepared himself."},{"name":"Flavius","group":"Timon and his household","desc":"Timon's steward. He tries again and again to show Timon that he is spending money he does not have, weeps in private over the waste, and is ignored until it is too late. When the house falls he shares his last money with the other servants and goes to find Timon in the woods. Timon calls him the one honest man, gives him gold, and sends him away with orders to hate mankind."},{"name":"Flaminius, Servilius and Lucilius","group":"Timon and his household","desc":"Timon's servants. Flaminius is sent to Lucullus, refuses a bribe to say he never found him, and throws the coins back. Servilius goes to Lucius and is turned away with excuses. Lucilius is the servant whose marriage Timon makes possible by matching the Old Athenian's dowry."},{"name":"Apemantus","group":"Timon's critics","desc":"A churlish (bad-tempered) philosopher, a Cynic who rails at everyone. He refuses to flatter Timon, eats roots and drinks water at his banquet, and warns that the guests are eating Timon alive. When Timon becomes a hermit, Apemantus visits him in the woods and accuses him of copying his manners out of bad luck rather than choice. The two hurl insults at each other until Timon throws a stone at him."},{"name":"Alcibiades","group":"Timon's critics","desc":"An Athenian captain and Timon's friend. He pleads with the Senate to spare a soldier who killed a man in a fight, is banished for pressing the case, and raises an army against Athens. He finds Timon in the woods and takes his gold. At the end the senators surrender, and Alcibiades agrees to punish only his and Timon's enemies. He reads Timon's epitaph and promises to make war and peace heal each other."},{"name":"Lucullus, Lucius and Sempronius","group":"The false friends","desc":"Lords who have taken Timon's gifts and feasted at his table. When he sends to borrow money, each refuses in his own way. Lucullus thinks the servant has brought a present, then offers him three coins to say he was not found. Lucius boasts in public that he would never refuse Timon, then claims he has just spent his money. Sempronius pretends to be offended that he was not asked first. All of them come back to the second banquet."},{"name":"Ventidius","group":"The false friends","desc":"A friend whom Timon frees from debtors' prison with five talents. When his father dies and leaves him rich, he offers the money back and Timon refuses it. Later, when Timon is in need, Ventidius also refuses to help."},{"name":"The Poet and the Painter","group":"Artists and traders","desc":"Artists who seek Timon's favour. In the first scene the Poet describes his allegory of Fortune's hill, which foretells Timon's fall. In Act 5 they hear that Timon has found gold and visit him with empty promises of new work. He overhears their plan, mocks them, and beats them away."},{"name":"The Jeweller and the Merchant","group":"Artists and traders","desc":"Traders who hope to sell to Timon in the first scene. Apemantus tells the Merchant that trade is his god."},{"name":"The Old Athenian","group":"Artists and traders","desc":"A thrifty old man who complains that Lucilius is courting his daughter. Timon matches the dowry so that the marriage can go ahead."},{"name":"The Senators","group":"Athens","desc":"The rulers of Athens. One of them sends Caphis to demand his money from Timon. In 3.5 they refuse Alcibiades' plea and banish him. When Alcibiades threatens the city they beg Timon to return and lead Athens, and then surrender the gates to Alcibiades."},{"name":"The creditors' servants","group":"Athens","desc":"Caphis, Philotus, Titus, Hortensius and the servants of Varro, Isidore and Lucius come to collect their masters' debts. Some are ashamed of the errand; together they corner Timon in his own house."},{"name":"The Strangers","group":"Athens","desc":"Three men who watch Lucius refuse Servilius. The First Stranger condemns Lucius's ingratitude and says he would have helped Timon himself."},{"name":"The Fool and the Page","group":"Athens","desc":"The Fool serves a brothel-keeper and trades jokes with Apemantus and the creditors' servants. The Page, from the same house, brings letters for Timon and Alcibiades."},{"name":"Cupid and the Amazons","group":"Athens","desc":"Performers in the masque at Timon's first banquet. Cupid praises Timon as patron of the five senses."},{"name":"Phrynia and Timandra","group":"Alcibiades' camp","desc":"Two prostitutes who travel with Alcibiades. Timon gives them gold on condition that they keep spreading disease."},{"name":"The Bandits","group":"Alcibiades' camp","desc":"Thieves who come to rob Timon in the woods. He gives them gold and a speech showing that everything in nature is a thief, which almost persuades one to give up stealing."},{"name":"The Soldier","group":"Alcibiades' camp","desc":"One of Alcibiades' men. He finds Timon's tomb by the sea, cannot read the inscription, and takes a wax copy to his captain."}],"scenes":{"a1s1":{"sum":"In Timon's house a Poet, a Painter, a Jeweller and a Merchant wait to sell to him. The Poet describes his poem: Fortune on a hill beckons one man up, and his followers desert him when she kicks him down. Timon arrives and pays five talents to free his friend Ventidius from prison, then matches an old man's dowry so that his servant Lucilius can marry. He accepts the poem, the painting and the jewel. Apemantus, a sour philosopher, insults everyone and refuses to dine. Alcibiades arrives with his horsemen and is welcomed. Two lords praise Timon's endless generosity on their way to his feast.","key":["The Poet's allegory of Fortune's hill tells the whole plot in advance: the climb, the followers, the fall.","Timon's first acts are generous and real (Ventidius, Lucilius), which makes his later fall harder to dismiss.","Apemantus's rudeness ('He that loves to be flattered is worthy o' the flatterer') sets up the play's argument about flattery."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Timon do for Ventidius?","o":["Lends him a house in the city","Pays five talents to free him from prison","Gives him his daughter","Makes him a senator"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What is the subject of the Poet's work?","o":["The siege of Troy and the fall of its towers to the Greeks","Fortune raising one man and his followers abandoning him","The life of Alcibiades","The gods of Athens"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Timon give money to Lucilius?","o":["To pay his debts to the moneylenders of the city","To match the Old Athenian's daughter's dowry","To buy his freedom from slavery in Timon's house","To start a business"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Why does the Old Athenian object to Lucilius?","o":["He is a foreigner with no Athenian blood","He is a poor servant with no fortune","He has been in prison for his debts","He is already married to another woman"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Why does Apemantus refuse to dine with Timon?","o":["He has already eaten his roots","He will not eat lords' flattery","He is angry at Alcibiades","He was not invited to the feast"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"At a great banquet Ventidius offers to repay the five talents and Timon refuses, saying a true gift expects nothing back. Apemantus sits apart, says a long grace over his roots and water, and warns that the guests are eating Timon. Timon makes a tearful speech about how friends exist to share each other's fortunes. Cupid introduces a masque of ladies dressed as Amazons, and the lords dance with them. Timon hands out jewels, horses and gifts while his steward Flavius, in asides, reveals that the coffers are empty and the lands mortgaged. Apemantus warns Timon that he will soon give himself away, and Timon refuses to listen.","key":["Timon's speech ('what need we have any friends, if we should ne'er have need of 'em?') shows his belief that friendship and wealth are shared.","Flavius's asides reveal the truth the guests cannot see: 'he owes for every word'.","Apemantus's grace ('Grant I may never prove so fond / To trust man on his oath or bond') foretells what will happen."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Ventidius offer at the banquet?","o":["A fine horse from his own stables in the hills","To repay the five talents, doubled","His sword and his armour, polished bright","A poem written in Timon's praise for the feast"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Apemantus eat and drink?","o":["Venison and wine","Roots and water","Bread and ale","Nothing at all"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Flavius reveal in his asides?","o":["That Timon is far richer than any man in Athens","That Timon's coffers are empty and his land is mortgaged","That Alcibiades is a traitor","That the masque was his idea"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"inference"},{"q":"Why does Timon refuse Ventidius's repayment?","o":["A gift that is paid back was never truly given","He has far more money than he will ever need","He wants Ventidius to owe him a great favour","He thinks that Ventidius is still too poor to pay"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does Apemantus say about the men who flatter Timon?","o":["They are honest friends to him","They will repay him in the end","They have nothing to give him back","They dip their meat in his blood"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"inference"}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"A senator adds up what Timon owes him and others: twenty-five thousand. He sees that Timon's waste cannot last and sends his servant Caphis to demand payment at once, before Timon's borrowed feathers are plucked and he is left a naked gull.","key":["The senator's image of Timon as a phoenix that will turn out to be a 'naked gull' marks the moment the creditors move.","His mocking picture of Timon turning gifts into gold ('the dog coins gold') shows that everyone knew Timon's gifts brought returns.","The senator 'loves and honours' Timon but will not 'break my back to heal his finger': self-interest wears friendly words."],"quiz":[{"q":"Whom does the senator send to Timon?","o":["Flavius","Caphis","Lucius","Apemantus"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the senator compare Timon to?","o":["A lion grown old and toothless in the hills of Greece","A phoenix that will become a naked gull","A tree in winter","A ship in a storm"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"concept"},{"q":"Why does the senator act now?","o":["He has fallen out with Timon over a horse","He fears Timon's fortune cannot last","Timon has insulted him","The Senate has ordered it"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"How much does Timon owe the senator and others?","o":["Five hundred talents","Two thousand crowns","A hundred talents","Twenty-five thousand"],"a":3,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the senator say Timon does with a horse he is given?","o":["Breeds ten able horses from it","Rides it to war against Sparta himself","Sells it and keeps all of the money","Gives it straight away to Alcibiades"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a2s2":{"sum":"Flavius despairs over a pile of bills. The servants of Timon's creditors confront Timon as he comes back from hunting, and he sends them to Flavius. Apemantus and a Fool mock the servants while they wait. Alone with Timon, Flavius at last shows him that all his land is mortgaged or lost and that he cannot pay half his debts. Timon is shocked but not afraid: he says he is rich in friends and sends servants to borrow from Lucius, Lucullus, Sempronius and the Senate. Flavius reports that the senators have already refused. Timon sends to Ventidius instead, confident he will repay.","key":["Flavius's memory of weeping by a 'wasteful cock' while the house blazed with feasts is the play's most touching picture of loyalty.","Timon's 'Unwisely, not ignobly, have I given' is his own judgement of himself.","Timon's plan to 'try the argument of hearts by borrowing' turns his ruin into a test of friendship."],"quiz":[{"q":"How far did Timon's land once stretch, according to him?","o":["To the sea, on every side of Attica","To Lacedaemon (Sparta)","To the walls of Rome itself","To Corinth and its harbour"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Timon say his debts will let him do?","o":["Retire","Test his friends","Join Alcibiades","Leave town"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How have the senators answered Flavius?","o":["With a gift of land and money for his lord","With polite excuses and a refusal","With a threat","With a promise for tomorrow"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why did Flavius not warn Timon sooner?","o":["He was afraid of losing his own place","Timon would not listen when he tried","He thought the money would never end","He was away in the country on business"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"implication"},{"q":"What does Timon say about his friends when Flavius weeps?","o":["They are all false to him","They owe him nothing at all","He has too many of them","He is wealthy in them"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"inference"}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"Lucullus receives Timon's servant Flaminius, expecting a present. When he hears that Timon wants to borrow fifty talents, he says he always warned Timon against spending, then offers Flaminius three coins to say he could not find him. Flaminius throws the money back and curses Lucullus, hoping the food Timon gave him turns to poison.","key":["Lucullus's dream of a 'silver basin and ewer' shows he values Timon for his gifts.","The bribe ('wink at me, and say thou sawest me not') shows how cheaply Lucullus values both friendship and honesty.","Flaminius's curse, that Timon's food should feed diseases in Lucullus, uses the play's imagery of eating and poison."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Lucullus expect when Flaminius arrives?","o":["Bad news","A gift","A summons","An apology"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"assumption"},{"q":"What does Lucullus offer Flaminius?","o":["Fifty talents and a horse for Timon's use","Three coins to say he did not find him","A job as his own steward in the city","A horse"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Flaminius do with the coins?","o":["Keeps them","Throws them back","Gives them to Timon","Gives them to a beggar"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Lucullus say about lending money in friendship?","o":["This is no time to lend on bare friendship","Friends should always lend without asking","He would lend Timon all he has gladly","He has already lent Timon a great deal"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What does Flaminius hope will happen to Lucullus?","o":["His house will burn down in the night","His servants will rob him of his gold","He will lose his own fortune at sea","Timon's food will turn to poison in him"],"a":3,"b":1,"p":"purpose"}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"In a public place Lucius tells three strangers that he would never refuse Timon, and is shocked to hear that Lucullus did. Then Servilius arrives asking him for a loan. Lucius at once claims he has just spent his money and is too poor to help, and asks Servilius to give Timon his regrets in his own words. The strangers condemn his ingratitude: Timon has been a father to Lucius, and even they, who never had anything from Timon, would have helped him.","key":["Lucius's boast that he would never have refused Timon is overturned within minutes: dramatic irony at its sharpest.","The First Stranger's speech ('policy sits above conscience') states the scene's moral.","Strangers who owe Timon nothing show more feeling for him than the friends who owe him everything."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Lucius say before Servilius arrives?","o":["That Timon is a fool who deserves his ruin","That he would never have refused Timon","That he owes Timon nothing at all, ever","That he is poor"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What excuse does Lucius give?","o":["He is ill in bed","He has just made a purchase and has no money","His wife forbids it","The Senate has ordered him not to"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"question"},{"q":"Who comments on the scene's ingratitude?","o":["Apemantus, the churlish philosopher","The three Strangers","Flavius","Alcibiades"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the First Stranger say Timon was to Lucius?","o":["A rival who tried to take away all his lands","A father who kept his credit with his purse","A stranger whom Lucius had met only once","A creditor who demanded every penny back"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Lucius say he feels about missing the chance to help?","o":["He is glad he kept his money safe","He thinks Timon will soon be rich","He does not care for Timon at all","He is a beast for not being ready"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"view"}]},"a3s3":{"sum":"Sempronius learns that Lucius, Lucullus and Ventidius have all refused Timon. He pretends to be insulted that he was not asked first, since he was the first to receive Timon's gifts, and refuses on that excuse. Timon's servant bitterly calls him a fine villain and sees that Timon must now keep to his house to avoid his creditors.","key":["Sempronius invents a point of honour ('Who bates mine honour shall not know my coin') to cover his meanness.","The servant's line 'Who cannot keep his wealth must keep his house' marks the end of Timon's open doors.","The servant says the devil made man 'politic' (scheming) and was outdone by him."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does Sempronius say he refuses?","o":["He has no money left after a bad year at sea","He is offended that he was not asked first","He is leaving Athens for Sparta in the morning","Timon insulted him"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Which friends does Sempronius hear have already refused?","o":["Lucius, Lucullus and Ventidius","Alcibiades and Apemantus, his oldest friends","The Poet and the Painter he paid so well","The senators only"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What must Timon now do, according to the servant?","o":["Sell his house","Keep to his house","Leave for Sparta","Join the army"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Sempronius say he would have helped if asked first?","o":["He would have given half of all he owns","He would have sold his house to pay it","Timon would have had three times the sum","He would have gone to the Senate for him"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the servant say about the devil and Sempronius?","o":["The devil did not know what he was doing in making man politic","The devil will soon come to take Sempronius away","The devil is more honest than any lord of Athens","The devil has been paid off by all of Timon's friends"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"inference"}]},"a3s4":{"sum":"Creditors' servants gather at Timon's house at dawn. They note the irony that Lucius, who wears Timon's jewels, is sending to collect from him. Flavius, muffled in a cloak, tells them their masters ate Timon's food when they should have sent their bills, and leaves. Servilius says Timon is ill. Timon bursts out in a rage, finds his own doors barred against him, and tells the servants to cut his heart into sums and count out his blood. Then, calmer, he orders Flavius to invite all his friends to one more feast.","key":["Timon's 'Cut my heart in sums... Tell out my blood' turns the debts into his own body.","Lucius's servant points out that his master wears Timon's jewels while dunning him.","'I'll once more feast the rascals': the first sign of Timon's revenge."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Timon tell the creditors' servants to do?","o":["Come back tomorrow morning, when Flavius has the money","Cut his heart into sums and count out his blood","See Flavius","Sue him"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What irony do the servants notice about Lucius?","o":["He is poorer than Timon now and cannot pay his own debts","He wears jewels Timon gave him while sending to collect","He is Timon's brother","He has already paid"],"a":1,"b":4,"p":"implication"},{"q":"What does Timon order at the end of the scene?","o":["His house to be sold to the highest bidder","Another feast for all his friends","Flavius to leave his service at once","His servants to arm themselves"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why are the creditors' servants at Timon's door so early?","o":["To catch him when he comes out","Their masters have sent them to a feast","They are waiting for Flavius to arrive","They want to see Timon's famous gardens"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"How does Flavius answer the servants?","o":["Their masters ate Timon's food and smiled","He promises they will be paid tomorrow","He tells them Timon has left the city","He gives them each a gold coin to leave"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"information"}]},"a3s5":{"sum":"The senators have condemned a soldier to death for killing a man in a quarrel. Alcibiades pleads for him: he acted in honourable anger and has served Athens well. A senator replies that true courage lies in bearing wrongs, not revenging them. Alcibiades argues back and offers his own victories as security. When he presses too hard, the senators banish him. Alone, he rages that he has defended moneylenders who now repay his wounds with exile, and vows to turn his troops against Athens.","key":["The debate between bearing wrongs and revenging them ('To revenge is no valour, but to bear') runs parallel to Timon's story.","Alcibiades' bitter 'banish usury, / That makes the Senate ugly' links the senators to the moneylending that ruined Timon.","His banishment, like Timon's ruin, shows Athens repaying service with ingratitude."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is Alcibiades' friend condemned?","o":["Treason","Killing a man in a quarrel","Theft","Debt"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What does the First Senator say true valour is?","o":["Fighting bravely in the front line","Bearing wrongs patiently","Obeying the law","Winning wars"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What is Alcibiades' punishment?","o":["Prison for life","Banishment","Exile","Death"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Alcibiades say about his own service to Athens?","o":["He has paid great taxes to it","He has bled for the state","He has never been rewarded","He has led no wars for it"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What does Alcibiades decide when he is banished?","o":["To find Timon and ask for his money","To leave Greece and go to live in Rome","To ask the people to overthrow the Senate","To turn his discontented troops on Athens"],"a":3,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a3s6":{"sum":"The lords arrive for the new feast, each guessing that Timon was only testing them and apologising for not lending. They gossip about Alcibiades' banishment. Timon makes them sit and says a mocking grace that asks the gods to bless his friends with nothing. The covered dishes are opened and contain only warm water. Timon throws the water in their faces, curses them as parasites, flies and wolves, beats them out with the dishes, and swears to hate all mankind. The lords scramble for their hats and gowns and decide he is mad.","key":["The grace ('For these my present friends, as they are to me nothing, so in nothing bless them') is Timon's turning point.","'Smoke and lukewarm water / Is your perfection': the dishes show what the friends are worth.","The final couplet ('One day he gives us diamonds, next day stones') reduces the scene to a joke for the lords."],"quiz":[{"q":"What is in the covered dishes?","o":["Gold coins","Warm water","Roots from the woods","Stones"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What do the lords think Timon was doing when he asked for loans?","o":["Testing them","Joking","Planning to leave","Mocking the Senate"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Timon swear at the end of the feast?","o":["To repay every debt","To hate all mankind","To join Alcibiades","To kill himself"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Timon pray for the senators in his grace?","o":["That the gods will give them long life","That the gods will make them rich","That the gods will make them nothing","That the gods will forgive their debts"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What do the lords say about Timon at the end?","o":["He is wise and has taught them a fair lesson","He will soon be rich again and forgive them","He is mad: jewels one day, stones the next","He has gone to join Alcibiades and his army"],"a":2,"b":2,"p":"inference"}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"Outside the walls of Athens, Timon turns back to curse the city. He calls on wives to be unfaithful, children to disobey, slaves to overthrow the senators, servants to steal and plagues to fall, wishing every bond of order turned into its opposite. He strips off his clothes and goes to the woods, where he expects to find the cruellest beast kinder than man.","key":["The long curse lists every bond of society (marriage, family, law, religion, trade) and calls for it to collapse.","'Timon will to the woods, where he shall find / Th' unkindest beast more kinder than mankind': the turn from city to wilderness.","He asks the gods to let his hate 'grow / To the whole race of mankind, high and low'."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where is Timon in this scene?","o":["In the Senate","Outside the walls of Athens","In his cave","At sea"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Timon take with him from Athens?","o":["His gold, jewels and plate","Nothing but nakedness","His servants","Apemantus"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does he expect to find in the woods?","o":["Peace and rest at last from all his cares","The cruellest beast kinder than mankind","His friends","Gold"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"assumption"},{"q":"What does Timon wish on the Athenians?","o":["That they will fight for Alcibiades in war","That they will grow old and wise in peace","Itches, blains and general leprosy","That they will all leave the city for good"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Timon wish on the senators?","o":["That they grow rich and live long lives","That they will be banished like Alcibiades","Cold sciatica to cripple them","That they will see their error and repent"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"In the empty house Flavius gives his last money to Timon's servants, and they part as friends, saying they have seen better days. Alone, Flavius reflects that Timon has been ruined by his own goodness and resolves to find him and serve him while his gold lasts.","key":["The servants' loyalty contrasts sharply with the lords': 'Yet do our hearts wear Timon's livery'.","'We have seen better days' was already a familiar phrase; here it is a funeral bell for Timon's fortune.","Flavius's 'Undone by goodness' gives the play's most sympathetic judgement of Timon."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Flavius do with his money?","o":["Keeps it all for himself and leaves","Shares it among the servants","Gives it to the creditors at the door","Buries it"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What do the servants say as they part?","o":["We'll meet in Sparta","We have seen better days","Curse Timon","Long live Alcibiades"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Flavius decide to do?","o":["Join Lucius as his steward","Find Timon and serve him","Go home","Become a soldier"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Flavius say about Timon's goodness?","o":["It has been his ruin","It will bring his luck back","It was always pretended","It is gone for good now"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"How do the servants say they will leave?","o":["As poor men all, into the sea of air","As friends of Alcibiades in the army","As servants of the senators of Athens","As beggars at the temple gates of Athens"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a4s3":{"sum":"Digging for roots by his cave, Timon finds gold, curses it as the thing that turns wrong into right, and buries most of it again. Alcibiades marches by with Phrynia and Timandra; Timon gives him gold to destroy Athens without mercy and gives the women gold to spread disease. Apemantus arrives, accuses Timon of copying him, and they trade insults until Timon throws a stone at him. Timon praises gold as the visible god that will set men fighting. Bandits come for his gold; he gives it to them with a speech about how sun, moon, sea and earth are all thieves. Finally Flavius arrives in tears. Timon admits he is the one honest man, gives him gold and sends him away.","key":["'Gold! yellow, glittering, precious gold!': Timon's speech on gold is the play's most famous passage.","Apemantus's charge that Timon's misanthropy is forced by bad luck, not chosen, is the central argument between the two cynics.","Timon's 'one honest man... and he's a steward' is the only exception he ever makes."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Timon find while digging?","o":["Water","Gold","A sword","A letter"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Timon give Alcibiades gold for?","o":["To pay all his soldiers' debts in full","To destroy Athens without mercy","To buy a house","To leave him alone"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Timon tell the bandits?","o":["To give up their crimes and live honestly ever after","That everything in nature is a thief","That he has no gold for them at all","To join Alcibiades"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Apemantus say Timon could have lived better?","o":["By saving his gold and spending none of it","By keeping his house and selling his land","By flattering those who once flattered him","By marrying a rich lady from another city"],"a":2,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does Timon call Flavius the one honest man?","o":["He weeps for Timon and wants nothing","He gives Timon all his own gold back","He has come to fight for him in Athens","He tells Timon that the lords are sorry"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"concept"}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"The Poet and the Painter, having heard of Timon's gold, come to offer him promises of new work. Timon overhears their plans, then greets them as 'honest men', tells each that he keeps company with a villain (the other), and beats them away. Flavius brings two senators, who beg Timon to come back and lead Athens against Alcibiades with absolute power. Timon tells them he does not care if Alcibiades kills them all, then says he will show the Athenians how to escape: let them come and hang themselves on his tree. He announces that he has made his grave on the seashore and goes into his cave.","key":["The Painter's 'Promising is the very air o' the time' satirises flatterers who never deliver.","The senators' offer shows Athens needs Timon now as Timon needed it in Act 3, and he refuses as they refused.","'Timon hath made his everlasting mansion / Upon the beached verge of the salt flood' prepares his death."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why do the Poet and Painter visit Timon?","o":["To apologise","They have heard he has gold","To warn him","Alcibiades sent them"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"What do the senators offer Timon?","o":["A great sum of money to repay his losses in full","Absolute power to lead Athens against Alcibiades","A pardon","A statue"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What 'kindness' does Timon offer the Athenians?","o":["Gold for every man and woman in the city","Use of his tree to hang themselves","Advice on how to win the war","His blessing"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"How does Timon test the Poet and Painter?","o":["He asks each to show him their new work","He offers gold to whoever can sing best","He asks them to bring him news of Athens","He tells each that the other is a villain"],"a":3,"b":2,"p":"information"},{"q":"Where has Timon made his grave?","o":["On the beached verge of the salt flood","In the temple of Athens by the gate","In his cave among the roots and gold","Under the tree where men may hang"],"a":0,"b":1,"p":"information"}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"Senators hear that Alcibiades' army is huge and close. A messenger reports that Alcibiades has written to invite Timon to join him. The senators who went to Timon return with nothing, and the city prepares for the attack.","key":["The senators' hope rests entirely on Timon, whom they let fall.","Alcibiades' letter to Timon shows the war is partly in his cause.","'Ours is the fall, I fear': the city that ruined Timon now fears ruin."],"quiz":[{"q":"What has Alcibiades sent to Timon, according to the messenger?","o":["A great chest of gold from his war chest","Letters inviting him to join the war","An army to guard Timon in the woods","A threat"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What do the returning senators report?","o":["Timon is coming","There is no hope from Timon","Timon is dead","Alcibiades has retreated"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What sound announces the enemy?","o":["Trumpets","A drum","Bells","Cannon"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What do the senators fear in Alcibiades's army?","o":["It has Timon at its head","It is made of Athenians","It is large and very close","It has no food left at all"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Who come back from Timon with no hope?","o":["Flavius and the servants of Timon","Alcibiades's own messengers","The Poet and the Painter of Athens","The senators sent to his cave"],"a":3,"b":1,"p":"purpose"}]},"a5s3":{"sum":"A soldier searching for Timon finds a rough tomb near the sea. He decides Timon is dead, cannot read the inscription, and takes a copy in wax for Alcibiades.","key":["Timon dies offstage: the play never shows how.","The soldier's 'Some beast rear'd this; here does not live a man' echoes Timon's wish to be cut off from mankind.","The wax copy carries Timon's last words to Athens."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the soldier find?","o":["Timon alive and cursing","Timon's tomb","Gold","Apemantus"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"Why does he take a wax copy?","o":["To sell it in the market at Athens","He cannot read the inscription","To hide it from Alcibiades","Timon asked him"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"purpose"},{"q":"Where is Timon buried?","o":["In Athens","Near the sea","In the Senate","Under his tree"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does the soldier say about the inscription?","o":["It is in Greek","He cannot read it","It is worn away","It is written in blood"],"a":1,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"Who does the soldier plan to show the wax copy to?","o":["His captain, Alcibiades","The senators of Athens","Flavius, Timon's steward","Apemantus in his cave"],"a":0,"b":4,"p":"inference"}]},"a5s4":{"sum":"Alcibiades arrives before Athens and declares the time of the senators' unchecked power is over. The senators plead that the men who wronged him are dead, that crimes are not inherited, and that he should punish only the guilty. Alcibiades throws down his glove and promises to punish only his and Timon's enemies. A soldier brings the wax copy of Timon's epitaph, which Alcibiades reads aloud. He honours Timon, then marches into the city promising to make war and peace heal each other.","key":["The senators' argument ('crimes, like lands, / Are not inherited') wins mercy that Timon refused them.","The epitaph ('Here lie I, Timon, who alive all living men did hate') ends Timon's story in curses.","Alcibiades' last lines join 'the olive' and 'the sword': peace made by force."],"quiz":[{"q":"What do the senators ask Alcibiades to do?","o":["Kill them all","Punish only the guilty","Leave Athens","Become a senator"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What token does Alcibiades give?","o":["His sword","His glove","His ring","His banner"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"information"},{"q":"What does Alcibiades promise at the end?","o":["The total destruction of the city and its walls","To use the olive with the sword","To leave Athens for ever","To bring Timon back"],"a":1,"b":1,"p":"implication"},{"q":"What do the senators say about the people who wronged Timon?","o":["Most of them are already dead","They have fled from the city","They are in prison already","They want to make amends"],"a":0,"b":2,"p":"inference"},{"q":"What does Timon's epitaph tell the reader to do?","o":["Pray for his soul and for Athens","Remember his gifts to the city","Pass by and curse, but do not stay","Weep for a man who loved too much"],"a":2,"b":1,"p":"information"}]}},"notes":{"6":"'Magic of bounty': Timon's generosity works like a spell, conjuring crowds of followers. The Poet speaks to the crowd as if it were a spirit summoned by Timon's power.","18":"The Poet claims his verse comes naturally, like gum oozing from a tree or fire from a struck flint. The play keeps asking whether the artists' praise is natural or bought.","26":"'Artificial strife' means art competing with nature. The Poet's praise of the painting as 'livelier than life' is the kind of flattery Apemantus attacks.","31":"A 'wide sea of wax': Roman writers wrote on wax tablets. The Poet insists his poem targets no one in particular, then describes a man who is plainly Timon.","35":"The Poet's allegory of Fortune on a hill was a common Renaissance image. Here it is a forecast of the play's plot: Timon is beckoned up, then his followers desert him.","40":"'The foot above the head': fortune's wheel turns the great upside down. The Painter's 'moral paintings' were emblem pictures of this kind.","43":"A talent was an ancient weight of silver or gold and a huge sum. Five talents to free Ventidius is a fortune, given in a moment.","46":"'Tis not enough to help the feeble up, / But to support him after': Timon's view of generosity is admirable. The play tests what happens when no one applies it to him.","69":"Timon matches the Old Athenian's offer so that Lucilius is 'equal' to the bride: he literally buys his servant a marriage.","77":"Timon prefers paintings to men because paintings are what they seem. It is an early sign of his later disgust at human falseness.","90":"Apemantus is a Cynic philosopher; the name comes from the Greek for 'dog-like'. He keeps calling himself and others dogs.","102":"Apemantus means that there are no honest Athenians, so killing one is 'doing nothing'.","128":"'He that loves to be flattered is worthy o' the flatterer' sums up Apemantus's view: Timon and his flatterers deserve each other.","138":"Apemantus attacks trade as a god that men worship instead of the real gods. Gold as a false god returns in Timon's speeches in Act 4.","148":"'The strain of man's bred out / Into baboon and monkey': mankind has degenerated into apes, which copy what they see.","177":"Timon refuses repayment: a real gift expects nothing back. 'Faults that are rich are fair' is ironic: rich men's faults are always excused.","186":"'Ira furor brevis est' is Latin for 'anger is a brief madness', from the Roman poet Horace. Timon will later show a madness of anger that is not brief.","189":"Apemantus pictures the guests dipping their bread in Timon's blood, like a Last Supper where the host is betrayed by those who eat with him.","192":"Apemantus's grace mocks the usual prayer before a meal. Its list of things not to trust (oaths, bonds, harlots, sleeping dogs, jailers, friends) foretells the play.","201":"Timon's speech is his creed: friends exist to share fortunes, and he would gladly be poorer to be closer to them. The gap between this ideal and the lords' behaviour drives the tragedy.","216":"Cupid presents the masque as a feast for the five senses, with Timon as their patron. Masques were lavish court entertainments of music, dance and costume.","221":"Apemantus's line 'Men shut their doors against a setting sun' predicts exactly how Timon's friends will treat him.","232":"Flavius's aside shows he knows the truth but cannot 'cross' Timon. 'Bounty had not eyes behind' means generosity cannot look back at what it costs.","254":"'He owes / For every word': Timon's promises are all bought on credit. His land is 'put to their books', mortgaged to his creditors.","263":"Alcibiades' 'defil'd land' is a pun: a soldier's only land is the battlefield, 'pitched' with tents and soaked with blood.","272":"'Serving of becks and jutting out of bums': the lords bow so low their bottoms stick out. Apemantus mocks bows and curtsies as bought.","281":"The senator's sums show how big the debts are: twenty-five thousand to him alone, with nine thousand to Varro and Isidore.","284":"'Every feather sticks in his own wing': when each lender takes back his own, Timon will be a plucked gull, not the phoenix he seems. A gull was also a fool.","292":"Flavius blames Timon for neither keeping accounts nor caring 'what is to continue'. The rhyme 'unwise... so kind' sums up his tragedy.","343":"'Corinth' was famous for brothels, so the Fool's mistress keeps a brothel. Scalding chickens was a joke about curing venereal disease in hot baths.","366":"A 'whoremaster' is a man who uses prostitutes. The Fool describes him as a spirit that takes every shape from lord to knight.","380":"Flavius has tried many times to show Timon the accounts. 'The greatest of your having lacks a half / To pay your present debts': Timon owes more than twice what he owns.","383":"Lacedaemon is Sparta. Timon still thinks of his lands as vast.","386":"A 'wasteful cock' is a running tap. Flavius wept in private while the cellars flowed with wine.","389":"'Unwisely, not ignobly, have I given': Timon's own verdict, and the one many readers accept.","400":"Flavius imitates the senators' broken, evasive phrases. The jerky rhythm shows excuses without substance.","401":"Timon blames the senators' age for their coldness: old blood is 'caked'. He still cannot believe his friends will fail him.","414":"Lucullus's dream of a silver basin and ewer (water jug) shows he expects a present. The comedy is that he is the one asked to give.","427":"'Solidares' were small coins. Lucullus's bribe is three coins against a request for fifty talents.","432":"Flaminius hopes the coins will scald Lucullus in hell, where misers were punished with molten metal.","449":"Lucius says 'fifty-five hundred talents', a muddled sum. The figures for money in this play are inconsistent, a sign that the text was never fully revised.","453":"Lucius swears by the gods that he cannot help, and insists on his 'own words' being passed on. His elaborate excuse is as empty as Lucullus's bribe.","461":"The First Stranger compares the flatterer to one who 'dips in the same dish', echoing Judas at the Last Supper.","463":"'Policy sits above conscience': self-interest has taken the seat of morality.","469":"Sempronius claims to be insulted at being asked last. His pretended honour hides plain meanness.","471":"The servant says that man has outdone the devil in villainy. 'Takes virtuous copies to be wicked' means he follows good examples for bad ends.","489":"Timon's day is compared to the sun's, but a spendthrift cannot rise again as the sun does.","534":"Timon turns the bills into weapons: 'knock me down with 'em'. He offers his body as payment.","556":"Timon's plan for a last feast is the first act of his revenge.","564":"Alcibiades argues that mercy is the 'virtue of the law'. The case of his friend is never named in detail, which keeps attention on the principle.","565":"The senator's argument that true valour is patient endurance is a Stoic idea. It reflects on Timon, who cannot bear his wrongs.","583":"Alcibiades accuses the senators of usury, moneylending at interest. The same senators lent money to Timon.","632":"Timon's mock grace reverses a blessing into a curse. It is his first public attack on his false friends.","636":"'Smoke and lukewarm water / Is your perfection': the dishes are a picture of the lords, hot air and nothing.","638":"'Minute-jacks' were figures that struck the hours on clocks: men who change with every minute.","654":"The 'stones' are the dishes Timon threw at them (some editions also put stones in the dishes). In this text the covered dishes hold only warm water.","658":"Timon's curse on Athens calls for every social bond to turn into its opposite. Compare Lear's curses on the storm and Ulysses' speech on 'degree' in Troilus and Cressida.","669":"'We have seen better days' was already a proverb. It appears in As You Like It too.","673":"Flavius's 'Undone by goodness' is the most sympathetic view of Timon in the play.","677":"Timon argues that wealth decides worth: raise a beggar and he gets honour, ruin a lord and he gets contempt.","679":"Timon's speech on gold was quoted by Karl Marx as a description of how money turns every quality into its opposite.","685":"'Misanthropos' is Greek for man-hater. Plutarch called the historical Timon by this name.","687":"'Gules' is the heraldic word for red. Timon tells Alcibiades to paint the ground red with blood.","703":"The 'tub' was the sweating treatment for venereal disease. Timon wants the women to spread it.","716":"Timon pays Alcibiades to spare no one: not old men, mothers, virgins or babies. The speech reverses every normal claim to pity.","735":"Timon calls the earth a 'common mother' who breeds both man and poisonous creatures. He asks her to stop breeding men.","740":"Apemantus argues that Timon's misanthropy is a pose forced on him by poverty, not a philosophy he chose.","755":"Timon replies that Apemantus never had anything to lose. His suffering is greater because he fell from the top.","775":"'The middle of humanity thou never knewest, but the extremity of both ends': Apemantus's sharpest judgement of Timon.","779":"A medlar is a fruit eaten when rotten, and also a pun on 'meddler'.","788":"Timon's chain of beasts preying on beasts comes from fables. He shows that every animal is subject to another.","809":"Timon calls gold 'thou visible god', a power that divides fathers from sons and makes enemies kiss.","834":"Timon's speech on universal theft (sun, moon, sea, earth) is a dark version of the order of nature.","851":"Timon's admission of 'one honest man' is his only exception to his hatred of mankind.","867":"The Painter's 'Promising is the very air o' the time' satirises a culture of empty promises.","874":"Timon says gold is worshipped in a temple lower than a pigsty.","932":"Timon answers the senators with their own coldness. He 'cares not' whether Athens falls.","943":"Timon's offer of his tree for the Athenians to hang themselves on comes from Plutarch's story of Timon.","945":"'His everlasting mansion / Upon the beached verge of the salt flood': Timon chooses a grave the sea will cover every day.","973":"Decimation, the Roman punishment of killing one soldier in ten, is offered as a way to limit Alcibiades' revenge.","974":"'Crimes, like lands, / Are not inherited': the senators argue that the innocent should not pay for the guilty.","984":"The epitaph combines two contradictory inscriptions from Plutarch, one asking readers not to seek his name and one naming him. The contradiction may be a sign that the play was left unrevised."},"gloss":{"rarity":"rare or unusual thing","bounty":"generosity","conjur'd":"summoned by magic","incomparable":"beyond comparison","untirable":"tireless","continuate":"lasting","estimate":"value, price","recompense":"payment","presentment":"presentation (to Timon)","rapt":"lost in thought","dedication":"poem dedicated to a patron","poesy":"poetry","confluence":"crowd","amplest":"fullest","levell'd":"aimed","unbolt":"explain","glib":"smooth","properties":"makes his own","tendance":"attendance, service","glass-fac'd":"mirror-faced, reflecting his patron","abhor":"hate","personate":"portray","steepy":"steep","pregnantly":"clearly","talents":"large sums of money","strait":"strict","enfranchis'd":"set free","creature":"servant, dependant","trencher":"wooden plate","counterpoise":"balance","satiety":"excess","unclew":"ruin","chid":"scolded","doit":"tiny coin","feigned":"pretended","traffic":"trade","confound":"destroy","apperil":"risk","churl":"bad-tempered man","humour":"mood","harness":"armour","dich":"do (in 'much good dich')","pelf":"money, possessions","fond":"foolish","gratulate":"greet","plenteous":"plentiful","depraves":"slanders","spurn":"kick","casket":"jewel box","coffer":"money chest","courser":"horse","brace":"pair","coil":"fuss","becks":"bows, nods","sullen":"sulky","importune":"press, beg","fracted":"broken","gull":"unfledged bird; fool","phoenix":"unique mythical bird","bonds":"signed promises to repay","riot":"wild spending","dues":"debts","forfeiture":"penalty for late payment","usurers":"moneylenders","bawd":"pimp","whoremaster":"man who uses prostitutes","husbandry":"management","auditors":"accountants","spilth":"spillage","englutted":"swallowed","broach":"open (a cask)","signet":"seal ring","ingenuously":"frankly","ewer":"water jug","solidares":"small coins","baseness":"meanness","nutriment":"food","disfurnish":"leave without means","politic":"cunning, scheming","wards":"bolts of a lock","prodigal":"spendthrift","muffled":"wrapped up","maws":"stomachs","cashiered":"dismissed","retentive":"confining","gaol":"prison","girdle":"waist","emboldens":"encourages","comely":"fine","misbegot":"born badly","repugnancy":"resistance","felon":"criminal","rioter":"brawler","pawn":"pledge","dotage":"senility","spleen":"anger","importunate":"pressing","summer-birds":"fair-weather friends","parasites":"hangers-on","minute-jacks":"time-servers","physic":"medicine","incontinent":"unchaste","pill":"plunder","sciatica":"pain in the hip and leg","blains":"blisters","livery":"servant's uniform","bark":"ship","flinty":"hard","humidity":"damp","dividant":"separable","lards":"fattens","rother":"ox","grize":"step","semblable":"equal, fellow","votarist":"worshipper","spital-house":"hospital","wappen'd":"worn-out","earnest":"down payment","canker":"cancer, ulcer","Misanthropos":"man-hater","gules":"red (in heraldry)","penurious":"poor","trenchant":"sharp","paps":"breasts","sans":"without","remorse":"pity","oathable":"able to be trusted with an oath","mountant":"lifted up","quillets":"quibbles","flamen":"priest","teems":"breeds","adder":"snake","conceptious":"fertile","liquorish":"sweet; tempting","unctuous":"greasy","carper":"fault-finder","caudle":"warm drink for invalids","surfeit":"overindulgence","caitiff":"wretch","swath":"swaddling clothes","confectionary":"sweet shop","botch'd":"badly patched","medlar":"fruit eaten when rotten","unthrift":"spendthrift","remotion":"keeping away","german":"related","swound":"faint","Hymen":"god of marriage","Dian":"Diana, goddess of chastity","solder'st":"weld","mast":"acorns","hips":"rosehips","composture":"manure","mystery":"trade, craft","requite":"repay","usuring":"moneylending","personating":"portrait","opulency":"wealth","alchemist":"one who tries to turn metal into gold","cauterizing":"burning","recanter":"one who takes back his words","whittle":"knife","epitaph":"words on a gravestone","verge":"edge","insculpture":"inscription","corse":"corpse","niggard":"stingy","leech":"doctor","pursy":"fat, short of breath","decimation":"killing every tenth man","rampir'd":"fortified","ports":"gates","Lacedaemon":"Sparta","Plutus":"god of wealth","Neptune":"god of the sea"}};
const PARTS={"33":[[0,6,"I'll explain. You see how men of every rank and every temper, the smooth and slippery ones as well as the serious and strict, offer their services to Lord Timon. His great fortune, joined to his good and gracious nature, wins over every kind of heart and makes it his to love and serve."],[7,11,"Yes, from the flatterer whose face is a mirror of his patron's, to Apemantus, who loves almost nothing better than hating himself. Even he kneels to Timon, and goes away content, rich in a nod from him."]],"35":[[0,4,"Sir, in my poem I have imagined Fortune sitting on a throne on top of a high and pleasant hill. The bottom of the hill is crowded with people of every merit and every nature who toil on this earth to improve their position."],[5,9,"Among all those whose eyes are fixed on this sovereign lady, I portray one man like Lord Timon, whom Fortune beckons up to her with her ivory hand. Her favour instantly turns his rivals into his slaves and servants."]],"189":[[0,5,"I despise your food. It would choke me, because I would never flatter you to earn it. Oh you gods, how many men are eating Timon, and he doesn't see it! It upsets me to see so many dip their bread in one man's blood, and the maddest part of it is that he encourages them."],[6,12,"I'm amazed that men dare trust themselves with other men. I think they should invite guests without knives: it would be good for the food and safer for their lives. There are plenty of examples. The fellow sitting next to him now, sharing his bread and drinking his health from the same cup, is the man readiest to kill him. It has been proved before."],[13,15,"If I were a great man I'd be afraid to drink at meals, in case they spotted where my windpipe was weakest. Great men should drink with armour on their throats."]],"192":[[0,5,"\"Come this way\"! What a fine fellow! He knows how to catch the tide. Those toasts will make you and your fortune look sick, Timon. Here's something too weak to make anyone sin: honest water, which never left a man in the mud. This and my food are equal partners. Feasts are too proud to thank the gods."],[6,15,"(Saying grace) Immortal gods, I don't ask for money. I pray for nobody but myself. Grant that I may never be so foolish as to trust a man's oath or his signed promise, or a prostitute's tears, or a dog that seems to be asleep, or a jailer with my freedom, or my friends if I should need them. Amen. So, tuck in. Rich men sin, and I eat roots."]],"201":[[0,5,"Oh, I'm sure, my good friends, that the gods themselves have arranged for me to have a lot of help from you. How else would you be my friends? Why else would you, out of thousands, be called friends, unless you belonged above all to my heart? I have said more good of you to myself than you could modestly say of yourselves, and to that extent I'm sure of you."],[6,9,"\"Oh you gods,\" I think, \"what need would we have of any friends if we never needed them? They would be the most useless creatures alive if we never had any use for them. They would be like sweet instruments hung up in their cases, keeping their music to themselves.\""],[10,16,"Why, I have often wished I were poorer so that I could come closer to you. We are born to do good to others, and what can we more truly call our own than our friends' riches? Oh, what a precious comfort it is to have so many friends who, like brothers, can command each other's fortunes! Oh joy, spilling over before it can even be born! My eyes can't hold back their tears. To cover up their weakness, I drink to you."]],"221":[[0,7,"Well, well! What a parade of vanity is coming this way! They're dancing! They're mad women. The glory of this life is just as mad as this display looks next to a meal of a little oil and roots. We make fools of ourselves to entertain ourselves, and waste our flattery toasting men whom, when they're old, we spit back out with poisonous spite and envy."],[8,13,"Who lives who isn't corrupted or corrupting others? Who dies without taking to the grave some kick given by a friend? I'd be afraid that the women dancing in front of me now would one day stamp on me. It has happened before: men shut their doors on a setting sun."]],"254":[[0,5,"(Aside) Where will this end? He orders us to provide feasts and give great gifts, all out of an empty money chest. He won't find out how much is in his purse, or let me show him what a beggar his heart has become, since it has no power to make its wishes come true."],[6,9,"His promises go so far beyond his means that everything he says is in debt. He owes for every word. He is so kind that he now pays interest on his kindness. His lands are mortgaged in his creditors' account books."],[10,14,"Well, I wish I could be gently retired from my job before I'm forced out of it! A man with no friends to feed is happier than one whose friends are worse than enemies. I'm bleeding inside for my lord."]],"281":[[0,3,"And recently five thousand. He owes Varro and Isidore nine thousand, besides the sum I lent him before, which makes it twenty-five thousand. And still he goes on wasting madly! It can't last, it won't."],[4,9,"If I want gold, I need only steal a beggar's dog and give it to Timon, and the dog turns into gold. If I wanted to sell my horse and buy twenty better ones, I need only give my horse to Timon, asking nothing, and straight away it gives birth to fine grown horses for me."],[10,13,"There's no porter at his gate to keep people out, only one who smiles and invites in everyone who passes. It can't last. No reasoning can find his fortune safe. Caphis, hey! Caphis, I say!"]],"284":[[0,4,"Put on your cloak and hurry to Lord Timon. Press him for my money. Don't be put off by a feeble refusal, or silenced when he says \"Give my regards to your master\" while twirling his hat in his right hand, like this."],[5,11,"Tell him my needs are calling for it; I have to serve my own turn out of my own money. His payment dates have passed, and by relying on his broken promises I've damaged my own credit. I love and honour him, but I can't break my own back to heal his finger. My needs are urgent, and my relief mustn't be tossed back to me in words; it must come at once."],[12,17,"Go. Put on a most demanding face, the look of a man asking for what he's owed. I'm afraid that when every borrowed feather is back in its own bird's wing, Lord Timon, who now shines like a phoenix, will be left a naked, plucked fool."]],"380":[[0,3,"Oh, my good lord! Many times I brought in my accounts and laid them in front of you. You would push them away and say you trusted my honesty."],[4,10,"When you ordered me to give back a large sum in return for some trivial gift, I shook my head and wept. Yes, against all proper manners, I begged you to keep a tighter hold on your money. I often had to put up with harsh rebukes when I reminded you of your shrinking fortune and your swelling debts."],[11,13,"My dear lord, though you're listening now, too late, now is the time to hear it: the whole of what you own is not enough to pay half your present debts."]],"400":[[0,5,"They reply, all together in one voice, that just now they're at a low point, short of money, can't do what they would like; they're sorry; you're honourable; but still they could have wished; they don't know; something has gone wrong; a noble nature can slip; they wish all were well; it's a pity."],[6,9,"And so, pretending to have other important business, with sour looks and these broken-off phrases, with half-lifted hats and cold nods, they froze me into silence."]],"401":[[0,6,"May the gods repay them! Please, man, cheer up. These old men have ingratitude in their blood by inheritance. Their blood is clotted and cold; it hardly flows. They aren't kind because they lack natural warmth. And human nature, as it grows back towards the earth, becomes dull and heavy for the journey to the grave."],[7,10,"(To a Servant) Go to Ventidius. (To Flavius) Please, don't be sad. You're loyal and honest. I say it frankly: you're not to blame."],[11,17,"(To the Servant) Ventidius has just buried his father, and by his death he has come into a great fortune. When he was poor, in prison and short of friends, I freed him with five talents. Greet him for me. Tell him to imagine that some real need has touched his friend, which asks to be remembered with those five talents."]],"432":[[0,4,"May these coins be among those that boil you in hell! May molten money be your damnation, you sickness pretending to be a friend! Has friendship such a weak and milky heart that it turns sour in less than two nights?"],[5,8,"Oh you gods! I feel my master's anger. This slave to his own rank still has my lord's food inside him. Why should it do him good and nourish him, when he himself has turned to poison?"],[9,12,"Oh, may it only feed diseases! And when he's sick to death, may the part of his body that my lord paid for have no power to drive out the sickness, but only drag out his dying."]],"453":[[0,4,"What a wicked beast I was to leave myself without money just when I could have shown myself honourable! How unlucky that only the day before I made a purchase that brought me a little profit and lost me a great deal of honour!"],[5,7,"Servilius, now, by the gods, I'm not able to do it. All the more beast me, I say! I was about to send to Lord Timon to borrow from him myself, as these gentlemen can witness. But I wouldn't have done it now for all the wealth of Athens."],[8,12,"Give my warmest regards to his good lordship. I hope his honour will think the best of me, since I have no power to be kind. And tell him this from me: say I count it one of my greatest sorrows that I can't oblige such an honourable gentleman. Good Servilius, will you do me the favour of telling him in my own words?"]],"461":[[0,5,"Why, this is the spirit of the world, and every flatterer's spirit is cut from the same cloth. Who can call a man who eats from the same dish his friend? As far as I know, Timon has been a father to this lord. He kept up his credit with his own purse and supported his estate."],[6,11,"Yes, Timon's money has paid his servants' wages. He never drinks without Timon's silver touching his lips. And yet, oh, see how monstrous man is when he shows his ungrateful side! Compared with what he has, he refuses Timon what charitable men give to beggars."]],"463":[[0,5,"For my own part, I've never enjoyed anything of Timon's in my life. None of his gifts ever came my way to make me his friend. Yet I swear, for his truly noble mind, his shining virtue and his honourable behaviour,"],[6,11,"if his need had turned to me, I would have treated my wealth as a gift and given the better half back to him, I love his heart so much. But I see that men must now learn to do without pity, because self-interest now ranks above conscience."]],"469":[[0,5,"What! Have they refused him? Have Ventidius and Lucullus refused him? And now he sends to me? Three of them? Hmm! It shows very little love or judgement in him. Must I be his last resort? His friends, like doctors, have given him up three times over. Must I take on the cure?"],[6,12,"He has insulted me badly by this. I'm angry with him; he should have known my rank. I can see no reason why his needs shouldn't have come to me first, because, honestly, I was the first man who ever received a gift from him. And does he think so little of me now that I'll be the last to repay it?"],[13,19,"No. That would make me a laughing stock among the others, and I'd be thought a fool among lords. I'd rather he had sent to me first, just for my own sake, than be given three times the sum, I was so eager to help him. But now go back, and add this answer to their feeble replies: whoever lowers my honour won't see my money."]],"471":[[0,6,"Excellent! Your lordship is a fine villain. The devil didn't know what he was doing when he made man crafty; he got in his own way by it. I can't help thinking that in the end man's villainy will make the devil look innocent. How cleverly this lord tries to look foul! He uses virtuous examples to be wicked, like those who, under a show of burning religious zeal, would set whole kingdoms on fire. His crafty love is of that kind."],[7,13,"He was my lord's best hope. Now everyone has run away except the gods. Now that his friends are dead, doors that never knew their bolts for many generous years must be used to guard their master safely. This is all that a generous life leaves him: a man who can't keep his wealth must keep to his house to avoid arrest."]],"513":[[0,6,"Yes. If money were as certain as your waiting, it would be sure enough. Why didn't you hand in your sums and bills when your false masters were eating my lord's food? Then they could smile and fawn on his debts and swallow the interest into their greedy stomachs."],[7,10,"You only harm yourselves by provoking me. Let me pass in peace. Believe me, my lord and I have finished: I have no more to count, and he has no more to spend."]],"564":[[0,6,"I come humbly to appeal to your virtues, because pity is the virtue of the law, and only tyrants use the law cruelly. Time and fortune have chosen to weigh heavily on a friend of mine who, in the heat of anger, has fallen foul of the law, which is too deep for anyone who plunges into it carelessly."],[7,13,"Apart from this misfortune, he is a man of fine qualities. He did not stain the deed with cowardice, an honour that pays for his fault. With noble anger and a fair spirit, seeing his reputation wounded to the death, he stood up to his enemy."],[14,16,"And he kept his anger so controlled and quiet, until it was spent, that it was as if he had only been arguing a point."]],"565":[[0,6,"You are defending too strained a contradiction, trying to make an ugly deed look fair. Your words have worked so hard that it seems they are trying to make manslaughter respectable and to treat quarrelling as a form of courage. That kind of courage is a bastard, born into the world when parties and factions first began."],[7,13,"The truly brave man is one who can wisely bear the worst insults men can speak, and treat his wrongs as outward things, wearing them carelessly like his clothes, never taking his injuries to heart and so putting it in danger. If wrongs are evils that force us to kill, what folly it is to risk our lives for evil!"]],"568":[[0,5,"Then, my lords, with your permission, forgive me if I speak like a soldier. Why do foolish men expose themselves in battle, instead of putting up with all threats, sleeping on them, and letting their enemies quietly cut their throats without resisting?"],[6,11,"If there is such courage in enduring, what are we doing fighting abroad? Then women who stay at home are braver, if enduring wins the prize, and the ass is a better captain than the lion, and the criminal loaded with chains is wiser than the judge, if wisdom lies in suffering."],[12,18,"Oh my lords, since you are great, be good and merciful. Who can't condemn rashness when his own blood is cold? I grant that killing is the most extreme taste of sin, but when it is done in self-defence it is, in mercy, entirely just. Anger is ungodly, but what man is never angry? Just weigh the crime with that in mind."]],"575":[[0,4,"A cruel fate! He could have died in war. My lords, if not for any qualities of his own, though his right arm could buy him the rest of his life and owe nobody anything, then, to move you more, add my services to his and join them together."],[5,10,"And because I know your reverend ages love security, I'll pledge all my victories and all my honour to you that he will repay you well. If he owes the law his life for this crime, let war take it in brave bloodshed, because war is just as strict as law."]],"586":[[0,1,"Now may the gods keep you alive until you are old enough to be nothing but bones, so that no one can bear to look at you!"],[2,7,"I'm worse than mad. I've kept their enemies away while they counted their money and lent it out at high interest, and I myself am rich only in great wounds. All that for this? Is this the healing ointment that the moneylending Senate pours into soldiers' wounds? Banishment!"],[8,13,"It doesn't come badly. I don't mind being banished. It's a cause worthy of my rage and fury, so that I can strike at Athens. I'll cheer up my discontented troops and win their hearts. It's an honour to be at war with most countries. Soldiers should put up with wrongs as little as gods do."]],"632":[[0,3,"Every man go to his seat as eagerly as he would go to kiss his mistress. The food will be the same everywhere. Don't make a formal city banquet of it, letting the food go cold while we argue about who takes the top place. Sit, sit. The gods require our thanks."],[4,8,"(Saying grace) You great benefactors, sprinkle our company with thankfulness. Make yourselves praised for your own gifts, but always keep something back to give, in case your godhead is despised. Lend each man enough so that no one need lend to another, because if you gods had to borrow from men, men would abandon the gods."],[9,14,"Make the food more loved than the man who gives it. Let no gathering of twenty be without a score of villains. If twelve women sit at the table, let a dozen of them be what they are. As for the rest of your enemies, oh gods, the senators of Athens together with the common rabble, whatever is wrong in them, you gods, make it fit for destruction."],[15,17,"As for my present friends here, since they are nothing to me, bless them with nothing, and to nothing they are welcome. Uncover the dishes, dogs, and lap it up."]],"658":[[0,6,"Let me look back at you. Oh you wall that circles those wolves, sink into the earth and stop protecting Athens! Wives, become unchaste! Children, stop obeying! Slaves and fools, drag the grave, wrinkled senators from their bench and rule in their place! Let young virgins turn at once into common whores; do it in front of your parents!"],[7,14,"Bankrupts, hold on to what you have; rather than pay it back, take out your knives and cut your creditors' throats! Bound servants, steal: your grave masters are big-handed robbers who plunder by law. Maid, go to your master's bed; your mistress is in the brothel! Son of sixteen, snatch the padded crutch from your old limping father and beat his brains out with it!"],[15,20,"Piety and fear, reverence for the gods, peace, justice, truth, respect within the home, night's rest and neighbourliness, teaching, manners, crafts and trades, ranks, ceremonies, customs and laws, collapse into your destructive opposites, and let chaos live!"],[21,27,"Plagues that afflict men, pile your powerful, infectious fevers on Athens, which is ripe to be struck down! Cold sciatica, cripple our senators, so that their limbs limp as lamely as their morals! Let lust and licence creep into the minds and bones of our young men, so that they swim against the stream of virtue and drown in debauchery!"],[28,33,"Itches and blisters, be sown in every Athenian breast, and let the harvest be leprosy for all! Let breath infect breath, so that their company, like their friendship, is nothing but poison! I'll take nothing from you but my nakedness, you hateful town! Take that too, with curses multiplied!"],[34,40,"Timon will go to the woods, where he'll find the cruellest beast kinder than mankind. May the gods destroy (hear me, all you good gods) the Athenians both inside and outside that wall! And grant that, as Timon grows older, his hatred may grow towards the whole human race, high and low! Amen."]],"673":[[0,6,"Oh, the terrible misery that greatness brings us! Who wouldn't wish to be free of wealth, since riches lead to misery and contempt? Who would want to be fooled by glory like this, or to live only in a dream of friendship, to have his splendour and everything that makes up high rank only painted on, like his varnished friends?"],[7,11,"Poor honest lord! Brought low by his own heart, ruined by goodness. What a strange, unnatural temperament, when a man's worst sin is doing too much good! Who will dare to be half so kind again? Generosity, which makes gods, still ruins men."],[12,18,"My dearest lord, blessed only to be cursed the more, rich only to be wretched, your great fortune has become your chief affliction. Alas, kind lord! He has rushed off in a rage from this ungrateful home of monstrous friends, and he has nothing with him to keep himself alive, or money to buy it."],[19,21,"I'll follow and find out where he is. I'll always serve his wishes with all my goodwill. As long as I have gold, I'll still be his steward."]],"677":[[0,7,"Oh blessed, life-giving sun, draw rotten damp up from the earth and infect the air below the moon! Take twin brothers from one womb, whose conception, life in the womb and birth can hardly be told apart, and give them different fortunes: the greater will scorn the lesser. Human nature, besieged by every ill, can't bear great fortune without despising its own kind."],[8,10,"Raise this beggar up and take that lord's wealth away: the senator will be born to contempt, the beggar to honour."],[11,19,"It's the pasture that fattens the ox's sides, and the lack of it that makes him thin. Who dares, who dares to stand up straight in pure manliness and say \"This man is a flatterer\"? If one is, they all are, because every step on fortune's stair is smoothed by the one below. The learned head bows to the golden fool. Everything is crooked. There's nothing straight in our cursed natures except outright villainy."],[20,22,"So let all feasts, gatherings and crowds of men be hated! Timon despises his fellow man, yes, even himself. May destruction seize mankind in its teeth! Earth, give me roots!"]],"679":[[0,5,"Whoever wants something better from you, may you season his taste with your strongest poison! What's this? Gold! Yellow, glittering, precious gold! No, gods, I'm not a half-hearted worshipper: I asked for roots, you clear heavens! This much of it will make black white, foul fair, wrong right, low noble, old young, coward brave."],[6,13,"Ha, you gods! Why this? What's this for, you gods? Why, this will drag your priests and servants away from your side and pull pillows from under the heads of strong men who are still alive. This yellow slave will tie religions together and break them apart, bless the cursed, make white leprosy adored, place thieves among senators on the bench and give them titles, bowing and approval."],[14,20,"This is what makes the worn-out widow marry again. Her, whom a hospital full of ulcerous patients would vomit at the sight of, this perfumes and spices back to the freshness of an April day. Come, damned earth, you common whore of mankind who sets nations quarrelling, I will make you do what your nature is. (Marching heard in the distance)"],[21,24,"Ha! A drum? You're lively, but I'll bury you all the same. You'll walk about, strong thief, when your gouty keepers can't stand. No, stay out here as a down payment."]],"716":[[0,5,"Because you were born to conquer my country by killing villains. Put away your gold. Go on; here's gold; go on. Be like a plague sent by the planets, when Jove hangs his poison in the sick air over some city deep in vice. Don't let your sword skip a single person."],[6,13,"Don't pity old age for its white beard; he's a moneylender. Strike the respectable-looking matron; only her clothes are honest, she herself is a bawd. Don't let the virgin's cheek soften your sharp sword, because those milky breasts that peep at men through the window bars aren't written on pity's page. Count them as dreadful traitors."],[14,17,"Don't spare the baby, whose dimpled smiles drain fools of their mercy. Think of it as a bastard whom the oracle has darkly prophesied will cut your throat, and chop it up without remorse."],[18,23,"Swear yourself against all pitiful sights. Put armour on your ears and eyes so strong that neither the screams of mothers, girls and babies, nor the sight of priests bleeding in their holy robes, can pierce it at all. Here's gold to pay your soldiers. Cause great destruction, and when your fury is spent, may you be destroyed yourself! Don't speak. Go."]],"720":[[0,6,"Enough to make a whore give up her trade, and to make whores into bawds. Hold up your aprons, you sluts. You can't be trusted to swear an oath, though I know you'll swear, swear terribly enough to make the immortal gods who hear you shudder and shake with fever. Spare your oaths; I'll trust your natures."],[7,10,"Stay whores. And if some preacher's pious breath tries to convert you, be strong in whoring, seduce him and burn him up. Let your secret fire overpower his smoky sermon, and don't change sides."],[11,15,"Yet for six months of the year may your efforts go the other way, suffering the pox yourselves. Thatch your thin, balding heads with wigs taken from the dead, some of them hanged men, no matter; wear them and deceive with them. Stay whores. Paint your faces so thick that a horse could get stuck in the mud on them. A pox on wrinkles!"]],"722":[[0,6,"Sow wasting diseases in men's hollow bones. Strike their sharp shins and stop them riding. Crack the lawyer's voice so that he can never again plead a false claim or squeal out his quibbles. Make the priest hoarse, who preaches against the flesh and doesn't believe himself."],[7,13,"Bring down the nose, flatten it, take the bridge right off the nose of the man who sniffs out his own private gain and ignores the common good. Make curly-haired thugs bald, and let the boastful soldiers who come home unscarred from war get some pain from you. Infect everyone, so that your work may destroy and kill the source of all erection."],[14,15,"Here's more gold. You damn others, and let this damn you, and may ditches be your graves!"]],"735":[[0,6,"whose immeasurable womb and endless breast give birth to everything and feed everything, whose same material, from which your proud child, arrogant man, is puffed up, also breeds the black toad and the blue adder, the golden newt and the blind poisonous snake, with all the hateful creatures under the bright sky that the sun's life-giving fire shines on,"],[7,8,"give the man who hates all your human sons one poor root from your plentiful breast!"],[9,14,"Dry up your fertile, conceiving womb. Let it never again produce ungrateful man! Be pregnant with tigers, dragons, wolves and bears. Breed new monsters that your upturned face has never shown to the marble palace of heaven above! Oh, a root! Thank you dearly."],[15,18,"Dry up your rich juices, your vines and ploughed fields, from which ungrateful man, with his sweet drinks and greasy food, smears over his clear mind so that all good sense slips out of it!"]],"740":[[0,6,"In you this is only an infected nature, a feeble, unmanly gloom that comes from your change of fortune. Why this spade, this place, these slave's clothes, these worried looks? Your flatterers still wear silk, drink wine, sleep soft, hug their diseased perfumed women, and have forgotten that Timon ever existed."],[7,12,"Don't shame these woods by putting on the airs of a fault-finder. Be a flatterer yourself now, and try to prosper by the very thing that has ruined you. Bend your knee as if it had a hinge, and let the mere breath of the man you court blow your cap off. Praise his worst vice and call it excellent."],[13,16,"That's how they spoke to you. You lent your ears, like innkeepers welcoming everyone, to rogues and anyone who came along. It's only right that you should turn rascal. If you had wealth again, rascals would get it. Don't try to look like me."]],"742":[[0,1,"You threw yourself away by being like yourself: a madman for so long, now a fool."],[2,7,"What, do you think the bitter wind, your rough servant, will warm your shirt for you? Will these mossy trees, older than the eagle, follow at your heels like pages and jump when you point? Will the cold stream, iced over like sugar, make you a warm drink in the morning to cure last night's excess?"],[8,12,"Call the creatures whose naked bodies live through all the spite of avenging heaven, whose bare, homeless bodies are exposed to the warring elements and live by nature alone. Tell them to flatter you. Oh, you'll find..."]],"754":[[0,3,"If you had put on this harsh, cold way of life to punish your pride, it would be good. But you do it because you're forced to. You'd be a courtier again if you weren't a beggar."],[4,9,"Poverty that is chosen outlives uncertain splendour and wins its crown sooner. One is always filling up and never full; the other has all it wishes. The best position, without contentment, is a troubled and wretched existence, worse than the worst position with contentment. Since you're miserable, you ought to want to die."]],"755":[[0,2,"Not on the advice of someone more miserable than me. You're a slave whom Fortune never held lovingly in her arms, but bred as a dog."],[3,10,"If you had gone, as I did from my baby clothes, through all the sweet stages this short life offers to those who can freely command its willing pleasures, you'd have thrown yourself into general excess, melted your youth away in different beds of lust, and never learned the icy rules of caution, but chased the sugary prey in front of you."],[11,18,"But I, who had the world as my sweet shop, with the mouths, tongues, eyes and hearts of men at my service, more than I could find work for, who had them sticking to me in countless numbers as leaves do to the oak, have seen them fall from the branches at one brush of winter, leaving me open and bare to every storm that blows. For me, who never knew anything but better, it's a heavy burden to bear."],[19,25,"Your life began in suffering, and time has hardened you to it. Why should you hate men? They never flattered you. What have you ever given? If you want to curse, it must be your father, that poor rag, who out of spite lay with some beggar woman and made you a born poor rogue. Get out! Go!"],[26,27,"If you hadn't been born the lowest of men, you'd have been a rogue and a flatterer."]],"788":[[0,5,"A beastly ambition, and may the gods let you achieve it. If you were the lion, the fox would trick you. If you were the lamb, the fox would eat you. If you were the fox, the lion would suspect you whenever, perhaps, the ass accused you. If you were the ass, your stupidity would torment you, and you'd still live only as the wolf's breakfast."],[6,12,"If you were the wolf, your greed would plague you, and you'd often risk your life for your dinner. If you were the unicorn, pride and rage would ruin you and make you the victim of your own fury. If you were a bear, the horse would kill you. If you were a horse, the leopard would seize you. If you were a leopard, you'd be a cousin of the lion, and your relatives' spots would be the jury that condemned you."],[13,15,"Your only safety would be distance, and your only defence absence. What beast could you be that wasn't prey to another beast? And what a beast you are already, not to see how much you'd lose by the change!"]],"809":[[0,5,"Oh you sweet killer of kings, and dear divider of a natural son from his father! You bright defiler of the purest marriage bed! You brave Mars! You ever young, fresh, loved and delicate suitor, whose blush melts the holy snow that lies in Diana's lap!"],[6,11,"You visible god, who welds together things that could never meet and makes them kiss! You who speak every language for every purpose! Oh you touchstone of hearts! Imagine that man, your slave, is rebelling, and by your power set men fighting among themselves until they destroy each other, so that the beasts may rule the world!"]],"834":[[0,4,"Nor on the beasts themselves, the birds and fish. You have to eat men. Still, I must thank you for being open, professional thieves and not working in holier disguises, because there is boundless theft in the respectable professions."],[5,11,"Rascal thieves, here's gold. Go, suck the subtle blood of the grape until the high fever boils your blood to froth, and so escape hanging. Don't trust the doctor: his medicines are poison, and he kills more men than you rob. Take wealth and lives together. Do villainy, since you profess to do it, like true craftsmen."],[12,18,"I'll give you examples of thieving. The sun is a thief, and with his great pull he robs the vast sea. The moon is an outright thief, and snatches her pale fire from the sun. The sea is a thief, whose liquid waves dissolve the moon into salt tears. The earth is a thief, which feeds and breeds on manure stolen from everything's excrement. Everything is a thief."],[19,26,"The laws, your bridle and your whip, commit unchecked theft with their rough power. Don't love yourselves. Go! Rob one another. Here's more gold. Cut throats. Everyone you meet is a thief. Go to Athens, break open shops. You can steal nothing that a thief doesn't lose. Don't steal any less because of what I give you, and may gold destroy you anyway! Amen."]],"841":[[0,7,"Oh you gods! Is that despised, ruined man over there my lord? So wasted and failing? Oh, monument and wonder of good deeds badly placed! What a change in an honourable man desperate poverty has made! What on earth is viler than friends who can bring the noblest minds to the lowest ends!"],[8,11,"How well this suits the fashion of our times, when man was told to love his enemies! Grant that I may always love, and rather court those who openly mean me harm than those who do me harm while pretending friendship!"],[12,14,"He has seen me. I'll show him my honest grief and still serve him as my lord with my life. My dearest master!"]],"851":[[0,4,"Did I have a steward so loyal, so honest, and now such a comfort? It almost turns my savage nature mild. Let me look at your face. Surely this man was born of woman."],[5,11,"Forgive my sweeping, exceptionless rashness, you eternally calm gods! I declare one honest man (don't mistake me, only one, no more, I beg you) and he is a steward. How gladly I would have hated all mankind! And you save yourself from it. But everyone except you I strike down with curses."],[12,16,"I think you're more honest than wise now, because by oppressing and betraying me you could have found another job sooner. Many men climb to a second master over their first lord's neck."],[17,20,"But tell me truly, for I must always doubt, however sure I am: isn't your kindness cunning and greedy, a moneylender's kindness, like rich men giving gifts and expecting twenty in return for one?"]],"852":[[0,3,"No, my most worthy master, in whose heart doubt and suspicion have, alas, been placed too late! You should have feared false times while you were feasting. Suspicion always comes when wealth is at its lowest."],[4,11,"What I show, heaven knows, is pure love, duty and devotion to your unmatched mind, and care for your food and livelihood. And believe me, my most honoured lord, I would exchange any benefit to me, now or in the future, for this one wish: that you had the power and wealth to repay me by making yourself rich."]],"853":[[0,2,"Look, it is so! You single honest man, here, take this. The gods have sent you treasure out of my misery. Go, live rich and happy,"],[3,10,"but on this condition: you shall build your house far from men. Hate everyone, curse everyone, show charity to no one, but let starving flesh slide off the bone before you help a beggar. Give to dogs what you refuse to men. Let prisons swallow them and debts wither them to nothing. Let men be like blighted woods, and may diseases lick up their false blood! And so, goodbye, and prosper."]],"879":[[0,5,"Sir, having often enjoyed your open generosity, and hearing that you had withdrawn and that your friends had deserted you, whose ungrateful natures (oh, hateful spirits! not all the whips of heaven are big enough) what, to you,"],[6,9,"whose starlike nobility gave life and power to their whole being! I'm overcome and cannot cover the monstrous size of this ingratitude with words of any size."]],"927":[[0,6,"They confess they forgot you too widely and grossly. Now the state, which seldom takes back what it has done, feeling the lack of Timon's help, also recognises its own failure in withholding help from Timon. It has sent us to express its sorrow,"],[7,12,"together with a reward richer than their offence could outweigh by even a grain: yes, such heaps and sums of love and wealth as will wipe out the wrongs they did you and write their love in you in figures you can always read as yours."]],"932":[[0,3,"Well, sir, I will. Therefore I will, sir, like this. If Alcibiades kills my countrymen, let Alcibiades know this from Timon: that Timon doesn't care."],[4,11,"But if he sacks fair Athens, and takes our fine old men by their beards, and gives our holy virgins up to the stain of insulting, beastly, mad-brained war, then let him know, and tell him Timon says it, that out of pity for our old and our young I cannot help but tell him I don't care. Let him do his worst. Their knives don't care, as long as you have throats to meet them."],[12,16,"As for me, there isn't a pocket-knife in that unruly camp that I don't value more than the most respected throat in Athens. So I leave you to the protection of the kindly gods, as thieves are left to their jailers."]],"945":[[0,5,"Don't come to me again, but tell Athens that Timon has made his everlasting home on the beach at the edge of the salt sea, which once a day the stormy waves will cover with their foam. Come there, and let my gravestone be your oracle."],[6,9,"Lips, let bitter words pass and let speech end. Whatever is wrong, let plague and infection put it right! Let graves be men's only work, and death their only profit! Sun, hide your beams! Timon's reign is over."]],"963":[[0,3,"By every description this should be the place. Who's here? Hello, speak! No answer! What's this? Timon is dead; he has lived out his span. Some beast built this; no man lives here."],[4,9,"Dead, certainly, and this is his grave. I can't read what's written on this tomb. I'll take an impression of the letters in wax. Our captain can read every kind of writing; he's an old interpreter, though young in years. By now he'll have laid siege to proud Athens, whose fall is the target of his ambition."]],"969":[[0,5,"Until now you have gone on filling your time with every kind of licence, making your own wishes the limit of justice. Until now, I and those who lived in the shadow of your power have wandered with folded arms and breathed out our sufferings in vain."],[6,10,"Now the time is ripe, when the crouching spirit, grown strong in the one who bears it, cries out of its own accord \"No more!\" Now exhausted wrong shall sit panting in your great easy chairs, and fat insolence shall lose its breath in fear and frantic flight."]],"973":[[0,4,"And the men who first caused you to leave are no longer alive. Shame at their lack of foresight has broken their hearts. March into our city, noble lord, with your banners flying."],[5,9,"If your revenge hungers for the food that nature hates, then kill one in every ten, take the tenth man fate assigns you, and by the chance of the spotted dice let the spotted, the guilty, die."]],"974":[[0,3,"Not everyone has offended. It isn't fair to take revenge on those who are alive for the sake of those who are gone. Crimes, unlike lands, aren't inherited."],[4,9,"So, dear countryman, bring in your soldiers but leave your anger outside. Spare your Athenian birthplace and those relatives who, in the storm of your anger, would fall along with the guilty. Like a shepherd, go into the fold and pick out the infected sheep, but don't kill them all together."]],"978":[[0,4,"Then there's my glove. Come down and open your gates, which haven't been attacked. Only those enemies of Timon's and of mine whom you yourselves pick out for punishment shall die, and no more."],[5,9,"And to settle your fears with my nobler intentions, not one of my men shall leave his quarters or break the course of ordinary justice within your city's bounds without being handed over to your public laws for the heaviest punishment."]],"984":[[0,3,"(Reads the epitaph) \"Here lies a wretched body, robbed of its wretched soul. Do not look for my name. May a plague destroy you wicked wretches who are left! Here I lie, Timon, who hated all living men while I was alive. Pass by and curse as much as you like, but pass by, and do not stop here.\""],[4,11,"These words express well your feelings at the end. Though you hated our human sorrows and scorned the tears that fall in small drops from stingy nature, your rich imagination taught you to make vast Neptune weep forever on your humble grave for faults forgiven. Noble Timon is dead, and of his memory we shall say more later."],[12,15,"Take me into your city, and I will use the olive branch together with my sword. I will make war produce peace, and peace limit war, and make each the doctor of the other. Let our drums strike up."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"money":["Gold and money","Debts, loans, talents and Timon's 'visible god', gold."],"friendship":["Friendship and generosity","Gifts, feasts and what friends owe each other."],"flattery":["Flattery and art","Poets, painters and lords who praise for profit."],"ingratitude":["Ingratitude","Friends and a city that forget what they were given."],"hate":["Misanthropy and curses","Timon's hatred of mankind and his great curses."],"beasts":["Beasts and nature","Dogs, wolves, roots and the wild against the city."],"justice":["Law, war and Athens","The Senate, mercy, banishment and revenge."],"fortune":["Fortune and change","Fortune's hill, rise and fall, prosperity and ruin."]},"quotes":[{"id":"6","who":"POET","text":"Magic of bounty! all these spirits thy power Hath conjur'd to attend!","themes":["friendship","flattery"],"note":"Timon's wealth draws a crowd like a spell."},{"id":"10","who":"MERCHANT","text":"breath'd, as it were, To an untirable and continuate goodness.","themes":["friendship"],"note":"The Merchant's praise of Timon's endless goodness."},{"id":"14","who":"POET","text":"When we for recompense have prais'd the vile, It stains the glory in that happy verse Which aptly sings the good.","themes":["flattery"],"note":"The Poet admits that bought praise is shameful."},{"id":"26","who":"POET","text":"It tutors nature: artificial strife Lives in these touches, livelier than life.","themes":["flattery"],"note":"Art praised as better than nature."},{"id":"33","who":"POET","text":"his large fortune, Upon his good and gracious nature hanging, Subdues and properties to his love and tendance All sorts of hearts;","themes":["friendship","money"],"note":"Timon's fortune wins every kind of follower."},{"id":"35","who":"POET","text":"Feign'd Fortune to be thron'd: the base o' the mount Is rank'd with all deserts,","themes":["fortune"],"note":"The allegory of Fortune's hill."},{"id":"39","who":"POET","text":"When Fortune in her shift and change of mood Spurns down her late beloved,","themes":["fortune","ingratitude"],"note":"The Poet's poem predicts Timon's fall."},{"id":"39","who":"POET","text":"Not one accompanying his declining foot.","themes":["fortune","ingratitude"],"note":"Followers desert a falling man."},{"id":"40","who":"PAINTER","text":"show Lord Timon that mean eyes have seen The foot above the head.","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Fortune turns the great upside down."},{"id":"44","who":"TIMON","text":"I am not of that feather to shake off My friend when he must need me.","themes":["friendship"],"note":"Timon's idea of friendship, which no one returns."},{"id":"46","who":"TIMON","text":"'Tis not enough to help the feeble up, But to support him after.","themes":["friendship"],"note":"Timon's generous creed."},{"id":"61","who":"OLD ATHENIAN","text":"His honesty rewards him in itself; It must not bear my daughter.","themes":["money"],"note":"The Old Athenian values wealth over honesty."},{"id":"69","who":"TIMON","text":"To build his fortune I will strain a little, For 'tis a bond in men.","themes":["friendship","money"],"note":"Timon sees generosity as a bond between men."},{"id":"77","who":"TIMON","text":"The painting is almost the natural man; For since dishonour traffics with man's nature, He is but outside:","themes":["flattery"],"note":"Timon already sees that men are all surface."},{"id":"82","who":"JEWELLER","text":"You mend the jewel by the wearing it.","themes":["flattery"],"note":"The Jeweller's flattery."},{"id":"90","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"When thou art Timon's dog, and these knaves honest.","themes":["beasts","hate"],"note":"Apemantus's first insult."},{"id":"98","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"Of nothing so much as that I am not like Timon.","themes":["hate"],"note":"Apemantus defines himself against Timon."},{"id":"110","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"No; I eat not lords.","themes":["beasts"],"note":"Apemantus's joke about eating and lords."},{"id":"116","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"Not so well as plain dealing, which will not cost a man a doit.","themes":["flattery","money"],"note":"Honesty is free; jewels are not."},{"id":"128","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"he that loves to be flattered is worthy o' the flatterer.","themes":["flattery"],"note":"Apemantus's key judgement on Timon."},{"id":"138","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"Traffic's thy god, and thy god confound thee!","themes":["money"],"note":"Trade as a false god."},{"id":"148","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"The strain of man's bred out Into baboon and monkey.","themes":["beasts","flattery"],"note":"Courtesy turns men into apes."},{"id":"154","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"Time to be honest.","themes":["flattery"],"note":"Apemantus answers a simple question with a moral."},{"id":"166","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"I will fly, like a dog, the heels of an ass.","themes":["beasts"],"note":"Apemantus the dog."},{"id":"169","who":"SECOND LORD","text":"Plutus, the god of gold, Is but his steward:","themes":["money","friendship"],"note":"Timon's wealth seems endless."},{"id":"169","who":"SECOND LORD","text":"no gift to him But breeds the giver a return exceeding All use of quittance.","themes":["money","friendship"],"note":"Every gift to Timon is repaid many times."},{"id":"177","who":"TIMON","text":"there's none Can truly say he gives, if he receives:","themes":["friendship"],"note":"A true gift expects nothing back."},{"id":"177","who":"TIMON","text":"faults that are rich are fair.","themes":["money"],"note":"Rich men's faults are excused."},{"id":"180","who":"TIMON","text":"ceremony was but devis'd at first To set a gloss on faint deeds, hollow welcomes,","themes":["flattery","friendship"],"note":"Timon rejects ceremony in favour of true friendship."},{"id":"180","who":"TIMON","text":"But where there is true friendship there needs none.","themes":["friendship"],"note":"Timon's trust in his guests."},{"id":"186","who":"TIMON","text":"Ira furor brevis est; But yond man is ever angry.","themes":["hate"],"note":"Anger as madness, a foretaste of Timon's own."},{"id":"189","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"O you gods! what a number Of men eats Timon, and he sees 'em not!","themes":["friendship","beasts"],"note":"The guests devour their host."},{"id":"189","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"It grieves me to see so many dip their meat In one man's blood;","themes":["ingratitude"],"note":"Feasting pictured as feeding on Timon."},{"id":"189","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"Great men should drink with harness on their throats.","themes":["ingratitude"],"note":"Guests may kill the man they toast."},{"id":"192","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"Feasts are too proud to give thanks to the gods.","themes":["friendship"],"note":"Apemantus attacks the feast."},{"id":"198","who":"ALCIBIADES","text":"So they were bleeding-new, my lord, there's no meat like 'em:","themes":["justice","beasts"],"note":"Alcibiades the soldier relishes enemies."},{"id":"201","who":"TIMON","text":"what need we have any friends if we should ne'er have need of 'em?","themes":["friendship"],"note":"Timon's belief that friends must be used."},{"id":"201","who":"TIMON","text":"We are born to do benefits; and what better or properer can we call our own than the riches of our friends?","themes":["friendship","money"],"note":"Timon's ideal of shared wealth."},{"id":"201","who":"TIMON","text":"O! what a precious comfort 'tis to have so many, like brothers, commanding one another's fortunes!","themes":["friendship"],"note":"The dream of friendship as brotherhood."},{"id":"202","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"Thou weepest to make them drink, Timon.","themes":["flattery"],"note":"Apemantus mocks Timon's tears."},{"id":"204","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"Ho, ho! I laugh to think that babe a bastard.","themes":["flattery"],"note":"Apemantus says the lords' tears are false."},{"id":"221","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"Like madness is the glory of this life,","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Splendour is a kind of madness."},{"id":"221","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"Who lives that's not depraved or depraves?","themes":["hate"],"note":"Apemantus on universal corruption."},{"id":"221","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"Men shut their doors against a setting sun.","themes":["fortune","ingratitude"],"note":"The proverb that sums up the play."},{"id":"232","who":"FLAVIUS","text":"'Tis pity bounty had not eyes behind, That man might ne'er be wretched for his mind.","themes":["friendship","money"],"note":"Flavius on generosity's blindness."},{"id":"254","who":"FLAVIUS","text":"He commands us to provide, and give great gifts, And all out of an empty coffer;","themes":["money"],"note":"Flavius reveals Timon's emptiness."},{"id":"254","who":"FLAVIUS","text":"His promises fly so beyond his state That what he speaks is all in debt; he owes For every word:","themes":["money"],"note":"Every word is bought on credit."},{"id":"254","who":"FLAVIUS","text":"Happier he that has no friend to feed Than such that do e'en enemies exceed.","themes":["friendship","ingratitude"],"note":"False friends are worse than enemies."},{"id":"263","who":"TIMON","text":"Methinks, I could deal kingdoms to my friends, And ne'er be weary.","themes":["friendship"],"note":"Timon's boundless giving."},{"id":"272","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"Serving of becks and jutting out of bums!","themes":["flattery"],"note":"Apemantus mocks the lords' bows."},{"id":"272","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"Methinks, false hearts should never have sound legs.","themes":["flattery"],"note":"Apemantus on bowing flatterers."},{"id":"274","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"I fear me thou wilt give away thyself in paper shortly:","themes":["money"],"note":"Apemantus foresees Timon's ruin by IOUs."},{"id":"277","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"O! that men's ears should be To counsel deaf, but not to flattery!","themes":["flattery"],"note":"Apemantus's parting couplet."},{"id":"281","who":"SENATOR","text":"If I want gold, steal but a beggar's dog And give it Timon, why, the dog coins gold;","themes":["money"],"note":"Gifts to Timon always pay."},{"id":"281","who":"SENATOR","text":"It cannot hold; no reason Can found his state in safety.","themes":["money","fortune"],"note":"The senator sees the end coming."},{"id":"284","who":"SENATOR","text":"I love and honour him, But must not break my back to heal his finger;","themes":["ingratitude","money"],"note":"Friendly words, selfish action."},{"id":"284","who":"SENATOR","text":"Lord Timon will be left a naked gull, Which flashes now a phoenix.","themes":["fortune","beasts"],"note":"Timon's borrowed splendour."},{"id":"292","who":"FLAVIUS","text":"never mind Was to be so unwise, to be so kind.","themes":["friendship"],"note":"Flavius sums up Timon."},{"id":"292","who":"FLAVIUS","text":"He will not hear, till feel:","themes":["money"],"note":"Timon will only learn by suffering."},{"id":"336","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"Poor rogues and usurers' men! bawds between gold and want!","themes":["money"],"note":"Apemantus on the creditors' servants."},{"id":"380","who":"FLAVIUS","text":"The greatest of your having lacks a half To pay your present debts.","themes":["money"],"note":"The truth Timon hears too late."},{"id":"384","who":"FLAVIUS","text":"O my good lord! the world is but a word; Were it all yours to give it in a breath, How quickly were it gone!","themes":["money","fortune"],"note":"Flavius on how fast wealth goes."},{"id":"386","who":"FLAVIUS","text":"I have retir'd me to a wasteful cock, And set mine eyes at flow.","themes":["friendship"],"note":"Flavius's private tears."},{"id":"388","who":"FLAVIUS","text":"Ah! when the means are gone that buy this praise, The breath is gone whereof this praise is made:","themes":["flattery","money"],"note":"Praise is bought and ends with the money."},{"id":"388","who":"FLAVIUS","text":"Feast-won, fast-lost; one cloud of winter showers, These flies are couch'd.","themes":["flattery","fortune"],"note":"Fair-weather friends."},{"id":"389","who":"TIMON","text":"Unwisely, not ignobly, have I given.","themes":["friendship"],"note":"Timon's own verdict."},{"id":"389","who":"TIMON","text":"Men and men's fortunes could I frankly use As I can bid thee speak.","themes":["friendship"],"note":"Timon's confidence in his friends."},{"id":"391","who":"TIMON","text":"I am wealthy in my friends.","themes":["friendship"],"note":"Timon's belief before the test."},{"id":"401","who":"TIMON","text":"These old fellows Have their ingratitude in them hereditary;","themes":["ingratitude"],"note":"Timon blames age for coldness."},{"id":"401","who":"TIMON","text":"'Tis lack of kindly warmth they are not kind;","themes":["ingratitude"],"note":"A pun on kind (generous) and kind (natural)."},{"id":"404","who":"TIMON","text":"Ne'er speak, or think That Timon's fortunes 'mong his friends can sink.","themes":["friendship"],"note":"Timon's trust, about to be destroyed."},{"id":"405","who":"FLAVIUS","text":"That thought is bounty's foe; Being free itself, it thinks all others so.","themes":["friendship"],"note":"Generosity assumes everyone is generous."},{"id":"420","who":"LUCULLUS","text":"Every man has his fault, and honesty is his.","themes":["ingratitude","flattery"],"note":"Lucullus calls Timon's honesty a fault."},{"id":"427","who":"LUCULLUS","text":"this is no time to lend money, especially upon bare friendship without security.","themes":["money","ingratitude"],"note":"Lucullus's refusal."},{"id":"427","who":"LUCULLUS","text":"good boy, wink at me, and say thou sawest me not.","themes":["ingratitude"],"note":"Lucullus's bribe."},{"id":"428","who":"FLAMINIUS","text":"Is't possible the world should so much differ, And we alive that liv'd?","themes":["ingratitude","fortune"],"note":"Flaminius is shocked by the change."},{"id":"428","who":"FLAMINIUS","text":"Fly, damned baseness, To him that worships thee.","themes":["money"],"note":"Flaminius throws the coins back."},{"id":"432","who":"FLAMINIUS","text":"Let molten coin be thy damnation,","themes":["money","hate"],"note":"Flaminius curses Lucullus."},{"id":"432","who":"FLAMINIUS","text":"Has friendship such a faint and milky heart It turns in less than two nights?","themes":["friendship","ingratitude"],"note":"Friendship goes sour like milk."},{"id":"442","who":"LUCIUS","text":"had he mistook him, and sent to me, I should ne'er have denied his occasion so many talents.","themes":["ingratitude"],"note":"Lucius's boast, about to be tested."},{"id":"453","who":"LUCIUS","text":"What a wicked beast was I to disfurnish myself against such a good time, when I might ha' shown myself honourable!","themes":["ingratitude"],"note":"Lucius's hollow excuse."},{"id":"457","who":"LUCIUS","text":"True, as you said, Timon is shrunk indeed; And he that's once denied will hardly speed.","themes":["ingratitude","fortune"],"note":"Lucius's cold couplet."},{"id":"461","who":"FIRST STRANGER","text":"Why, this is the world's soul; and just of the same piece Is every flatterer's spirit.","themes":["flattery","ingratitude"],"note":"The Stranger generalises."},{"id":"461","who":"FIRST STRANGER","text":"Who can call him His friend that dips in the same dish?","themes":["friendship","ingratitude"],"note":"An echo of Judas."},{"id":"461","who":"FIRST STRANGER","text":"And yet, O! see the monstrousness of man, When he looks out in an ungrateful shape,","themes":["ingratitude","hate"],"note":"Ingratitude as monstrous."},{"id":"462","who":"THIRD STRANGER","text":"Religion groans at it.","themes":["ingratitude"],"note":"Even religion is shocked."},{"id":"463","who":"FIRST STRANGER","text":"Men must learn now with pity to dispense; For policy sits above conscience.","themes":["ingratitude"],"note":"Self-interest rules."},{"id":"469","who":"SEMPRONIUS","text":"Must I be his last refuge? His friends, like physicians, Thrice give him over;","themes":["ingratitude"],"note":"Sempronius's excuse."},{"id":"469","who":"SEMPRONIUS","text":"Who bates mine honour shall not know my coin.","themes":["ingratitude","money"],"note":"Sempronius hides meanness behind honour."},{"id":"471","who":"SERVANT","text":"The devil knew not what he did when he made man politic; he crossed himself by't:","themes":["hate","ingratitude"],"note":"Man outdoes the devil."},{"id":"471","who":"SERVANT","text":"Who cannot keep his wealth must keep his house.","themes":["money","fortune"],"note":"Timon must hide from his creditors."},{"id":"489","who":"LUCIUS' SERVANT","text":"'Tis deepest winter in Lord Timon's purse;","themes":["money","fortune"],"note":"Winter in the purse."},{"id":"496","who":"HORTENSIUS","text":"And now ingratitude makes it worse than stealth.","themes":["ingratitude"],"note":"Even a creditor's servant sees the shame."},{"id":"513","who":"FLAVIUS","text":"If money were as certain as your waiting, 'Twere sure enough.","themes":["money"],"note":"Flavius's bitter wit."},{"id":"527","who":"TIMON","text":"What! are my doors oppos'd against my passage?","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Timon trapped in his own house."},{"id":"527","who":"TIMON","text":"The place which I have feasted, does it now, Like all mankind, show me an iron heart?","themes":["ingratitude","hate"],"note":"The beginning of Timon's hatred."},{"id":"534","who":"TIMON","text":"Knock me down with 'em: cleave me to the girdle.","themes":["money"],"note":"Bills as weapons."},{"id":"536","who":"TIMON","text":"Cut my heart in sums.","themes":["money"],"note":"Timon's body as payment."},{"id":"538","who":"TIMON","text":"Tell out my blood.","themes":["money"],"note":"Counting blood like coins."},{"id":"554","who":"TIMON","text":"I'll once more feast the rascals.","themes":["friendship","hate"],"note":"Timon plans the mock banquet."},{"id":"559","who":"FIRST SENATOR","text":"Nothing emboldens sin so much as mercy.","themes":["justice"],"note":"The senator's harsh law."},{"id":"564","who":"ALCIBIADES","text":"For pity is the virtue of the law, And none but tyrants use it cruelly.","themes":["justice"],"note":"Alcibiades pleads for mercy."},{"id":"567","who":"FIRST SENATOR","text":"To revenge is no valour, but to bear.","themes":["justice"],"note":"The senator's Stoic answer."},{"id":"568","who":"ALCIBIADES","text":"Who cannot condemn rashness in cold blood?","themes":["justice"],"note":"Alcibiades on judging in calm."},{"id":"576","who":"FIRST SENATOR","text":"He forfeits his own blood that spills another.","themes":["justice"],"note":"The law of blood for blood."},{"id":"582","who":"FIRST SENATOR","text":"We banish thee for ever.","themes":["justice","ingratitude"],"note":"Athens rewards Alcibiades with exile."},{"id":"583","who":"ALCIBIADES","text":"Banish your dotage; banish usury, That makes the Senate ugly.","themes":["justice","money"],"note":"Alcibiades attacks the moneylending Senate."},{"id":"586","who":"ALCIBIADES","text":"Is this the balsam that the usuring senate Pours into captains' wounds?","themes":["justice","ingratitude"],"note":"Wounds repaid with banishment."},{"id":"586","who":"ALCIBIADES","text":"Soldiers should brook as little wrongs as gods.","themes":["justice"],"note":"Alcibiades chooses revenge."},{"id":"605","who":"SECOND LORD","text":"The swallow follows not summer more willing than we your lordship.","themes":["flattery","fortune"],"note":"The lords as summer birds."},{"id":"606","who":"TIMON","text":"Nor more willingly leaves winter; such summer-birds are men.","themes":["flattery","fortune"],"note":"Timon sees through them."},{"id":"632","who":"TIMON","text":"Lend to each man enough, that one need not lend to another;","themes":["money","hate"],"note":"Timon's mock grace."},{"id":"632","who":"TIMON","text":"For these my present friends, as they are to me nothing, so in nothing bless them, and to nothing are they welcome.","themes":["hate","friendship"],"note":"The curse disguised as a blessing."},{"id":"632","who":"TIMON","text":"Uncover, dogs, and lap.","themes":["beasts","hate"],"note":"The guests become dogs."},{"id":"636","who":"TIMON","text":"You knot of mouth-friends! smoke and lukewarm water Is your perfection.","themes":["friendship","flattery"],"note":"The dishes picture the friends."},{"id":"638","who":"TIMON","text":"Most smiling, smooth, detested parasites, Courteous destroyers, affable wolves, meek bears,","themes":["flattery","beasts","hate"],"note":"Timon's torrent of insults."},{"id":"638","who":"TIMON","text":"You fools of fortune, trencher-friends, time's flies, Cap and knee slaves, vapours, and minute-jacks!","themes":["flattery","fortune"],"note":"Fair-weather friends named."},{"id":"640","who":"TIMON","text":"Burn, house! sink Athens! henceforth hated be Of Timon man and all humanity!","themes":["hate"],"note":"Timon becomes Misanthropos."},{"id":"654","who":"FOURTH LORD","text":"One day he gives us diamonds, next day stones.","themes":["fortune"],"note":"The lords' glib couplet."},{"id":"658","who":"TIMON","text":"O thou wall, That girdles in those wolves, dive in the earth, And fence not Athens!","themes":["hate","beasts"],"note":"Timon's curse on the city."},{"id":"658","who":"TIMON","text":"And let confusion live!","themes":["hate","justice"],"note":"Disorder wished on Athens."},{"id":"658","who":"TIMON","text":"Nothing I'll bear from thee But nakedness, thou detestable town!","themes":["hate"],"note":"Timon strips himself of Athens."},{"id":"658","who":"TIMON","text":"Timon will to the woods; where he shall find Th' unkindest beast more kinder than mankind.","themes":["hate","beasts"],"note":"From city to wild."},{"id":"658","who":"TIMON","text":"his hate may grow To the whole race of mankind, high and low!","themes":["hate"],"note":"Hatred of all humanity."},{"id":"668","who":"THIRD SERVANT","text":"Yet do our hearts wear Timon's livery,","themes":["friendship"],"note":"The servants' loyalty."},{"id":"669","who":"FLAVIUS","text":"'We have seen better days.'","themes":["fortune","friendship"],"note":"The servants' farewell."},{"id":"673","who":"FLAVIUS","text":"O! the fierce wretchedness that glory brings us.","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Flavius on greatness."},{"id":"673","who":"FLAVIUS","text":"Undone by goodness. Strange, unusual blood, When man's worst sin is he does too much good!","themes":["friendship"],"note":"Flavius's verdict on Timon."},{"id":"673","who":"FLAVIUS","text":"For bounty, that makes gods, does still mar men.","themes":["friendship","money"],"note":"Generosity ruins men."},{"id":"677","who":"TIMON","text":"There's nothing level in our cursed natures But direct villainy.","themes":["hate"],"note":"All human nature is crooked."},{"id":"677","who":"TIMON","text":"Destruction fang mankind! Earth, yield me roots!","themes":["hate","beasts"],"note":"Timon turns to the earth."},{"id":"679","who":"TIMON","text":"Gold! yellow, glittering, precious gold!","themes":["money"],"note":"The great speech on gold begins."},{"id":"679","who":"TIMON","text":"Thus much of this will make black white, foul fair, Wrong right, base noble, old young, coward valiant.","themes":["money"],"note":"Gold turns everything into its opposite."},{"id":"679","who":"TIMON","text":"This yellow slave Will knit and break religions; bless the accurs'd,","themes":["money"],"note":"Gold corrupts religion."},{"id":"679","who":"TIMON","text":"Thou common whore of mankind, that putt'st odds Among the rout of nations,","themes":["money","hate"],"note":"Gold as a whore."},{"id":"685","who":"TIMON","text":"I am Misanthropos, and hate mankind.","themes":["hate"],"note":"Timon names himself."},{"id":"685","who":"TIMON","text":"For thy part, I do wish thou wert a dog, That I might love thee something.","themes":["hate","beasts"],"note":"Beasts above men."},{"id":"691","who":"TIMON","text":"As the moon does, by wanting light to give:","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Timon's fall like the waning moon."},{"id":"695","who":"TIMON","text":"Promise me friendship, but perform none:","themes":["friendship","hate"],"note":"Timon's twisted request."},{"id":"716","who":"TIMON","text":"Be as a planetary plague, when Jove Will o'er some high-vic'd city hang his poison In the sick air:","themes":["hate","justice"],"note":"Timon urges Alcibiades on."},{"id":"716","who":"TIMON","text":"Put armour on thine ears and on thine eyes,","themes":["hate","justice"],"note":"No pity for anyone."},{"id":"728","who":"TIMON","text":"Yes, thou spok'st well of me.","themes":["flattery"],"note":"Praise as harm."},{"id":"735","who":"TIMON","text":"Ensear thy fertile and conceptious womb, Let it no more bring out ingrateful man!","themes":["hate","beasts"],"note":"Timon asks nature to stop breeding man."},{"id":"740","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"This is in thee a nature but infected; A poor unmanly melancholy sprung From change of fortune.","themes":["hate","fortune"],"note":"Apemantus's charge against Timon."},{"id":"740","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"Do not assume my likeness.","themes":["hate"],"note":"Apemantus resents his imitator."},{"id":"742","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"A madman so long, now a fool.","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Apemantus's summary of Timon's life."},{"id":"747","who":"TIMON","text":"Thou flatter'st misery.","themes":["flattery","hate"],"note":"Even misery can be flattered."},{"id":"754","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"Willing misery Outlives incertain pomp,","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Chosen poverty outlasts wealth."},{"id":"755","who":"TIMON","text":"Thou art a slave, whom Fortune's tender arm With favour never clasp'd, but bred a dog.","themes":["fortune","beasts"],"note":"Timon's reply to Apemantus."},{"id":"755","who":"TIMON","text":"Who had the world as my confectionary,","themes":["money","fortune"],"note":"Timon's lost sweetness."},{"id":"755","who":"TIMON","text":"That numberless upon me stuck as leaves Do on the oak, have with one winter's brush Fell from their boughs,","themes":["fortune","ingratitude"],"note":"Friends fall like leaves."},{"id":"768","who":"TIMON","text":"For here it sleeps and does no hired harm.","themes":["money"],"note":"Gold is harmless where no one uses it."},{"id":"775","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"The middle of humanity thou never knewest, but the extremity of both ends.","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Apemantus on Timon's extremes."},{"id":"779","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"What man didst thou ever know unthrift that was beloved after his means?","themes":["money","friendship"],"note":"Love ends with money."},{"id":"784","who":"TIMON","text":"Women nearest; but men, men are the things themselves.","themes":["flattery","hate"],"note":"Men are flattery itself."},{"id":"788","who":"TIMON","text":"What beast couldst thou be that were not subject to a beast?","themes":["beasts"],"note":"Nature as a chain of prey."},{"id":"789","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"the commonwealth of Athens is become a forest of beasts.","themes":["beasts","justice"],"note":"Athens as a wild forest."},{"id":"792","who":"TIMON","text":"I had rather be a beggar's dog than Apemantus.","themes":["beasts","hate"],"note":"Insult contest."},{"id":"807","who":"TIMON","text":"I am sick of this false world, and will love nought But even the mere necessities upon't.","themes":["hate"],"note":"Timon renounces the world."},{"id":"809","who":"TIMON","text":"O thou sweet king-killer, and dear divorce 'Twixt natural son and sire!","themes":["money"],"note":"Gold divides families."},{"id":"809","who":"TIMON","text":"thou visible god, That solder'st close impossibilities, And mak'st them kiss!","themes":["money"],"note":"Gold as a god."},{"id":"814","who":"APEMANTUS","text":"Live, and love thy misery!","themes":["hate"],"note":"Apemantus's farewell."},{"id":"834","who":"TIMON","text":"for there is boundless theft In limited professions.","themes":["money","justice"],"note":"Respectable trades are theft."},{"id":"834","who":"TIMON","text":"The sun's a thief, and with his great attraction Robs the vast sea;","themes":["money","beasts"],"note":"Nature itself steals."},{"id":"834","who":"TIMON","text":"each thing's a thief;","themes":["money","hate"],"note":"Universal theft."},{"id":"838","who":"FIRST BANDIT","text":"There is no time so miserable but a man may be true.","themes":["friendship"],"note":"The bandit's turn to honesty."},{"id":"841","who":"FLAVIUS","text":"What viler thing upon the earth than friends Who can bring noblest minds to basest ends!","themes":["ingratitude","friendship"],"note":"Flavius on false friends."},{"id":"849","who":"TIMON","text":"Strange times, that weep with laughing, not with weeping!","themes":["hate"],"note":"Timon on a tearless world."},{"id":"851","who":"TIMON","text":"I do proclaim One honest man, mistake me not, but one;","themes":["hate","friendship"],"note":"Timon's one exception."},{"id":"851","who":"TIMON","text":"How fain would I have hated all mankind!","themes":["hate"],"note":"Flavius spoils Timon's perfect hate."},{"id":"852","who":"FLAVIUS","text":"You should have fear'd false times when you did feast;","themes":["friendship","fortune"],"note":"Flavius's gentle rebuke."},{"id":"853","who":"TIMON","text":"Hate all, curse all, show charity to none,","themes":["hate"],"note":"Timon's terms for Flavius's gift."},{"id":"867","who":"PAINTER","text":"Promising is the very air o' the time;","themes":["flattery"],"note":"The age of empty promises."},{"id":"867","who":"PAINTER","text":"To promise is most courtly and fashionable;","themes":["flattery"],"note":"The Painter's cynicism."},{"id":"869","who":"TIMON","text":"Thou canst not paint a man so bad as is thyself.","themes":["flattery","hate"],"note":"Timon on the Painter."},{"id":"874","who":"TIMON","text":"What a god's gold, That he is worshipp'd in a baser temple Than where swine feed!","themes":["money","beasts"],"note":"Gold worshipped in a pigsty."},{"id":"880","who":"TIMON","text":"Let it go naked: men may see't the better.","themes":["flattery"],"note":"Timon wants truth unclothed."},{"id":"913","who":"TIMON","text":"You are an alchemist; make gold of that.","themes":["money","flattery"],"note":"Timon pays the Poet with blows."},{"id":"918","who":"SECOND SENATOR","text":"Men are not still the same; 'twas time and griefs That fram'd him thus.","themes":["fortune"],"note":"The senator on Timon's change."},{"id":"921","who":"TIMON","text":"Thou sun that comfort'st, burn! Speak and be hang'd!","themes":["hate"],"note":"Timon greets the senators."},{"id":"932","who":"TIMON","text":"There's not a whittle in the unruly camp But I do prize it at my love before The reverend'st throat in Athens.","themes":["hate","justice"],"note":"Timon's contempt for Athens."},{"id":"934","who":"TIMON","text":"My long sickness Of health and living now begins to mend, And nothing brings me all things.","themes":["hate","fortune"],"note":"Timon welcomes death."},{"id":"943","who":"TIMON","text":"Come hither, ere my tree hath felt the axe, And hang himself.","themes":["hate"],"note":"Timon's last 'kindness' to Athens."},{"id":"945","who":"TIMON","text":"Timon hath made his everlasting mansion Upon the beached verge of the salt flood,","themes":["hate","beasts"],"note":"Timon's grave by the sea."},{"id":"945","who":"TIMON","text":"Graves only be men's works and death their gain!","themes":["hate"],"note":"Timon's last curse."},{"id":"945","who":"TIMON","text":"Sun, hide thy beams! Timon hath done his reign.","themes":["fortune"],"note":"Timon's farewell."},{"id":"959","who":"THIRD SENATOR","text":"Ours is the fall, I fear; our foes the snare.","themes":["justice","fortune"],"note":"Athens fears ruin."},{"id":"963","who":"SOLDIER","text":"Some beast rear'd this; here does not live a man.","themes":["beasts","hate"],"note":"The soldier at the tomb."},{"id":"969","who":"ALCIBIADES","text":"Now breathless wrong Shall sit and pant in your great chairs of ease,","themes":["justice"],"note":"Alcibiades turns the tables."},{"id":"974","who":"FIRST SENATOR","text":"crimes, like lands, Are not inherited.","themes":["justice"],"note":"The senators' plea."},{"id":"974","who":"FIRST SENATOR","text":"Approach the fold and cull th' infected forth, But kill not all together.","themes":["justice","beasts"],"note":"Punish only the guilty."},{"id":"978","who":"ALCIBIADES","text":"Those enemies of Timon's and mine own, Whom you yourselves shall set out for reproof, Fall, and no more.","themes":["justice"],"note":"Alcibiades limits his revenge."},{"id":"984","who":"ALCIBIADES","text":"Here lie I, Timon, who alive all living men did hate.","themes":["hate"],"note":"The epitaph."},{"id":"984","who":"ALCIBIADES","text":"And I will use the olive with my sword; Make war breed peace, make peace stint war,","themes":["justice"],"note":"Peace made by force."}]};
const EX={"intro":["Timon is the richest and most generous lord in Athens. He pays his friends' debts, buys every poem and painting he is offered, and fills his house with feasts and gifts. His steward Flavius knows the money has run out; the philosopher Apemantus warns him that his guests are flatterers. Timon will not listen.","When his creditors demand payment, Timon sends to the friends he has enriched, sure they will help. Every one of them refuses. Timon invites them to a last banquet, serves them warm water, throws it in their faces and leaves Athens cursing all mankind. Meanwhile the captain Alcibiades is banished for pleading for a friend, and turns his army against the city.","In the woods Timon digs for roots and finds gold. He gives it to Alcibiades to destroy Athens, to prostitutes to spread disease and to thieves to keep stealing. Only his steward's loyalty makes him admit one honest man. When the Senate begs him to save the city he refuses, and he dies alone by the sea. Alcibiades enters Athens, reads Timon's bitter epitaph and promises peace."],"keyfacts":[["Full title","The Life of Timon of Athens"],["Written","About 1605 to 1606, probably with Thomas Middleton; first printed in the First Folio of 1623"],["Genre","Tragedy (in the First Folio's Tragedies); also a satire and a moral fable about wealth and ingratitude"],["Setting","Athens and the woods near the sea, in ancient Greece"],["Protagonist","Timon"],["Antagonists","Timon's false friends (Lucullus, Lucius, Sempronius, Ventidius), the Senate, and in the end mankind itself"],["Inciting incident","The creditors call in Timon's debts (2.1 to 2.2)"],["Turning point","The mock banquet of warm water, where Timon turns on his friends (3.6)"],["Climax","Timon refuses the Senate's plea to save Athens and prepares his grave (5.1)"],["Time span","A short time in Athens, then an unspecified time while Alcibiades raises his army"],["Major conflicts","Generosity against ingratitude; Timon against his friends; Alcibiades against the Senate; Timon against mankind"],["Themes","Gold and money, friendship and generosity, flattery and art, ingratitude, misanthropy, beasts and nature, law and war, fortune"]],"timeline":[{"s":"a1s1","t":"The Poet describes Fortune's hill","id":35},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Timon pays to free Ventidius","id":44},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Timon makes Lucilius's marriage possible","id":69},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Apemantus insults the flatterers","id":128},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Timon refuses Ventidius's repayment","id":177},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Apemantus says grace over roots","id":192},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Timon's speech on friendship","id":201},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Flavius reveals the coffers are empty","id":254},{"s":"a2s1","t":"A senator sends Caphis to collect","id":284},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Creditors confront Timon","id":307},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Flavius shows Timon he is ruined","id":380},{"s":"a2s2","t":"Timon sends to his friends for loans","id":394},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Lucullus offers Flaminius a bribe","id":427},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Lucius refuses Servilius","id":453},{"s":"a3s3","t":"Sempronius refuses on a point of honour","id":469},{"s":"a3s4","t":"Timon is besieged by creditors","id":536},{"s":"a3s4","t":"Timon orders a final feast","id":554},{"s":"a3s5","t":"Alcibiades pleads for a soldier","id":564},{"s":"a3s5","t":"The Senate banishes Alcibiades","id":582},{"s":"a3s6","t":"The mock banquet of warm water","id":636},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Timon curses Athens and leaves","id":658},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Flavius shares his money with the servants","id":669},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Timon digs and finds gold","id":679},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Timon pays Alcibiades to destroy Athens","id":716},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Apemantus and Timon trade insults","id":755},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Timon gives gold to the bandits","id":834},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Timon admits Flavius is honest","id":851},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Timon refuses to save Athens","id":932},{"s":"a5s3","t":"A soldier finds Timon's tomb","id":963},{"s":"a5s4","t":"Alcibiades reads the epitaph and enters Athens","id":984}],"irony":{"35":"The Poet's allegory describes exactly what will happen to Timon, and Timon buys the poem.","44":"Timon will not shake off a friend in need; every friend he has will shake him off.","176":"Ventidius offers to repay now that he is rich; when Timon is poor he refuses to help.","200":"The First Lord wishes Timon would use their hearts; when he does, they refuse.","201":"Timon asks what need we have of friends if we never need them. He will soon find out.","221":"Apemantus predicts that the dancers will one day stamp on Timon.","391":"'I am wealthy in my friends': Timon's confidence comes just before every friend fails him.","404":"Timon tells Flavius never to think his fortunes can sink among his friends.","414":"Lucullus dreams of a silver basin and ewer as a gift, while Timon is asking him for money.","442":"Lucius says he would never have refused Timon, minutes before he does exactly that.","449":"Lucius jokes that Timon cannot be short of money; Timon has nothing.","469":"Sempronius claims to be hurt that Timon did not come to him first.","591":"The lords think Timon was only testing them, and come back expecting a feast.","605":"The Second Lord compares himself to a swallow following summer, not seeing that he left in winter.","629":"'Will't hold?' The lords wonder if Timon's wealth will last, unaware of what is in the dishes.","929":"The Senate offers Timon absolute power, as he once offered his friends everything.","978":"Alcibiades promises to punish Timon's enemies; Timon wanted all Athens punished."},"devices":{"eating":"Eating, feasts and devouring","beasts":"Beasts and dogs","gold":"Gold, debts and money","weather":"Seasons, sun and weather","sea":"Sea, tides and flood","theft":"Theft and disease"},"dev":{"6":[["gold","Bounty as a spell that conjures followers.","Magic of bounty!"]],"35":[["weather","Fortune's hill as a picture of rising and falling.","Feign'd Fortune to be thron'd"]],"148":[["beasts","Men degenerated into apes.","Into baboon and monkey."]],"169":[["gold","Timon richer than the god of wealth.","Plutus, the god of gold"]],"189":[["eating","Guests dipping bread in their host's blood.","dip their meat In one man's blood"]],"192":[["sea","Water as honest, never leaving a man in the mire.","Honest water, which ne'er left man i' the mire:"]],"201":[["eating","Friends as instruments hung up in their cases.","sweet instruments hung up in cases"]],"221":[["weather","The setting sun of Timon's fortune.","Men shut their doors against a setting sun."]],"254":[["gold","Every word Timon speaks is in debt.","he owes For every word"]],"284":[["beasts","Timon as a phoenix that will turn into a plucked gull.","a naked gull, Which flashes now a phoenix"]],"292":[["sea","Timon's spending as a flood that will not stop.","Nor cease his flow of riot"]],"386":[["sea","Wine spilt, tears flowing.","when our vaults have wept With drunken spilth of wine"]],"388":[["weather","Flatterers as flies that hide at the first winter shower.","one cloud of winter showers, These flies are couch'd."]],"432":[["eating","Timon's food feeding disease in Lucullus.","Has my lord's meat in him:"]],"461":[["eating","The false friend who shares the dish.","dips in the same dish"]],"489":[["weather","Winter in Timon's purse.","'Tis deepest winter in Lord Timon's purse;"]],"536":[["gold","Timon's heart cut up into sums.","Cut my heart in sums."]],"605":[["weather","Friends as swallows that follow summer.","The swallow follows not summer more willing"]],"632":[["beasts","The guests as dogs lapping water.","Uncover, dogs, and lap."]],"638":[["beasts","Friends as wolves and bears.","affable wolves, meek bears"]],"658":[["beasts","The senators and citizens as wolves within the wall.","That girdles in those wolves"],["theft","Plagues and fevers called down on the city.","Plagues incident to men"]],"668":[["sea","Timon's house as a sinking ship.","Leak'd is our bark"]],"677":[["beasts","The pasture fattens the ox, poverty makes it lean.","It is the pasture lards the rother's sides"]],"679":[["gold","Gold as a slave and a whore.","This yellow slave"]],"735":[["beasts","The earth as a mother breeding monsters.","Go great with tigers, dragons, wolves, and bears;"]],"755":[["weather","Friends falling like leaves in a winter wind.","with one winter's brush Fell from their boughs"]],"788":[["beasts","The chain of beasts preying on beasts.","If thou wert the lion, the fox would beguile thee"]],"809":[["gold","Gold as a visible god.","thou visible god"]],"834":[["theft","Sun, moon, sea and earth as thieves.","The sun's a thief"],["sea","The sea dissolving the moon into tears.","resolves The moon into salt tears"]],"874":[["gold","Gold worshipped in a pigsty.","worshipp'd in a baser temple Than where swine feed!"]],"945":[["sea","Timon's grave washed daily by the sea.","Upon the beached verge of the salt flood"]],"974":[["beasts","Alcibiades as a shepherd culling infected sheep.","Like a shepherd Approach the fold"]]},"stage":{"hall":"The great hall of Timon's house in Athens","banquet":"Timon's banqueting room, set for a feast","house":"Before Timon's house, where creditors wait","room":"A room in a lord's house in Athens","street":"A public place in Athens","senate":"The Senate House of Athens","walls":"The walls and gates of Athens","cave":"Timon's cave in the woods near the sea","tomb":"Timon's rough tomb at the edge of the sea"},"stagerules":[["banqueting","banquet"],["hall in timon","hall"],["before timon's house","house"],["senate","senate"],["walls","walls"],["tomb","tomb"],["cave","cave"],["room","room"],["public","street"]],"place":{"a1s1":"Timon's hall","a1s2":"Timon's banqueting room","a2s1":"A senator's house","a2s2":"Before Timon's house","a3s1":"Lucullus's house","a3s2":"A public place","a3s3":"Sempronius's house","a3s4":"Timon's hall","a3s5":"The Senate House","a3s6":"Timon's banqueting room","a4s1":"Outside the walls of Athens","a4s2":"Timon's empty house","a4s3":"The woods, before Timon's cave","a5s1":"The woods, before Timon's cave","a5s2":"Before the walls of Athens","a5s3":"Timon's tomb by the sea","a5s4":"Before the walls of Athens"},"arc":{"Timon's wealth":[10,10,8,4,3,2,1,0,0,0,0,0,6,5,5,0,0],"Timon's hatred":[0,0,0,1,1,2,3,6,6,9,10,10,9,10,10,10,10]},"arcnote":"Timon's wealth seems limitless in Act 1, but Flavius's asides show it is already borrowed. It collapses in Act 2 when the debts are called in, and each refusal in Act 3 drains what is left of his credit. His hatred is zero while he is rich; it starts when his own doors are barred against him (3.4) and peaks at the mock banquet and the curse on Athens. In the woods he finds gold again, but wealth no longer brings love: he uses it as a weapon, and his hatred dips only once, for Flavius. At the end he has neither wealth nor life, and his epitaph keeps the hatred alive.","iago":[["a1s1","Timon pays five talents to free Ventidius and funds Lucilius's marriage."],["a1s2","Timon refuses repayment and showers his guests with gifts from an empty coffer."],["a1s2","Flavius tries to warn Timon and is put off."],["a2s1","A senator decides Timon's wealth cannot last and calls in his debt."],["a2s2","Creditors' servants confront Timon at his gate."],["a2s2","Flavius shows that every acre is mortgaged and the debts are double the estate."],["a2s2","Timon sends to Lucius, Lucullus, Sempronius, Ventidius and the Senate for loans."],["a3s1","Lucullus refuses and offers a bribe."],["a3s2","Lucius refuses with false excuses."],["a3s3","Sempronius refuses on a pretended point of honour."],["a3s4","Timon is trapped in his own house by creditors and decides on a last feast."],["a3s6","Timon serves warm water, drives out his friends and swears to hate mankind."],["a4s1","Timon curses Athens and leaves for the woods."],["a4s3","Timon finds gold and uses it to fund destruction."],["a4s3","Timon names himself Misanthropos and rejects Apemantus."],["a5s1","Timon refuses to save Athens and offers his tree for hanging."],["a5s1","Timon prepares his grave by the sea."],["a5s4","Timon's epitaph is read: he died hating all men."]],"rel":{"1":[["TIMON","VENTIDIUS","love"],["TIMON","POET","trust"],["POET","TIMON","deceive"],["APEMANTUS","TIMON","hate"],["TIMON","ALCIBIADES","love"],["LUCILIUS","TIMON","love"],["FIRST LORD","TIMON","deceive"],["FLAVIUS","TIMON","love"]],"2":[["TIMON","FLAVIUS","trust"],["FLAVIUS","TIMON","love"],["SENATOR","TIMON","hate"],["CAPHIS","TIMON","hate"],["TIMON","LUCIUS","trust"],["TIMON","LUCULLUS","trust"],["TIMON","SEMPRONIUS","trust"],["APEMANTUS","CAPHIS","hate"]],"3":[["LUCULLUS","TIMON","deceive"],["FLAMINIUS","LUCULLUS","hate"],["LUCIUS","TIMON","deceive"],["SEMPRONIUS","TIMON","deceive"],["FLAMINIUS","TIMON","love"],["SERVILIUS","TIMON","love"],["ALCIBIADES","FIRST SENATOR","hate"],["TIMON","FIRST LORD","hate"]],"4":[["TIMON","ALCIBIADES","trust"],["TIMON","APEMANTUS","hate"],["APEMANTUS","TIMON","hate"],["TIMON","TIMANDRA","hate"],["TIMON","FIRST BANDIT","hate"],["FLAVIUS","TIMON","love"],["TIMON","FLAVIUS","trust"]],"5":[["POET","TIMON","deceive"],["TIMON","POET","hate"],["TIMON","FIRST SENATOR","hate"],["FIRST SENATOR","TIMON","deceive"],["ALCIBIADES","FIRST SENATOR","hate"],["ALCIBIADES","TIMON","love"]]},"context":[["Date","Probably written around 1605 to 1606, between King Lear and Macbeth by most reckonings. There is no record of a performance in Shakespeare's lifetime, and it was first printed in the First Folio of 1623."],["Authorship","Most scholars now agree that Thomas Middleton wrote a large part of the play, including the banquet of 1.2 and much of Act 3 (the friends' refusals and the creditors' servants). The changes in style, verse and spelling of names between sections support this."],["An unfinished play?","The text has loose ends: sums of money that do not add up, characters who are named and then vanish, a soldier who cannot read but takes a wax copy, and two contradictory epitaphs. Many editors think the play was never finished or revised for the stage."],["The Folio","Timon appears in the Tragedies section, in the space between Romeo and Juliet and Julius Caesar. That space was probably meant for Troilus and Cressida, which was delayed by a rights problem; Timon, shorter, was put in its place."],["Plutarch","Shakespeare found Timon in a short digression in Plutarch's Life of Antony, which tells of Timon the man-hater, his fig tree for hanging, and his grave by the sea with its epitaphs. Plutarch's Life of Alcibiades supplied the general who turns against Athens."],["Lucian","The Greek satirist Lucian wrote a dialogue, Timon the Misanthrope, in which Timon digs, finds gold and drives away the flatterers who return. The play shares this plot, though scholars disagree on whether Shakespeare read it directly."],["Athens","The play's Athens is not historical so much as a picture of a rich commercial city. Alcibiades was a real Athenian general of the fifth century BC who was exiled and returned."],["Money and credit","Jacobean London ran on credit. Courtiers borrowed huge sums to keep up appearances, and King James's lavish gifts to favourites were a scandal. Timon's debts and bonds would have looked very familiar."],["Usury","Lending money at interest was legal in England from 1571 but still condemned by moralists. The play's senators and creditors are all lenders, and Alcibiades' attack on 'usury' would have landed."],["Patronage","Poets and painters depended on rich patrons. The Poet and Painter who flatter Timon, then promise work they never deliver, satirise that world from the inside."],["Cynics","Apemantus is modelled on the Cynic philosophers of ancient Greece, such as Diogenes, who rejected wealth and manners and were called 'dogs'."],["Reputation","Long seen as a failure or a fragment, Timon has been revived in the twentieth and twenty-first centuries as a play about money, debt and financial crisis."]],"critics":{"money":[["Karl Marx, 'Economic and Philosophic Manuscripts' (1844)","Paraphrased: Marx quoted Timon's speech on gold to show how money turns every human quality into its opposite and binds people only by what they can buy."],["Coppélia Kahn, essay on Timon and patronage (1987)","Paraphrased: Kahn read the play as a picture of a gift economy collapsing into a credit economy, with Timon trying to buy love he cannot afford."]],"friendship":[["G. Wilson Knight, 'The Wheel of Fire' (1930)","Paraphrased: Knight saw Timon as an idealist whose love for mankind is so great that its betrayal turns into an equally great hatred."],["Rolf Soellner, 'Timon of Athens: Shakespeare's Pessimistic Tragedy' (1979)","Paraphrased: Soellner argued the play is Shakespeare's darkest view of human nature, offering no real hope beyond Flavius's loyalty."]],"hate":[["William Hazlitt, 'Characters of Shakespear's Plays' (1817)","Paraphrased: Hazlitt praised the play's intensity, finding Timon's misanthropy written with a force Shakespeare never surpassed."],["Samuel Johnson, notes on the play (1765)","Paraphrased: Johnson found it a domestic tragedy with a clear moral, a warning against ostentatious liberality that buys flattery rather than friendship."]],"flattery":[["John Jowett, introduction to the Oxford edition (2004)","Paraphrased: Jowett emphasised the satirical side of the play and Middleton's part in it, seeing the artists and lords as targets of a city comedy."]],"fortune":[["Una Ellis-Fermor, 'Timon of Athens: An Unfinished Play' (1942)","Paraphrased: Ellis-Fermor argued that the play's gaps and contradictions show it was left as a draft, which explains why its tragic shape feels incomplete."]],"justice":[["A. D. Nuttall, 'Timon of Athens' (1989)","Paraphrased: Nuttall stressed the parallel between Timon and Alcibiades, two men wronged by Athens, one who withdraws into curses and one who returns with an army."]]},"symbols":{"Gold":"\\bgold\\w*","Friend":"\\bfriends?\\w*","Dog":"\\bdogs?\\b","Feast":"\\bfeast\\w*|\\bbanquet\\w*","Plague":"\\bplagues?\\b|\\bcurse\\w*","Beast":"\\bbeasts?\\b|\\bwolf\\b|\\bwolves\\b"},"symbols2":[["Gold","First Timon's endless gifts, then the gold he digs up in the woods and turns into a weapon against mankind."],["The banquets","The lavish feast of 1.2 and the mock feast of warm water in 3.6 frame Timon's fall."],["Roots","Apemantus eats roots at the feast; Timon digs for roots and finds gold instead. Roots stand for the bare needs of nature."],["Fortune's hill","The Poet's allegory in 1.1 is the play's map: the climb, the followers and the fall."],["The cave and the sea","Timon's cave and his grave on the shore, covered daily by the tide, show his withdrawal from mankind."],["The epitaph","Timon's last words, carried to Athens in wax: hatred fixed in stone."]],"solo":{"292":1,"432":1,"471":1,"586":1,"658":1,"673":1,"677":1,"841":1},"why":["Sets up Timon's bounty as a kind of magic that summons followers.","Shows how Athens talks about Timon before he appears: as goodness itself.","The Poet admits the problem with paid praise, then goes on praising.","Art praised above nature: the flattery the play keeps exposing.","Wealth and good nature together win every heart, for now.","The allegory that predicts the whole plot.","Fortune's mood swing is the play's structure in one sentence.","The followers' desertion, foretold in Act 1.","Even the Painter sees the moral; no one applies it to Timon.","Timon's loyalty to Ventidius is the standard his friends will fail.","His creed of lasting help, which no one shows him.","Money valued above honesty from the very first scene.","Timon's generosity framed as a 'bond in men'.","Timon prefers honest paintings to dishonest men: a seed of misanthropy.","A flatterer's line, exposed by Timon's 'Well mock'd'.","Apemantus attacks everyone at once, including Timon.","Apemantus defines himself by not being Timon; by Act 4 they are alike.","Eating and lords: the play's image of guests devouring the host.","Honesty costs nothing; jewels cost a fortune.","The core of Apemantus's case against Timon.","Trade worshipped as a god, anticipating Timon's 'visible god', gold.","Courtesy as apish imitation: man degenerating into beast.","A one-line moral from the play's railing philosopher.","Apemantus the 'dog' turns the insult back on his attackers.","Timon seems richer than the god of wealth.","Every gift to Timon pays back sevenfold, which is why he is surrounded.","Timon's ideal of the gift without return.","Rich men's faults are called fair: the play's satire of wealth.","Timon wants friendship without empty ceremony.","His trust in true friendship, about to be tested.","Anger as madness: a warning Timon will not heed.","The key image of the guests eating Timon.","Feasting pictured as feeding on a man's blood.","Guests may become murderers: a cynical warning.","Apemantus attacks the pride of the feast.","Alcibiades the soldier is at home only in blood.","Timon's creed: friends exist to be used.","Timon's dream of wealth shared among friends.","Friendship pictured as brotherhood, never to be realised.","Apemantus sees Timon's tears as a ploy.","The lords' feelings are false offspring.","Splendour is a kind of madness.","Apemantus sees corruption everywhere.","The proverb that predicts Timon's fate.","Flavius sees that generosity never looks behind it.","The empty coffer behind the lavish feast.","Timon's words are all in debt.","False friends are worse than enemies.","Timon's giving knows no limit.","Apemantus's mockery of bowing flatterers.","False hearts on bent legs.","Apemantus predicts ruin by paper IOUs.","Deaf to advice, open to flattery.","Everyone knew gifts to Timon brought returns.","The creditors see the end coming.","The language of love covering self-interest.","Phoenix to plucked gull: Timon's fall in an image.","Flavius's verdict: too kind to be wise.","Timon will learn only by suffering.","The creditors' servants as go-betweens for gold.","The truth Timon hears too late.","Wealth goes in a breath.","Flavius's private loyalty.","Praise lasts only as long as the money.","Fair-weather friends hide at the first shower.","Timon's own judgement of his giving.","Timon's confidence before the test.","His belief that friends are wealth.","Timon excuses the senators' refusal.","A pun on 'kind': generosity and human nature.","Timon's trust at its height.","Generosity assumes everyone is generous.","Lucullus turns honesty into a fault.","Lucullus's refusal in plain business terms.","The bribe that shows Lucullus's worth.","Flaminius's shock at the change in the world.","Flaminius rejects base money.","Flaminius curses Lucullus with molten coin.","Friendship turns like milk.","Lucius's boast before he is tested.","Lucius's empty excuse.","Lucius drops Timon in a couplet.","The Stranger generalises from Lucius to all flatterers.","The Judas image of betrayal at table.","Ingratitude as monstrous.","Even religion is shocked.","Self-interest has replaced conscience.","Sempronius's excuse disguises meanness as wounded pride.","Honour as a cover for refusal.","Man outdoes the devil in scheming.","The end of Timon's open house.","Winter in Timon's purse.","Even a creditor's servant sees the shame.","Flavius's bitter wit.","Timon trapped in his own house.","Timon's house becomes like all mankind: iron-hearted.","Bills become weapons.","Timon turns his body into money.","Blood counted out like coins.","Timon plans the mock banquet.","The Senate's harsh law.","Alcibiades' case for mercy.","The senator's Stoic answer, which neither Timon nor Alcibiades follows.","Judging calmly from outside the heat of anger.","Blood for blood: the law Alcibiades rejects.","Athens rewards service with exile.","Alcibiades links the Senate to usury.","Wounds repaid with banishment.","Alcibiades chooses revenge.","The lords as summer birds.","Timon sees through them.","Timon's mock grace begins.","A blessing turned into a curse.","The guests become dogs.","The dishes are a picture of the friends.","Timon's flood of insults.","Fair-weather friends named and shamed.","Timon becomes the man-hater.","The lords reduce the scene to a joke.","Timon's curse on Athens begins.","Disorder wished on the whole city.","Timon strips himself of Athens.","From city to wilderness.","Hatred of all humanity.","The servants' loyalty in a single image.","The servants' farewell.","Greatness brings misery.","Flavius's verdict on Timon.","Generosity that makes gods ruins men.","All human nature is crooked.","Timon turns to the earth.","The great speech on gold begins.","Gold turns everything into its opposite.","Gold corrupts religion.","Gold as a whore that sets nations fighting.","Timon names himself.","Beasts are better than men.","Timon's fall like the waning moon.","Timon's twisted request.","Timon urges total destruction.","No pity for anyone.","Praise itself is harm.","Timon asks nature to stop breeding man.","Apemantus's charge against Timon.","Apemantus resents his imitator.","Apemantus sums up Timon's life.","Even misery can be flattered.","Chosen poverty outlasts wealth.","Timon's reply: Apemantus never had anything to lose.","Timon's lost sweetness.","Friends fall like leaves in winter.","Gold is harmless where no one uses it.","Apemantus on Timon's extremes.","Love ends with money.","Men are flattery itself.","Nature as a chain of prey.","Athens as a forest of beasts.","The insult contest between two cynics.","Timon renounces the world.","Gold divides families.","Gold as a visible god.","Apemantus's farewell.","Respectable trades are theft.","Nature itself steals.","Universal theft.","A thief's surprising turn to honesty.","Flavius on false friends.","A world without tears.","Timon's one exception.","Flavius spoils Timon's perfect hate.","Flavius's gentle rebuke.","Timon's terms for Flavius's gift.","The age of empty promises.","The Painter's cynicism.","Timon on the Painter.","Gold worshipped in a pigsty.","Truth unclothed.","Timon pays the Poet with blows.","A senator explains Timon's change.","Timon greets the senators with a curse.","Timon's contempt for Athens.","Timon welcomes death.","Timon's last 'kindness'.","Timon's grave by the sea.","Timon's last curse.","Timon's farewell.","Athens fears ruin.","Timon has gone beyond mankind.","Alcibiades turns the tables.","The senators' plea.","Punish only the guilty.","Alcibiades limits his revenge.","The epitaph's hatred.","Peace made by force."],"insults":[{"id":"90","f":"When thou art Timon's dog, and these knaves honest.","who":"Apemantus","n":"Apemantus's first words to Timon refuse the courtesy everyone else offers."},{"id":"106","f":"he's but a filthy piece of work.","who":"Apemantus","n":"Apemantus calls the Painter filthy, the maker included."},{"id":"107","f":"You're a dog.","who":"Painter","n":"The obvious insult for a Cynic, which Apemantus turns back."},{"id":"108","f":"Thy mother's of my generation:","who":"Apemantus","n":"If he is a dog, the Painter's mother is a bitch."},{"id":"138","f":"Traffic's thy god, and thy god confound thee!","who":"Apemantus","n":"A curse that is also a philosophy."},{"id":"165","f":"Away, unpeaceable dog!","who":"Second Lord","n":"The lords use 'dog' to dismiss him."},{"id":"336","f":"Poor rogues and usurers' men!","who":"Apemantus","n":"Apemantus scorns the creditors' servants."},{"id":"338","f":"Asses.","who":"Apemantus","n":"One word answers 'What are we?'"},{"id":"352","f":"thou wast born a bastard, and thou'lt die a bawd.","who":"Apemantus","n":"Apemantus to the Page."},{"id":"353","f":"Thou wast whelped a dog, and thou shalt famish a dog's death.","who":"Page","n":"The Page answers in kind."},{"id":"430","f":"now I see thou art a fool, and fit for thy master.","who":"Lucullus","n":"Lucullus insults Flaminius for refusing the bribe."},{"id":"471","f":"Your lordship's a goodly villain.","who":"Servant","n":"Mock praise for Sempronius."},{"id":"515","f":"For you serve knaves.","who":"Flavius","n":"Flavius turns 'serve' into an insult."},{"id":"632","f":"Uncover, dogs, and lap.","who":"Timon","n":"Timon's guests become dogs."},{"id":"638","f":"Most smiling, smooth, detested parasites,","who":"Timon","n":"The opening of Timon's flood of abuse."},{"id":"638","f":"Cap and knee slaves, vapours, and minute-jacks!","who":"Timon","n":"Bowing slaves, hot air and time-servers."},{"id":"704","f":"Hang thee, monster!","who":"Timandra","n":"Timandra answers Timon's abuse."},{"id":"739","f":"consumption catch thee!","who":"Timon","n":"Timon greets Apemantus with a curse."},{"id":"793","f":"Thou art the cap of all the fools alive.","who":"Apemantus","n":"The king of fools."},{"id":"794","f":"Would thou wert clean enough to spit upon!","who":"Timon","n":"Too dirty even to spit on."},{"id":"800","f":"Away, thou issue of a mangy dog!","who":"Timon","n":"The insult match reaches its peak."},{"id":"804","f":"Beast!","who":"Apemantus","n":"The exchange collapses into single words."},{"id":"913","f":"Out, rascal dogs!","who":"Timon","n":"Timon beats the Poet and Painter out."}],"pronouns":[{"id":"44","f":"I am not of that feather to shake off","n":"Timon speaks of Ventidius warmly, as a friend he will not desert."},{"id":"89","f":"Good morrow to thee, gentle Apemantus!","n":"Timon uses friendly 'thee' to Apemantus, who refuses the warmth."},{"id":"177","f":"Honest Ventidius: you mistake my love;","n":"Timon uses polite 'you' among the guests at a public feast."},{"id":"274","f":"Thou givest so long, Timon,","n":"Apemantus's 'thou' is blunt, not affectionate: he talks to Timon as an equal or inferior."},{"id":"380","f":"O my good lord!","n":"Flavius always uses 'you' and 'my lord' to Timon, even when scolding him."},{"id":"683","f":"A beast, as thou art.","n":"Timon's 'thou' to Alcibiades is now contempt."},{"id":"849","f":"What! dost thou weep? Come nearer. Then I love thee,","n":"Timon's 'thou' softens into affection for Flavius."},{"id":"926","f":"O! forget What we are sorry for ourselves in thee.","n":"The senators use 'thee' to Timon to sound intimate while asking for help."}],"predict":{"a1s1":["Timon's generosity is rewarded","Timon's generosity is praised, but the Poet's allegory foretells his fall"],"a1s2":["Flavius gets Timon to listen","Timon puts Flavius off and keeps giving"],"a2s1":["The senator waits patiently","The senator sends Caphis to demand payment at once"],"a2s2":["Timon panics","Timon is sure his friends will lend to him"],"a3s1":["Lucullus helps Timon","Lucullus refuses and offers a bribe"],"a3s2":["Lucius keeps his promise","Lucius refuses with excuses"],"a3s3":["Sempronius lends the money","Sempronius refuses on a point of honour"],"a3s4":["Timon pays his creditors","Timon rages and orders a final feast"],"a3s5":["The Senate pardons the soldier","The Senate banishes Alcibiades"],"a3s6":["Timon forgives his friends","Timon serves them warm water and curses them"],"a4s1":["Timon goes to Alcibiades","Timon curses Athens and leaves for the woods"],"a4s2":["The servants abandon Timon","The servants part as friends and Flavius goes to find Timon"],"a4s3":["Timon lives in poverty","Timon finds gold and uses it to spread destruction"],"a5s1":["Timon agrees to save Athens","Timon refuses and prepares his grave"],"a5s2":["Timon comes to Athens' rescue","The senators wait without hope"],"a5s3":["The soldier finds Timon alive","The soldier finds his tomb"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Timon and Apemantus","pts":["Both end up rejecting mankind, but Apemantus chose poverty and Timon had it forced on him.","Apemantus warns Timon in Act 1; Timon ignores him. In Act 4 Timon outdoes him in railing.","Apemantus says Timon knew only extremes; Timon says Apemantus never had anything to lose."],"ids":[740,755]},{"t":"Timon and Alcibiades","pts":["Both serve Athens and are repaid with ingratitude.","Timon withdraws into curses; Alcibiades returns with an army.","Alcibiades makes peace on terms; Timon dies refusing any."],"ids":[658,978]},{"t":"Flavius and the lords","pts":["The lords praise Timon while he is rich and refuse him when he is poor.","Flavius warns Timon while he is rich and stays loyal when he is poor.","Flavius gives away his last money; the lords will not lend any."],"ids":[669,453]},{"t":"Lucullus, Lucius and Sempronius","pts":["Lucullus refuses with a bribe, Lucius with a lie, Sempronius with pretended pride.","Each has received gifts from Timon.","Each comes back to the second banquet expecting more."],"ids":[427,453,469]},{"t":"The two banquets","pts":["The first is a lavish feast with a masque, gifts and toasts to friendship.","The second is warm water, stones and curses.","Apemantus eats roots at the first; at the second the guests are given nothing."],"ids":[201,632]}],"analysis":{"TIMON":["At the start Timon is goodness itself: he frees Ventidius, makes a servant's marriage possible and gives without counting. His creed is that friends exist to share each other's fortunes, and that a gift which expects a return is not a gift. But his generosity is also blind. He will not look at his accounts, will not listen to Flavius, and treats bought praise as love.","The refusals of Act 3 shatter that belief completely. Timon has no middle ground, as Apemantus says: when he cannot love all men, he hates all men. The mock banquet and the curse on Athens show the same total commitment turned inside out. His language changes from warm prose and toasts to some of the most ferocious curses in Shakespeare.","In the woods Timon's hatred is almost a philosophy: everything is a thief, gold is a visible god, man is worse than any beast. Yet he cannot escape the world. Gold finds him, visitors find him, and Flavius forces him to admit one honest man. Readers still argue whether Timon is a tragic idealist destroyed by the world or a foolish man whose extremes are his own fault.","He dies offstage, alone, having arranged his grave so that the sea will wash it daily. His epitaph keeps his hatred alive after death."],"APEMANTUS":["Apemantus is the play's professional cynic. He refuses Timon's food, eats roots, insults everyone and is the only one who tells Timon the truth in Act 1. He sees the guests as devouring Timon and warns him that men shut their doors against a setting sun.","In Act 4 he visits Timon and treats him as a rival. He says Timon's misanthropy is a pose, forced on him by poverty: Timon would be a courtier again if he could. The insult match between them is both comic and bitter.","Apemantus is not simply right. His railing is also a kind of performance, and Timon accuses him of never having had anything to lose. Together they show two ways of rejecting mankind."],"FLAVIUS":["Flavius is Timon's steward and the play's moral centre. He weeps in private over Timon's waste, tries again and again to show him the accounts, and tells him the truth at last.","When the house falls, Flavius shares his money with the other servants and sets out to find Timon. His loyalty is the only thing that breaks Timon's hatred, and Timon calls him the one honest man.","Even Flavius cannot save Timon. He is given gold and sent away, and he brings the senators in Act 5 only to see Timon refuse them."],"ALCIBIADES":["Alcibiades is a soldier who loves blood and honour. He is Timon's friend in Act 1 and the play's second victim of Athenian ingratitude: banished for pleading for a comrade.","Unlike Timon, he turns his anger into action. He raises an army, takes Timon's gold and marches on Athens. Yet he is also willing to bargain: he accepts the senators' terms and promises to punish only his and Timon's enemies.","He has the last word. His reading of the epitaph honours Timon while making peace with the city Timon cursed, and his promise to use 'the olive with my sword' suggests a harder kind of peace."],"LUCULLUS":["Lucullus expects a gift, preaches thrift and offers a bribe. He is the first of the friends to fail Timon, and his excuse is the most shameless: he says he always warned Timon against spending."],"LUCIUS":["Lucius boasts to strangers that he would never have refused Timon, then does exactly that, claiming he has just spent his money. He wears Timon's jewels while sending to collect from him."],"POET":["The Poet's allegory of Fortune's hill tells the whole plot in Act 1. He criticises paid praise and practises it. In Act 5 he returns with the Painter, hoping for more gold, and is beaten away. He stands for art that serves wealth."]},"motifs":[["Gold and money","Debts, talents, bonds and bills fill the first half; in the second, Timon's gold becomes a weapon and a false god.",[24,45,60,97,131,132,160]],["Feasting and devouring","Two banquets frame the fall; guests are said to eat Timon; Apemantus eats roots.",[31,32,34,111,113,114]],["Beasts and dogs","Apemantus is the 'dog'; Timon turns his guests into dogs and wolves and prefers beasts to men.",[15,21,113,122,136,155,156]],["Fortune","The Poet's hill and Fortune's change of mood predict everything; Flavius and Apemantus return to it.",[5,6,7,43,126,137]],["Seasons and weather","Summer birds, winter showers and a setting sun mark the change from wealth to poverty.",[43,64,91,109,110,150]],["Nature and theft","In the woods Timon calls on the earth for roots and calls everything in nature a thief.",[130,142,162,163,164]]],"bigq":[["Is Timon a tragic hero or a fool?",["His generosity is genuine and admirable","He refuses all advice and will not look at his accounts","He moves from one extreme to the other, as Apemantus says","He dies refusing any compromise, which some see as grandeur and others as stubbornness"],[65,127,152,145]],["Is Timon's hatred justified?",["Every friend he enriched refuses him","Strangers and servants show loyalty, so not all men are false","He admits Flavius is honest, which breaks his rule","He curses the innocent along with the guilty"],[95,117,168,169]],["Is Apemantus right?",["He warns Timon truthfully in Act 1","His railing never helps anyone","He says Timon's misanthropy is forced, not chosen","He and Timon end up mirror images"],[19,31,143,152]],["What does the play say about money?",["Money buys praise but not friendship","Gold turns everything into its opposite","Lenders and senators are linked by usury","Wealth without generosity is no better than generosity without wealth"],[63,73,106,132,160]],["Why does Alcibiades get the last word?",["He was wronged like Timon but chose action","He makes peace on terms","He honours Timon while ignoring his wishes","His 'olive with my sword' suggests peace backed by force"],[108,189,191,193]],["Is the play finished?",["Plots and characters vanish","Money sums do not add up","There are two epitaphs","Yet its shape (rise, fall, withdrawal) is complete"],[5,192]]],"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present Timon?",[10,36,65,95,135,183]],["How does Shakespeare present friendship and ingratitude?",[9,73,78,83,86,127]],["How does Shakespeare present money in the play?",[45,60,97,131,132,160]],["How does Shakespeare present Apemantus?",[15,19,31,143,152,161]],["How does Shakespeare present Flavius?",[44,62,71,127,168,170]],["How does Shakespeare present Athens and its rulers?",[100,102,106,107,156,189]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short quotations, built into my own sentences","Explained what the quotation suggests, not just what it says","Zoomed in on a single word or image","Named a method (imagery, soliloquy, aside, curse, irony, structure) and its effect","Linked to context where it helps the argument","Considered more than one interpretation, especially of Timon's hatred and the ending","Referred to the beginning, middle and end of the play","Ended with a conclusion that answers the question"],"models":[[36,"Shakespeare presents Timon's idea of friendship as generous but dangerously naive.","He asks 'what need we have any friends if we should ne'er have need of 'em?', seeing friends as people to be used in need.","The irony is that he says it to the very men who will refuse him.","His tears at the feast, mocked by Apemantus, show how sincerely he believes it.","In Jacobean England, where courtiers lived on credit, his faith would have looked both noble and reckless."],[131,"Timon's speech on gold shows his hatred turning into a philosophy.","'Gold! yellow, glittering, precious gold!' starts as wonder and becomes disgust.","The list of opposites ('black white, foul fair') shows gold destroying every value.","He calls it a 'yellow slave' and a 'common whore', the same insults he gives mankind.","Marx later quoted the speech as an account of money's power to corrupt."],[95,"Shakespeare marks the start of Timon's hatred when his own house turns against him.","'Are my doors oppos'd against my passage?' shows him trapped by creditors.","He compares his house to 'all mankind' with 'an iron heart', the first time he generalises.","The feast he plans at the end of the scene is his first act of revenge.","Some productions play the outburst as madness; others as a terrible clarity."],[143,"Apemantus challenges Timon's misanthropy as a pose.","He says it is 'a poor unmanly melancholy sprung / From change of fortune'.","This questions whether Timon's hatred is principled or simply the anger of a man who lost his money.","Timon's reply, that Apemantus never had anything to lose, is equally sharp.","The scene leaves the audience to judge between two kinds of cynic."],[127,"Flavius offers the play's most sympathetic judgement of Timon.","'Undone by goodness' turns Timon's flaw into a virtue.","'When man's worst sin is he does too much good' is a paradox that sums up the tragedy.","His loyalty contrasts with every lord's betrayal.","Flavius is the only character Timon cannot hate, which complicates Timon's view of mankind."],[193,"Alcibiades' closing lines suggest a peace built on force.","'I will use the olive with my sword' joins the symbols of peace and war.","He promises to 'make war breed peace', so violence is still the means.","The Senate surrenders only because of his army.","The ending may be hopeful or deeply ironic, depending on how it is staged."]],"lang":{"un":[["189","I wonder men dare trust themselves with men:","I'm amazed men dare to trust other men."],["254","his land's put to their books.","His land is mortgaged and entered in his creditors' account books."],["388","Feast-won, fast-lost;","Friends won by feasting are lost when the fasting begins."],["463","For policy sits above conscience.","Self-interest has taken the place of morality."]],"pun":[["401","'Tis lack of kindly warmth they are not kind;","kind","pun","Kind means both generous and natural: their nature is cold, so they are not kind."],["264","Ay, defil'd land, my lord.","defil'd","pun","The battlefield is defiled with blood and 'filed' with soldiers."],["779","An thou hadst hated medlars sooner,","medlars","pun","Medlar (a fruit) and meddler (a busybody)."],["128","he that loves to be flattered is worthy o' the flatterer.","worthy","wordplay","Worthy as deserving praise, and worthy as deserving the flatterer he gets."],["508","He goes away in a cloud:","cloud","pun","Flavius is muffled in a cloak and also under a cloud of disgrace."],["515","For you serve knaves.","serve","pun","'Serve' as do (the answer will not serve) and serve as servants."]],"st":[["679","Gold! yellow, glittering, precious gold!",[["Gold","Exclamation stressed first."],["glittering","Three syllables rushing in wonder."]]],["640","Burn, house! sink Athens!",[["Burn","Hard stress on the command."],["sink","Second command answers the first."]]],["945","Sun, hide thy beams! Timon hath done his reign.",[["Sun","Opening stress, a command to the sky."],["done","The end of his reign stressed."]]]]},"dir":[["636","smoke and lukewarm water","How should Timon reveal the dishes?",[["Calmly, with a smile, then explode","The surprise makes the cruelty sharper."],["In a rage from the start","He has been holding back since Act 3."],["Coldly, like a judge","He is passing sentence on them."]]],["679","Gold! yellow, glittering, precious gold!","How should Timon react to the gold?",[["With joy that turns to disgust","He remembers what gold did to him."],["With horror at once","He sees it as a curse."],["With bitter laughter","Fortune is mocking him."]]],["851","I do proclaim One honest man,","How should Timon say this?",[["Reluctantly","It breaks his rule of hatred."],["Tenderly","Flavius has touched him."],["Angrily","He resents being proved wrong."]]],["755","Thou art a slave, whom Fortune's tender arm","How should Timon answer Apemantus?",[["With contempt","Apemantus never had anything to lose."],["With wounded pride","He feels his suffering is greater."],["As a debate","Two philosophers arguing."]]],["984","Here lie I, Timon, who alive all living men did hate.","How should Alcibiades read the epitaph?",[["With respect","He honours Timon."],["With discomfort","The hatred includes him."],["Formally, to the city","He uses it as a warning to Athens."]]],["527","What! are my doors oppos'd against my passage?","How should Timon enter in 3.4?",[["Bursting out wildly","He has been driven mad by creditors."],["Slowly and disbelieving","He cannot believe what is happening."],["As a trapped animal","His house has become a cage."]]]],"ev":{"ctx":[["685","945"],["336","685","943"],["513","536"],["945","685"],["685","934","945"],["33","128","685"],["583","586","978"],["254","281","380"],["583","254"],["33","867","869"],["98","110","192"],["669","685","934"]],"cr":{"money":[["913","281","138"],["254","281","388"]],"friendship":[["685","527","849"],["668","669","685"]],"hate":[["685","849","784"],["254","388","658"]],"flattery":[["77","128","869"]],"fortune":[["513","963"]],"justice":[["583","586","978"]]}}};
